window.PRAPJ_DOCUMENTS = [{"id": "01", "title": "Business Case", "filename": "01_PRAPJ_Business_Case.docx", "sha256": "32975b4da4df020e722b4ce17176345b253cd9f51626396d12b0a16adb2fd16a", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Subtitle", "text": "PROJECT RESEARCH AND PRACTICE JOURNAL"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "Business Case\nResearch and Analysis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Subtitle", "text": "A formal journal connecting project evidence,\nprofessional decisions and practitioner contribution."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Team DOMINUS  |  PMGT5850 Capstone"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Prepared for sponsor review  |  Lydia Wen"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Recommendation: establish PRAPJ for formal publication, with a professional value proposition, supported contribution and an accountable implementation programme."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "All financial amounts are hypothetical; no project budget or journal funding is committed. Final deliverable acceptance pending. Host arrangements and appointments remain open."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 1", "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Read the Decision brief for the recommendation. The main chapters develop the evidence and operating case; the appendices make policy choices, calculations and sources traceable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "Decision brief\t3"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "1. Mandate, scope and research method\t6"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "2. Interview analysis and problem definition\t8"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "3. Research evidence and competitive positioning\t15"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "4. Stakeholder value and the proposed service\t20"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "5. Strategic rationale and delivery alternatives\t24"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "6. Operating model credibility and sustainable ownership\t27"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "7. Financial feasibility, resource capacity and long-term sustainability\t35"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "8. Publication validation, benefits and readiness\t47"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "9. Delivery plan, accountability and handover\t51"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "10. Risk, resilience and the publication recommendation\t54"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "Appendix A. Publication design and benchmark decisions\t57"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "Appendix B. Model definitions and reproducibility\t59"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "Appendix C. Evidence coverage, limitations and package alignment\t61"}, {"type": "text", "style": "TOC entry", "text": "Appendix D. Source register\t63"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Figures and tables are placed beside the analysis they support. All figures are original analytical diagrams or plots; their labels distinguish evidence, hypotheses and planning scenarios."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "Decision brief", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ should be established as a formally published Project Research and Practice Journal with a clear professional purpose: help people make better-informed project decisions and turn documented practice into knowledge that others can examine and use. The recommended direction is to launch a durable journal with accountable ownership, independent editorial judgement and a manageable first-year publication programme. Its case rests on a distinctive combination of rigorous evidence, practical interpretation and supported practitioner contribution. The launch programme should build these capabilities and test their effectiveness as it moves towards publication."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The journal addresses a useful opportunity between established research publishing and the information channels professionals use every day. Existing project-management journals already publish valuable practice-oriented work. PRAPJ earns its place by making the complete reader and contributor experience serve practical use: identify a project problem, understand the relevant evidence, judge the conditions for applying it, and contribute a documented lesson or unanswered question. A recognisable journal gives this exchange a continuing editorial home and a citable published record. Its value comes from consistently delivering that experience, rather than claiming that project research or accessible summaries do not already exist."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Four stakeholder interviews have been completed: Paul Rodman on 31 August, Louis Taborda on 3 September, Michael Hopmere on 24 September and James Bawtree on 8 October 2026. The interviews provide qualitative support and design evidence; they do not establish population demand, accepted appointments, promised submissions or funding. [L1, L2, L8–L10, L12, W31]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Why readers and contributors should choose PRAPJ"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For experienced and mid-career professionals, the proposed value is trustworthy material organised around decisions they recognise: resource shortages, cross-project dependencies, governance, deliverability, prioritisation, risk, AI-related complexity and sustainability. Each contribution should explain the project objective, evidence, practical action and limits of transfer. Readers can begin with a short interpretation and inspect the full supporting contribution when they need more depth. James's interview sharpens this professional focus; students and emerging professionals are a secondary audience for selected explanatory material. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For practitioner contributors, PRAPJ offers a structured route from experience to an assessable publication. A short initial conversation, a guided outline and editorial writing support can reduce the barrier of starting a formal manuscript. Contributors retain responsibility for accuracy, permissions and substantive author decisions, and receive no guarantee of acceptance. For research authors, a linked brief connects the full research to professional questions and makes its practical relevance easier to find. Clear assessment routes and visible source links protect the credibility of both forms. [L1, L2, L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For a publishing institution or professional supporter, the value is a sustained exchange between research and practice, an accountable home for applied knowledge and a source of well-defined future research questions. These benefits can be assessed through reading, comprehension, useful application, contributor experience and continuity. The journal should earn support through that record. Downloads, publication counts and supportive interviews alone cannot demonstrate all of those outcomes."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "The recommended publication programme"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Retain four core tracks: Research Articles, including Evidence Reviews; Practitioner Cases; Research-to-Practice Briefs; and Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes. An illustrative first operating year contains 16 substantive items: four research contributions, six cases, four linked briefs and two challenges or notes. Release completed items when ready and assemble two curated collections. These are hypothetical planning volumes, not promised acceptances; linked outputs from the same research are not independent studies."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Build the first collections around a small number of recurring professional problems. Begin with text contributions and source-linked interpretation, while testing a small, accessible multimedia extension. Video interviews or explainers should support an identifiable contribution and carry a transcript or captions, source links, permissions and an accurate assessment label. The journal can add shorter student-oriented explanations from the same expert material. Multimedia is a delivery format, not a new category of independently validated evidence. Its adoption and scale require an explicit capacity decision."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Prefer diamond open access so that neither readers nor authors face a publishing charge under the proposed model. Secure an authorised publisher, editorial ownership and a supported platform before inviting real submissions. Institutional hosting or external publishing services can support the PRAPJ title; choosing such a service does not require abandoning the journal's identity or mission. Board candidates remain prospective until they accept an appointment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Financial status and the purpose of the model"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The whole capstone project has no approved or allocated actual budget. There is no committed journal funding. Every PRAPJ budget, cost, income, staffing valuation, reserve and cashflow in this report is hypothetical. The project lead confirmed this status on 8 October 2026. The model illustrates the resources a future publishing arrangement would need; it is not a spending authorisation, an actual expenditure report, a quotation or evidence of available money. [L9]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The illustrative core Year 1 cash envelope is AUD 37,605, including a hypothetical 15% contingency. A separately modelled paid validation exercise is AUD 6,440; this is not a capstone allocation or required payment. Contributed editorial and reviewer time is valued separately to show work that remains necessary even if supplied in kind. An optional interview-support and multimedia scenario adds AUD 2,657, rounded, only if its work is additional to the existing allowances. No budget is created by presenting these calculations."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The resource model supports a credible publication proposition by exposing its workload. The base scenario requires 48 completed external reports. A proposed roster of 25 active, subject-mapped reviewers, each accepting three assignments annually, provides an expected 60 reports at an assumed 80% completion yield; planning 48 uses 80% of that capacity. These figures are hypothetical readiness targets, not recruitment achievements or guaranteed performance."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "The decision this case supports"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Endorse the establishment of PRAPJ and its professional value proposition, and use this package to organise the work required for formal publication. Confirm the continuing publisher and accountable owner; adopt the editorial, author and review policies; verify resource availability and reviewer capacity; assemble cleared initial contributions; and approve the opening of submissions when the service can fulfil its commitments. This is a publication implementation programme, with evidence checks that strengthen the journal's offer to readers and contributors."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The earlier alternatives appraisal is retained for transparency. It shows why a dedicated journal offers control of the contribution model and why service feasibility needs attention. Its close numerical ranking is an illustrative judgement, not proof that any title will succeed. The present recommendation is to develop PRAPJ for formal publication and choose the most suitable support arrangement. Reader testing should improve the publication experience, while proportionate operating controls protect author trust and the future published record."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The capstone deadline is 20 November 2026. Prepared documents and mockups can satisfy the project's deliverable requirements before the journal opens. Formal publication remains a subsequent operating milestone, with its date determined by readiness. No funding, appointment, supplier agreement, submission acceptance or public launch is represented as already completed."}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "1. Mandate, scope and research method", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Project purpose and acceptance boundary"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The supplied project proposal requires three outcomes: a clear, factual and persuasive business case addressing stakeholder needs; Terms of Reference and prospective shortlists for the Advisory and Editorial Boards; and the necessary templates and guidelines with both a digital and hardcopy mockup. The business case is one part of that package. It analyses the proposed service and its feasibility; it does not absorb the separate author guide, governance instruments, website copy or reusable templates. [L3–L6]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The project has no approved or allocated actual budget, and no journal funding is committed. All financial figures are hypothetical. Host, final board membership and the formal publication date remain to be confirmed. The website implementation remains assigned to another team. The printable specimen demonstrates a journal issue layout. Neither is a production submission system or an actual published issue."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The three capstone Success Measures concern prepared deliverables. They should be judged through a quality review of the package, with formal acceptance recorded separately. The success of an operating journal is a later question involving use, quality, affordability and continuity. Keeping these two levels separate prevents a completed capstone from being mistaken for a funded journal and prevents an unlaunched journal from being mistaken for a missing mockup."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decision structure"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The report considers strategic need, alternative ways of meeting that need, commercial and operating arrangements, affordability and delivery management. This structure is informed by the five-case approach described in HM Treasury's business-case guidance, which distinguishes strategic, economic, commercial, financial and management questions. It is an analytical aid for PRAPJ, not a claim that this Australian capstone is subject to UK public-spending approval rules. No benefit–cost ratio or net present value is fabricated where benefits and future funding cannot credibly be monetised. [R01]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Evidence assembled"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The research combines the original records, interview summaries, the project lead's 8 October confirmations, , the Microsoft Project schedule, the three Emerald pages recommended in the project correspondence and relevant linked guidance, the IJPM journal and author pages, selected comparator journals, original scholarly sources on research–practice relationships, and official publishing infrastructure and standards sources. Market, operational and financial evidence were examined separately and then reconciled against the proposed contribution model."}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "2. Interview analysis and problem definition", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Michael Hopmere, 24 September: relevance, participation and assessment"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The supplied PRAPJ interview summary repeatedly connects the gap to how research is expressed and used. Technical language and unclear practical implications can prevent a professional reader from seeing why an article matters. The design implication is more demanding than shortening an abstract: the publication must state the decision it informs, the evidence supporting the claim and the conditions that limit its use. This does not mean reducing methodological detail; the detailed article and practical interpretation serve different reading tasks. [L1]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The interview also describes a two-way relationship. Researchers should explain practical implications, while practitioners should share observations and questions that can shape research. A one-directional summary service addresses only part of that aspiration. PRAPJ's challenge/notes track is therefore a proposal to make practitioner questions visible, while its case track provides a more structured route for experience to become examinable evidence. Neither route should promise that every experience is generalisable or publishable. [L1]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Starting with practitioner cases is attractive because cases can make the purpose of the journal tangible. It is also demanding: access, employer permission, outcome evidence and reputational sensitivity can be harder to resolve than prose structure. The interview's preference for reviewers with academic and practical understanding is interpreted as a need for combined expertise. The proposed two-reviewer process is an editorial design choice, not a verbatim requirement extracted from the participant. [L1]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The discussion of quick editorial handling and the editor's final decision suggests a service expectation: contributors should understand what happens next and receive a reasoned outcome. Speed cannot be obtained simply by imposing deadlines on unpaid reviewers. The business case connects proposed service targets to assignment capacity, reminders, escalation and the ability to close or slow intake. The interview's suggestion of eight articles per quarter is treated as a later scale scenario, not an accepted launch quota. [L1]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Paul Rodman, 31 August: trusted channels and defensible decisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Paul Rodman's account places experienced colleagues, internal specialists and established standards ahead of journals in routine knowledge seeking. Research can still matter when it supports defensible advice, especially where a decision must be explained to a client. A journal therefore competes with trusted relationships and fast internal answers, as well as with other journals. The practical question is whether a PRAPJ item improves a real decision sufficiently to justify the reader's search and reading time. [L2]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The interview identifies resistance when evidence conflicts with preferred assumptions about project cost or risk. This suggests an organisational adoption barrier that plain language alone cannot remove. A technically clear brief may still be ignored when it is inconvenient. PRAPJ should help users explain evidence and its limitations, but it cannot promise to overcome incentives, authority structures or political disagreement within projects. Measuring downloads as though they represented changed decisions would conceal this limitation. [L2]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Paul describes uneven retention and transfer of lessons, the pressure to move to the next project and reluctance to expose failure. The proposed response is a case format that records context, evidence, mixed outcomes and transfer limits without requiring unnecessary disclosure of commercially sensitive details. Permission and confidentiality remain substantive constraints; anonymisation does not automatically make a case safe or credible. An account stripped of every consequential detail may be unsuitable for publication even when permission is granted. [L2]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The interest in project data, risk analysis, reputable institutional support and possible professional-development value is conditional. It identifies reasons to test the concept, not a pledge to publish, review, fund or join a board."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "James Bawtree, 8 October: practical usefulness and an easier contribution route"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "James supports the opportunity for PRAPJ when its design starts with professional use. His account reinforces Paul's observation that practitioners often turn first to standards, recognised frameworks and direct sources. He describes a divide in working habits rather than a lack of available research. The implication is that a new journal must become a useful part of a professional's decision process: make relevant material easy to recognise, consume and apply, and distribute it through channels professionals already use. His support is conditional on that design, rather than an unconditional endorsement of every proposed operating feature. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "His description of project reviews provides a concrete model for trustworthy case evidence. He compares status reports, project schedules and interviews, looking for discrepancies between reported progress, recorded completion and people's accounts. PRAPJ cases should therefore explain which sources support each important claim, where those sources disagree and how the author investigated the disagreement. This does not require every case to contain the same three sources. It requires transparent reasoning and an honest account of what is missing or unverifiable. A coherent story is stronger when readers can inspect its basis rather than being asked to trust the narrator alone. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Transferability depends on objectives and conditions as well as sector. James's ESG example suggests that learning may cross from manufacturing to healthcare when the relevant objective and mechanisms are comparable. Accordingly, templates should distinguish the originating project objective, delivery setting, intervention, evidence, outcome and conditions of use. A reader should be able to explain both why a lesson might help and why it might fail in their setting. Similar labels such as 'agile', 'governance' or 'sustainability' are not sufficient evidence of comparability. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "James identifies resource shortages, dependencies between projects sharing resources, organisational silos, governance weaknesses, implausible business cases and excessive 'priority one' initiatives as useful content areas. AI-related complexity and sustainability broaden that problem agenda. These are his suggested priorities, not a ranked survey of the profession. They supply a coherent starting point for commissioning: select cases and research that illuminate a specific decision within these problems, then compare the reader response across topics. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Writing support is an enabling service. James describes editors interviewing an expert and shaping the conversation into an article, with a 30–60-minute discussion as an example of a manageable starting contribution. That interval is his illustration of the conversation, not a promise that a manuscript can be completed or accepted in an hour. Editors would still need time to verify facts, locate records, resolve permissions, distinguish evidence from recollection and secure contributor approval. Where editorial assistance is substantive, its contribution must be acknowledged and authorship assigned by the adopted policy. An editor who helped create the submission should not provide its supposedly independent acceptance decision. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "He recommends an initial focus on mid-career and experienced professionals because a useful exchange may encourage them to contribute back. Recognition matters, but the offer should also include relevant learning, a useful professional conversation and access to material they can apply. Students and emerging practitioners can benefit from selected derivative explanations after the expert material exists. This supports one coherent evidence base with different levels of explanation, rather than requiring the journal to commission unrelated products for every audience at launch. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Finally, James advocates testing video and other media alongside written content. A video interview could introduce a problem, a brief could explain the decision and a full article could carry the detailed evidence. These layers must remain linked and consistently qualified. A compelling speaker or high view count does not replace evidence assessment. Accessible transcripts or captions, contributor approval, permissions, source links and correction handling belong in the workflow before any media is released. The first test should determine which format adds value and how much additional effort it needs, rather than assuming that all readers prefer video. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Louis Taborda, 3 September: support for research, practice and education"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dr Louis Taborda supports the establishment of the Project Research and Practice Journal (PRAPJ), regarding it as a worthwhile initiative with potential value for both professional practice and project management education. He participated in an interview with the project team on 3 September 2026 and subsequently confirmed the supportive position recorded by the project lead. His support concerns the journal’s central purpose: creating a meaningful connection between research knowledge and the experience of people working on projects."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Taborda’s professional background provides relevant context for this position. His career has included teaching mathematics and computer science, working as an IT consultant, and teaching project management in higher education. The University of Sydney has also documented his interest in experiential learning and in involving students in real projects through industry collaboration. These experiences place his support at the intersection of professional application and education, where the relationship between knowledge and its practical use is especially important."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Within that context, PRAPJ offers a credible opportunity to make research more accessible to professional judgement. Practitioners need to understand how findings relate to a particular decision, what assumptions underpin them, and where their application may be limited. A journal connecting research with documented project experience could help readers examine these questions more carefully. Its value would come from helping professionals interpret evidence in context, while preserving the reasoning and limitations necessary for informed decisions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The educational rationale is equally relevant. Experiential learning connects conceptual understanding with practical activity and reflection. A publication bringing research into conversation with project experience could provide material through which students examine decisions, consider alternatives, and understand why approaches produce different results in different circumstances. For educators, this creates opportunities to connect theoretical concepts with the uncertainty and complexity encountered in practice. Taborda’s confirmed support is therefore consistent with his publicly documented commitment to learning through practical application."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed exchange also creates value for research. Professional experience can reveal unresolved questions, recurring difficulties, and contextual factors that deserve systematic investigation. When practitioners have a credible route to share documented experience, researchers gain opportunities to engage with problems that matter outside academic settings. Conversely, research can help practitioners question familiar assumptions and assess whether lessons from one project are transferable to another. This reciprocal relationship gives PRAPJ a substantive purpose beyond simply adding another publication to the field."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Taborda’s support should be understood in relation to this purpose. It supports the proposition that a journal connecting research, practice and education is worth developing. It also provides a relevant stakeholder perspective for the business case, complementing the views collected from the other interview participants. His contribution strengthens the qualitative rationale for PRAPJ by connecting the proposed journal with an established educational concern: enabling people to apply knowledge thoughtfully rather than treating theoretical understanding and practical experience as separate activities."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For the project team, this support provides encouragement to develop the journal’s purpose into a coherent publication offering. The business case, governance proposals, author guidance and journal mockups create materials through which that purpose can be examined and refined. Their practical value depends on whether the eventual journal makes the connection between evidence and professional decisions clear to its readers and contributors."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Overall, Taborda regards PRAPJ as a worthwhile initiative with potential to strengthen relationships between project research, professional practice and education. His confirmed support contributes to the case for establishing the journal and pursuing its development with a clear practical and educational purpose. It also provides qualitative stakeholder support for the journal’s rationale, while the project’s separate feasibility analysis and implementation planning address the resources and arrangements needed to realise that potential."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Interview register and evidence strength"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Interviewee", "Confirmed interview date", "Permitted inference"], ["Paul Rodman", "31 August 2026", "Attributed practitioner insights about trust, evidence and case-sharing barriers"], ["Louis Taborda", "3 September 2026", "Support for establishing PRAPJ; research–practice–education rationale, with biography and analysis identified separately"], ["Michael Hopmere", "24 September 2026", "Attributed academic insights about relevance, two-way exchange and assessment"], ["James Bawtree", "8 October 2026", "Attributed practitioner insights about goals, context, writing support, multimedia and useful engagement"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Convergence and productive disagreement"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Michael, Paul and James converge on credible knowledge that professionals can understand and use. Michael emphasises a two-way research–practice exchange and a publication route. Paul explains trust, advice and the organisational obstacles to sharing and applying evidence. James adds detailed product and service requirements: goals and context, visible evidence comparison, assisted contribution and testing non-text formats. Louis's confirmed support adds a professional and educational rationale: connecting research knowledge with practical judgement and experiential learning. The expanded account explains how that purpose complements the other perspectives; it does not attribute agreement with every specific review, format or funding proposal to him. [L10, L12, W31]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The differences improve the design. Michael's aspiration for frequent publication creates a capacity question; James's emphasis on usefulness argues against treating volume as the main success measure. Paul's confidentiality concerns qualify the idea of easy contribution: an interview can lower writing effort but cannot make restricted evidence publishable. James's multimedia preference should be tested with other users, while the permanent text record remains a clear, inspectable basis for assessment and citation. A coherent journal can respond to all three concerns without claiming that one format suits everyone."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Theme", "Evidence anchor", "Publication response", "Evidence to collect"], ["Accessible practical meaning", "Michael's relevance discussion; James's application focus", "Decision statements, linked briefs and problem-led discovery", "Readers identify a relevant action and its limits"], ["Trust and evidence", "Paul's knowledge-source discussion; James's triangulation", "Visible source types, discrepancies, methods and accountable assessment", "Readers can explain why the claim is credible and what remains uncertain"], ["Supported practitioner contribution", "Michael's two-way exchange; James's editor-interview example", "Guided outlines and a permission-aware interview-to-manuscript route", "Cleared outlines, time required and contributor experience"], ["Context and transfer", "Paul's lessons discussion; James's goal-comparability example", "Objective, setting, conditions and transferability fields", "Readers distinguish relevant similarities from material differences"], ["Confidentiality", "Paul and James on sensitive examples", "Permission checks, justified generalisation and secure private files", "Sufficient detail can be assessed and responsibly published"], ["Format and accessibility", "James's video and derivative-content proposal", "Small tests of source-linked multimedia and shorter explanations", "Format completion, comprehension, preference and additional workload"], ["Practical value over volume", "James's feature-to-benefit progression; Michael's metrics", "Dashboard covering output, engagement, usefulness and considered use", "Ratings with denominators, return/recommendation and documented examples"], ["Research, practice and education", "Louis’s confirmed support as reported by the project lead; expanded account and public biography [L12, W31]", "Team implication: cases and linked briefs can support professional judgement and reflective teaching", "Readers and educators assess usefulness, contextual limits and learning application"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This is a purposive evidence base for developing a publication. The summaries support specific design choices; Louis’s expanded support account strengthens the practical and educational rationale. Together they do not establish representative demand or eliminate the need to learn from actual readers and contributors. [L1, L2, L8, L10, L12, W31]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "The problem PRAPJ can reasonably address"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The working problem statement is: some project professionals may struggle to locate, interpret and contribute sufficiently trustworthy evidence for time-sensitive project decisions, while some researchers lack a practical route to hear and examine those professionals' questions. This is deliberately narrower than claiming that research is generally inaccessible, that practitioners do not use evidence, or that existing journals are inadequate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Four mechanisms sit within the proposed journal's influence: the way a contribution explains its decision relevance; the transparency of its evidence and boundaries; the route by which practitioners contribute; and the channels through which a relevant reader encounters it. Organisational incentives, confidential data ownership, research career incentives and overall project governance remain important external conditions. PRAPJ can support those conditions but cannot control them."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-1.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 1. Proposed problem-to-service logic; arrows represent hypotheses, not demonstrated causal effects."}]}, {"id": "s4", "title": "3. Research evidence and competitive positioning", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "What the research actually establishes"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The literature is not a single body of experimental evidence that a new journal will close a gap. It combines conceptual arguments, research-network synthesis, qualitative empirical studies and institutional experience. The following evidence table preserves those distinctions. Findings in adjacent management fields are useful for designing PRAPJ, but they require testing in project-management settings."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Source and evidence type", "Verified finding or argument", "Relevance and boundary for this business case"], ["Winter, Smith, Morris and Cicmil (2006): synthesis of the Rethinking Project Management network", "The network's two-year work produced five directions: complexity, social processes, value creation, project conceptualisation and practitioner development. It was presented as an agenda to stimulate research, not an exclusive agenda. [M01]", "Establishes a substantial history of questioning how project research relates to developing practice. It neither counts dissatisfied readers nor tests a publishing business model."], ["Cicmil, Williams, Thomas and Hodgson (2006): methodological and conceptual argument with research examples", "The article redirects attention towards lived project experience, context-dependent judgement, ethics and reflexivity, rather than relying only on prescribed project-management practices. [M02]", "Supports cases that explain context and actual decisions. It does not justify treating every practitioner narrative as reliable evidence."], ["Chaves (2021): project-management position paper", "The paper recommends practitioner involvement across research and publishing, practice-relevant genres and help with writing. It explicitly suggests that special sections may be a more manageable initial step than wholly practitioner-dedicated outlets. [M03]", "Supports participation and varied formats while directly challenging an automatic leap to a new journal. These are argued recommendations, not measured intervention effects."], ["Van de Ven and Johnson (2006): conceptual paper", "The authors distinguish a transfer problem, differences between kinds of knowledge, and a knowledge-production problem. Engaged scholarship is proposed as a response to the last of these. [M04]", "Provides a useful diagnostic distinction: a better summary, a contextual explanation and a jointly framed research question solve different problems. It is not evidence that any one format guarantees use."], ["Mohrman, Gibson and Mohrman (2001): empirical exploration involving ten companies", "Perceived usefulness was associated with organisational self-design informed by findings and opportunities for researchers and practitioners to interpret results together. The accessible institutional working paper precedes the journal version. [M05]", "Supports testing joint interpretation, rather than equating publication with application. The study concerns perceived usefulness in its participating organisations, not journal subscriber demand or causal project-performance gains."], ["Božič, Bachkirov and Černe (2022): qualitative grounded-theory study of 47 practising managers", "Participants identified practice-engaged research, relevance and complementary knowledge as enablers. Barriers included trust, cognition, coping and heuristic information processing. [M06]", "Indicates that reader-side conditions matter alongside academic communication. The sample is not a representative estimate of project professionals or an Australian market survey."], ["Makin (2021): case study in a medium-sized IT consultancy", "An 11-month engagement examined attempts to use organisation-theory and information-systems research. The study describes a pragmatic knowledge boundary and considers collaborative practices and boundary objects as ways of addressing it. [M07]", "Supports attention to situated interpretation and practical artefacts. A single embedded case cannot establish universal effectiveness, and the researcher's involvement requires attention to perspective."], ["Bansal and colleagues (2012): reflective account of the Network for Business Sustainability", "The authors draw on their collaboration to argue for intermediary organisations that can support exchange beyond what isolated individuals can achieve. [M08]", "Supports treating translation and coordination as organised work with owners and resources. It does not show that PRAPJ needs the same structure or that journal revenue will fund it."], ["Locatelli and colleagues (2023): project-management manifesto", "The authors defend the relevance of contemporary project scholarship, emphasise its social-science foundations and invite collaboration with neighbouring disciplines. They argue for both academic rigour and social relevance. [M09]", "Prevents a misleading claim that existing project research is merely technical or intrinsically irrelevant. The manifesto is an agenda-setting perspective, not a demand assessment for PRAPJ."], ["Tkachenko, Hahn and Peterson (2017): integrative literature review", "The review brings together research-practice literature from management, applied psychology and human-resource development, synthesising seven themes and proposing a knowledge-production model. [M10]", "Demonstrates that the issue extends beyond project management. Its abstract establishes scope and purpose; this review does not infer detailed results or sample counts from inaccessible full text."]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Three implications follow from this mixed evidence base. First, PRAPJ should avoid a deficit story in which academics possess valuable knowledge and practitioners simply fail to read it. A professional may understand an article yet reasonably decide that its setting, incentives or evidence do not fit the present decision. Conversely, a familiar practice may be attractive because it is convenient rather than because it is well supported. The editorial task is to make both the evidence and the contextual judgement inspectable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Second, practical relevance should be defined before production, then revisited after use. A research article can nominate an intended decision, audience and limitation; a practitioner reviewer can test whether those statements are comprehensible; a later reader can explain what was useful, irrelevant or misleading. These are different observations. An editorial claim of relevance should not be recorded as demonstrated use. A reader's favourable reaction should not be recorded as improved project performance. This proposed distinction keeps the benefits case measurable without overstating causal effects."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Third, the intervention should address the part of the problem it can control. PRAPJ can improve article labelling, preparation support, evidence disclosure, review and interpretation. It cannot directly alter employers' incentives, grant organisations access to confidential data, give practitioners protected reading time, or guarantee academic recognition. The business case should fund and evaluate the controllable service while recording these external conditions as dependencies. An attractive website may make the service easier to access, but it does not supply the editorial relationships or evidence quality on which usefulness depends."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Competitive boundary and comparator selection"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The relevant market is the set of ways a project professional acquires credible help for a decision and the ways a contributor obtains a useful publishing route. This includes journals, professional-body summaries, organisational knowledge, colleagues, training and commissioned advice. Restricting the comparison to journals with similar titles would miss important substitutes. It would also confuse a shortage of publishing places with a shortage of usable evidence: the former does not automatically establish the latter."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Seven journals were selected for transparent qualitative comparison: prominent general project-management outlets; publications explicitly associated with practice, cases or society; and a closely named historical comparator. This is a purposive benchmark, not a census, ranking or exhaustive definition of “quality.” The matrix records features verified on official publisher, professional-association or university pages. A feature not established by the consulted page is marked unverified rather than absent. Promotional self-descriptions are evidence of positioning, not independent proof of effectiveness. [M11-M18]"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Comparator", "Verified scope or offer", "Verified practice-facing route or signal", "Strategic implication for PRAPJ"], ["International Journal of Project Management (IJPM)", "APM describes broad project-management coverage, cross-industry applications and double-blind review. [M11]", "The separate APM Research Summary Series translates selected IJPM articles for practitioners. [M12]", "Competing on “research plus practical meaning” alone would be weak. Test a more specific contribution workflow and decision format."], ["International Journal of Managing Projects in Business (IJMPB)", "Emerald positions the journal as a connection between academia and project practice through rigorous, practice-informed research. [M13]", "Its page supplies detailed author policies and guidance for special-issue proposals. [M13]", "An existing journal already claims the central bridge position. A partner collection deserves investigation alongside an independent launch."], ["Project Management Journal (PMJ)", "PMI states a mission combining theory and evidence-based practice across projects, programmes, portfolios and project-based organising. Journal access is a membership benefit. [M14]", "PMI explicitly lists webinars, videos, summaries and Thoughtlets among routes serving practitioners. [M14]", "Accessible formats and professional distribution are established competitive capabilities. PRAPJ must demonstrate a reason to return beyond a shorter article."], ["Project Management Research and Practice (PMRP)", "UTS's archived page describes research and practitioner contributions addressing socially responsible change. It records a move to another platform in 2020 and archives 2016-2018 issues. [M15]", "Archived contents distinguish practitioner cases with single-blind review and research articles with double-blind review. [M15]", "A close conceptual predecessor exists. Investigate naming, continuity and possible collaboration; do not infer closure or business failure from the archive or an inaccessible current site."], ["Journal of Project Management Practice (JPMP)", "The University of Malaya page describes a double-blind journal covering theory, research and practice across sectors, published twice yearly. [M16]", "It expressly includes innovative practice case studies assessed for reader relevance. [M16]", "Practice cases and broad professional relevance are established formats. PRAPJ needs a stronger proposition than adding a case category."], ["Project Leadership and Society (PLS)", "IPMA's current journal page identifies PLS as open access and describes interactions between projects and economic, social, political and organisational processes. [M17]", "Its association-owned description gives the journal an explicit connection to society and a professional research community. [M17]", "Openness and societal orientation alone are insufficient differentiation. PRAPJ should specify the decisions and contribution service it will serve."], ["International Journal of Project Organisation and Management (IJPOM)", "Inderscience describes a broad organisational and cross-industry audience including practitioners. It lists four issues annually, a subscription route and optional open-access articles. [M18]", "Its topics include risk, post-deployment review, knowledge management, governance and consultancy projects. [M18]", "The proposed initial topics are not unoccupied subject territory. Execution and contributor support must matter more than a new topic label."]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "APM's summary programme is a particularly relevant service comparator. Its official account says the series followed consultation about access, format and language barriers; experienced technical writers prepare summaries with original authors where possible, and reader feedback is invited. PRAPJ can learn from this division of labour. It should not claim that linked summaries, plain language or a feedback route are unprecedented. The programme demonstrates an operating precedent, not a quantified unmet market or a guarantee that a second service will attract readers. [M12]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The market evidence also counsels against using headline journal metrics as a shortcut. Citation indicators, acceptance rates, readership labels, publication frequency and service-time figures answer different questions and may cover different periods. Without aligned definitions and current comparable data, a numerical ranking would create apparent precision. This chapter therefore does not compare impact factors, infer market share from web traffic, or use a publisher's historical acceptance rate as PRAPJ's forecast. The appropriate next benchmark is a task-based comparison of content usefulness and contribution effort."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-2.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 2. Comparator feature evidence from official sources; an unverified feature is not treated as absent."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Market size: what can and cannot be estimated"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "No credible estimate of the obtainable PRAPJ audience can be derived from the supplied evidence. The number of project professionals globally, the value of project expenditure, association membership or total journal downloads would describe broad populations or activities. None establishes how many people would use this particular service, contribute evidence, return repeatedly or fund its operation. Applying an arbitrary penetration percentage to such a population would create a misleading market forecast."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A more useful early market model is a set of linked, measured funnels. For readers, record invited people, eligible respondents, completed content tasks, first-time use and later return. For authors, record expressions of interest, eligible outlines, permission-feasible proposals, completed submissions and publishable contributions. For reviewers, record expertise, annual report capacity, conflicts, invitation response and actual completion. For funders, distinguish an introduction, an expression of strategic interest, a service contribution and a written funding commitment. The pilot requires all these flows; growth in one does not compensate automatically for failure in another."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The service is also constrained by its own capacity. A small qualified readership may be adequate for a focused evidence-exchange pilot, while a large audience with no suitable submissions or reviewers would be operationally fragile. Conversely, a large submission queue may increase costs before demonstrating reader value. The economically relevant question is therefore the cost of a useful, quality-controlled contribution and its sustained use by the intended audience. It is not the cheapest cost per page or the highest number of downloads. These are proposed management measures; the present package reports no achieved values."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Geographical scope should remain explicit. The capstone's networks may make an Australian starting cohort practical, but the four interviews do not establish nationwide demand. An English-language publication may receive international interest without having the editorial resources to cover every jurisdiction or sector. Commissioning should identify where a case occurred and which institutional conditions matter. The pilot can accept wider participation while evaluating a bounded initial audience, avoiding a public promise of universal relevance that its evidence cannot support."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "A proposed seven-part service design"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The following service-design analysis translates positioning into operating choices. These are proposals for PRAPJ, not a description of proven customer preferences. Its purpose is to expose the work and dependencies behind the public proposition."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Element", "Proposed design choice", "Why it matters and what must be tested"], ["Product", "Four contribution tracks, with a decision-relevance statement and evidence limits appropriate to each", "Tests whether users need original research, documented cases, translations and questions as a connected service rather than four unrelated content categories"], ["Price", "Preferred diamond access, conditional on funding; disclose the substantial non-cash effort required from authors and reviewers", "Removing a fee does not remove time, permission or production costs; examine willingness and ability to contribute labour separately from willingness to read"], ["Place", "Accessible public content distributed through approved professional and institutional channels", "The website is a destination; distribution must reach people at the moment they have a relevant problem"], ["Promotion", "Demonstrate a specific decision task and invite critical feedback", "Avoid launch claims based on broad enthusiasm; measure whether a sample earns attention compared with an existing option"], ["People", "Research, practice, translation and managing-editor responsibilities, supported by independent reviewers", "The service depends on qualified judgement and coordination; headcount alone cannot establish capacity or subject coverage"], ["Process", "Predictable intake, scope checks, proportional review, revisions, permissions, production and corrections", "A reliable process reduces uncertainty for contributors while protecting quality; test the workflow before opening submissions"], ["Physical and digital evidence", "A readable specimen, clear review labels, real publication details when assigned, and consistent policies", "These make the proposition inspectable; presentation quality must not imply appointments, peer review, indexing or publication status that do not exist"]]}]}, {"id": "s5", "title": "4. Stakeholder value and the proposed service", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Who would use, contribute to and sustain PRAPJ"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A journal has several interdependent audiences. Readers need useful evidence; authors need a fair and worthwhile publication route; reviewers need manageable work and confidence that their effort matters; institutions need accountable expenditure and continuity. A concept that attracts readers but cannot obtain credible manuscripts is not viable. Neither is a well-designed manuscript workflow without a reachable readership. PRAPJ should test these sides together, while recognising that they may be motivated by different benefits."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The primary launch audience is experienced and mid-career project professionals: sponsors and PMO leaders making governance choices, consultants developing defensible advice, and delivery practitioners addressing risks, dependencies and learning. James's interview strengthens this proposed focus. Researchers are both contributors and users of practice-generated questions. Students and emerging professionals are a secondary audience for selected explanations derived from the same expert evidence. These priorities are design choices informed by interviews, not validated demographic market segments. [L8]"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Stakeholder", "Job the service should help accomplish", "Main adoption barrier", "Evidence of value to collect"], ["Sponsor or PMO leader", "Compare an intervention and its conditions before approving a change", "Evidence is late, generic or politically inconvenient", "A documented use in a decision brief, with permission"], ["Consultant or specialist", "Explain a defensible recommendation and its uncertainty", "Client relevance and source credibility", "Correct application to a realistic client scenario"], ["Delivery practitioner", "Learn from a comparable decision or outcome", "Time, terminology and lack of comparability", "Finds a relevant lesson and identifies a transfer limit"], ["Practitioner author", "Turn experience into an assessable contribution", "Time, permissions and fear of reputational damage", "A feasible case outline with accessible evidence"], ["Research author", "Reach professional readers without weakening the research", "Career incentives and added translation effort", "Willingness to submit under the actual proposed conditions"], ["Reviewer or editor", "Improve credible work within an explicit commitment", "Unpaid workload and conflicts", "Accepted subject-specific assignments and timely reports"], ["Host, library or sponsor", "Sustain a legitimate, useful publication", "Unclear ownership, costs and service obligations", "Written scope, resource commitment and succession owner"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Value proposition and editorial boundaries"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed promise is that a reader can quickly identify the decision, inspect the basis of the claim and judge where the lesson may travel. It should be visible at article level, not merely in an About page. Every core contribution therefore includes a short decision-relevance statement. Research retains methods and limitations; cases distinguish observations from interpretations; briefs link to identifiable source research; and challenges separate a question from a claim that has already been tested."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ should publish mixed and adverse outcomes when they are well evidenced and responsibly presented. Requiring success stories would undermine the learning purpose and encourage selective reporting. At the same time, a case must not become a grievance, an advertisement or an allegation without an appropriate evidential basis. Editors need a route to decline material whose claims cannot be assessed or whose necessary permissions cannot be secured."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The service should support authors through format guidance and clinics, while keeping assistance separate from acceptance decisions. A practitioner–researcher pair may improve method and interpretation, but co-authorship must reflect actual contribution. Researchers should not be added merely to make a case appear academically credible. Commercial sponsorship must not buy manuscript acceptance, reviewer selection or suppression of inconvenient findings."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "A concrete promise to founding contributors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A founding author should be able to see what they gain and what they must provide. PRAPJ offers a publication route designed to connect an identifiable project problem with inspectable evidence and professional learning. Research authors retain methodological depth while a linked brief makes decision relevance visible. Practitioner contributors can begin with a guided outline or an editor-supported conversation, then develop a manuscript with sufficient records, permissions and context for assessment. The proposed diamond model removes author charges only when institutional support is secured and that policy is adopted; the current project cannot promise a funded service."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The initial contributor proposition should be tested in concrete terms: a relevant audience, clearly stated assessment, useful editorial feedback, accurate attribution, a durable article record and a source-linked practical interpretation. Do not substitute unearned prestige signals, promised citation impact or immediate indexing for these benefits. A new journal earns credibility through the quality of its initial contributions, the independence of its decisions and its treatment of contributors."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An interview-supported route can make participation easier without weakening authorship or review. A proposed workflow is: topic conversation; evidence and permissions screen; approved outline; supported drafting; contributor verification and declarations; independent assessment; final proof and publication. Allow the contributor to withdraw an unsuitable outline before formal submission. Record assistance and any authorship role, avoid hidden promotional ghostwriting, and assign an unconflicted handling editor. Support is a preparation service, never an acceptance guarantee."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Problem-led collections and a layered content model"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The first publication programme should demonstrate a recognisable answer to 'What project decision does this help me make?' Suggested commissioning clusters are: resource and dependency decisions; governance and deliverability; prioritisation; and risk, sustainability or AI-related complexity. Each cluster can combine a detailed research or case contribution with a short interpretation and, where useful, a supported media explanation. James proposed these problem areas, but the eventual selection should follow available credible content and user feedback. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The core text is the authoritative contribution with methods or case evidence, limitations and publication metadata. A brief helps a time-constrained professional judge relevance. Optional video or audio explains a bounded point and links back to the text; captions or a transcript support access. A selected micro-explanation can introduce a concept to an emerging professional. Each derivative states its source, author/editor responsibility, date, rights and assessment status. Corrections propagate through the linked set. Viewing four formats derived from one contribution remains engagement with one evidence family."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This design gives PRAPJ a persuasive identity without multiplying full article categories. Multimedia is an optional delivery extension, while the four core tracks and their review routes remain stable. A small launch team should commission fewer, well-supported examples and expand when users value them and capacity is demonstrated. The incremental workload scenario in the financial chapter makes this choice visible."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Reader journey and distribution hypothesis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The journey begins before a reader reaches the journal. A professional may encounter an article through a colleague, an association, a course, a search or a project-specific query. The first screen should identify the project problem, intended objective, reading or viewing format, contribution type and level of assessment. Discovery should support problem-led browsing as well as conventional issue and article navigation. A brief should make its source and limitations easy to reach. The reader should then be able to inspect the full contribution, download a stable version and cite it accurately."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Distribution should initially concentrate on a small number of authorised, relevant channels. Record which channel brings people who actually read and understand the material, rather than rewarding the largest number of impressions. A voluntary email digest may support return visits only after the host has adopted a contact and consent process. No mailing list or distribution agreement is assumed in this business case."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed challenge route closes the loop: readers can submit a structured question about a decision, context and evidence gap. Editors triage it for relevance, ethical concerns and whether existing evidence already answers it. Suitable questions may inform a commissioned review, a case comparison or a future research call. The value is the quality of the question and response, not the number of comments on a page."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Professional development: a useful hypothesis, not accreditation"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PMI's official guidance permits certification-relevant self-directed reading as an Education PDU activity and recognises structured professional discussion as another route. This supports testing a reflective reading record, but it does not make PRAPJ an authorised training provider or guarantee that a specific article qualifies for an individual's claim. Readers remain responsible for the rules applying to their credential. [R02]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A proposed learning record would ask the reader to note the source, date, time spent, relevant professional capability, one learning point and one limitation. It should not automatically generate a certificate or calculate credits from article length. The test should ask whether this small reflection step improves useful return visits; it should not assume that a CPD label creates demand. If a professional body later agrees to a recognised arrangement, publish the exact scope and effective date."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Theory of change and failure points"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed sequence is resources and permission-ready evidence → fair assessment and translation → accessible, trustworthy contributions → comprehension and considered use → better documented learning and professional questions. Every arrow can fail. An article may be published but never found; it may be found but misunderstood; it may be understood but irrelevant; it may be relevant but impossible to apply in an organisation. Benefits evaluation must observe more than the first two stages."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-3.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 3. PRAPJ theory of change and the evidence required at each stage. This is a proposed logic model."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The journal can reasonably claim a contribution to better-informed decisions when readers describe how its evidence was used and, where possible, provide a corroborating artefact. It should not attribute a project's financial savings, schedule improvement or success to one article without a defensible evaluation. Citation counts and page views provide different signals and cannot replace evidence of professional use."}]}, {"id": "s6", "title": "5. Strategic rationale and delivery alternatives", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "What the alternatives explain about the journal choice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Option A uses existing journals, standards, internal learning and professional networks without establishing a new publication. Its advantage is low incremental organisational complexity. Its limitation is that the project cannot control formats, access, editorial priorities or a dedicated practitioner-question route. This option should not be described as doing nothing; a carefully signposted evidence resource can still help readers."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Option B creates a curated evidence digest under an existing institution or professional group. It selects and interprets relevant research, obtains any necessary permissions and distributes concise summaries. It can test reader usefulness before building an original-research operation. It still requires editorial judgement, translation, quality checks and continuity. It does not automatically offer practitioners a durable, independently assessed case-publication route."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Option C develops a partner-hosted collection or practice section with an established publication. It could combine a distinct thematic programme with existing reputation, production and preservation arrangements. The trade-offs are policy compatibility, partner willingness, control over article types and possible charges or limits on ownership. No partner has agreed to this model; the business case treats it as a credible option to investigate, not an available offer."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Option D establishes PRAPJ as a dedicated journal, beginning with a controlled first operating year. It offers the greatest control over the four-track model, reciprocal participation and learning about the service. It also requires the most explicit ownership of editorial, administrative, financial and preservation responsibilities. This control is strategically valuable because it enables one coherent standard for practical interpretation, contribution support and reciprocal research questions. The formal journal direction is the project's intended outcome; the comparison explains the additional responsibilities that accompany it."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Appraisal method and score justification"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Scores range from 1, weak, to 5, strong, against five criteria. The weighted total is the sum of each score divided by five and multiplied by its percentage weight. Weights reflect the project's emphasis on practical value and two-way contribution; they are proposed project judgements, not survey findings. Practical usability concerns the service's designed ability to help a reader; credibility concerns the support for trustworthy assessment; feasibility includes people and continuity as well as cash; participation concerns a contribution route; control concerns the ability to change and learn."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Criterion", "Weight", "A Existing channels", "B Digest", "C Partner collection", "D PRAPJ journal"], ["Practical usability", "25%", "2", "4", "4", "5"], ["Editorial credibility", "20%", "4", "3", "5", "3"], ["Feasibility and affordability", "25%", "5", "4", "3", "2"], ["Two-way contribution", "20%", "1", "2", "4", "5"], ["Control and future learning", "10%", "1", "3", "2", "5"], ["Weighted total /100", "100%", "57", "66", "75", "77"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The high usability and participation scores for D describe its proposed design potential, not observed performance. Its credibility score is lower than C because a new title lacks an established operating record. C's feasibility score remains moderate because negotiation and policy fit are unresolved. B scores well for usability but lower for two-way contribution because summarising published work is not equivalent to assessing original practitioner evidence. A is strong on immediate feasibility and existing editorial credibility, but offers little project control."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "These justifications expose a possible optimism bias: the model rewards D for functions not yet demonstrated. The appropriate corrective is not to add a general confidence percentage to the total, but to test those functions and rescore. At validation, ask the sponsor and at least one practitioner and publishing representative to score independently, discuss disagreements and record the final rationale. Do not average away a hard constraint such as no legal publisher or no funding."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-4.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 4. Illustrative option scores under base and feasibility-priority weights. Scores are analyst judgements, not measured preferences."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Sensitivity and switching conditions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "In the base case D leads C by two points. Reducing D's feasibility score from 2 to 1 reduces its total by five points to 72, so C leads. Raising feasibility weight from 25% to 35% and reducing two-way participation from 20% to 10%, with all other weights unchanged, gives C 73 and D 71. Under that weight transfer, the options tie when feasibility reaches 30% and participation falls to 15%. This demonstrates that the preferred option depends on the sponsor's priorities, not just the arithmetic."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Other single-point changes are similarly material: a one-point reduction in D's practical usability reduces its score to 72; a one-point reduction in credibility gives 73. The numerical exercise therefore cannot establish robust superiority. The journal recommendation instead rests on the project's stated publication purpose and the strategic value of control over its integrated reader/contributor model. Conversely, a partner that cannot offer the proposed practice route or acceptable rights terms may deserve a lower participation or control score. Such a change requires an actual finding, not a convenient assumption to preserve the preferred answer."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Hard conditions and value of further information"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The current task is to establish PRAPJ for formal publication. The alternatives appraisal remains useful because it distinguishes the benefits of a dedicated journal from the services it could obtain through a host or publishing partner. An institutionally supported PRAPJ title can retain its mission while reusing technical and production infrastructure. A practice section within someone else's title offers a different degree of identity and editorial control. These arrangements should not be treated as interchangeable merely because both involve a partner."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before opening real submissions, establish an accountable publishing entity, adopted policies, independent handling, a credible first-content pipeline and available operating resources. No actual capstone budget or committed journal funding currently exists; all resource estimates are hypothetical. Readiness requirements are implementation dependencies, not evidence that the journal lacks a worthwhile purpose. They protect contributors from investing in an unsupported service."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Further information should improve the chosen journal model: reader comparisons refine the article experience; contributor outlines test support needs; reviewer assignments test capacity; and host proposals clarify responsibilities and cost. The same evidence can inform the service configuration and first-year scale. A bounded concept test may be conducted with available in-kind effort; the paid validation estimate is only a hypothetical alternative and does not create an expenditure request."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Recommended sequence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Advance PRAPJ as a formal journal. First, agree its professional value proposition and appoint a continuing implementation owner. Second, choose the supporting institutional or external service arrangement and allocate responsibility for editorial operations, production, preservation and records. Third, recruit a permission-ready founding contribution pipeline and test the reader and contributor experience using the supplied templates and mockups. Fourth, verify resources and workflow readiness, then open submissions and publish the initial contributions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use the first operating year to refine the service with actual evidence. Keep the journal's mission stable while adapting topic priorities, support intensity, publishing cadence and optional media. Should an operating constraint emerge, adjust the implementation plan or defer a specific service commitment under the authorised owner's direction. The case for publication is strongest when the proposed value is matched by a credible ability to deliver it."}]}, {"id": "s7", "title": "6. Operating model credibility and sustainable ownership", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "The decision is a publishing service commitment"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ should be established as a formally published journal with an accountable publishing organisation that accepts the continuing obligations created by publication. Confirm the resources required for real submissions and publication; the current capstone has no actual budget and the operating figures are hypothetical. A functioning website is one component. The service also requires manuscript handling, independent assessment, production, rights clearance, metadata maintenance, complaints handling, correction of the published record and preservation after the founding team leaves. The capstone can prepare those arrangements; it cannot demonstrate that unspecified staff or institutions have accepted them."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The recommended configuration is a journal owned by a confirmed institution, society or other authorised organisation, supported by a named managing editor and an independently led editorial team. Hosting can be purchased or provided institutionally. Ownership should remain explicit even when most technical work is outsourced. The sponsor and continuing owner should organise a controlled first operating year, with scale informed by useful content, reliable operations, manageable effort and credible future support. This is a proposed model, not an assertion that any particular institution is the intended publisher."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before selecting a platform, the sponsor should obtain a short operating agreement covering the journal title, domain, public identity, intellectual-property arrangements, access to editorial records, authority to appoint editors, financial delegations, complaints escalation and succession. The publishing organisation, service provider and Editor-in-Chief should sign only within their respective authority. The agreement should preserve editorial independence while identifying who may respond to a security incident, legal complaint or serious integrity allegation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "What the primary benchmarks establish"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "COPE's core-practices framework covers misconduct, authorship, complaints, interests, reproducibility, ethical oversight, intellectual property, management, review and corrections. Its relevance is that publication ethics depends on implemented processes and trained people as well as written policies. Using the framework does not establish COPE membership, certification or an investigation outcome. The draft PRAPJ policies provide a starting point; the pilot must assign operational owners and test them. [O07]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The joint COPE–DOAJ–OASPA–WAME transparency principles ask journals to make their identity, scope, schedule, review, ownership, editorial team, fees, rights, business model and preservation arrangements clear. They also separate editorial judgement from commercial influence. These are a widely used professional benchmark, not a universal statutory licensing regime. PRAPJ should use them as a public-information audit and record any deliberate deviation with its reason and responsible decision maker. [O08]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Edinburgh Diamond illustrates an institutional division of labour: its library supplies hosting, technical support, identifiers and preservation, while service users retain editorial responsibilities and perform submission, review and production work. Its conditions include institutional publisher identification and a management succession plan. This demonstrates a possible arrangement, not a service available automatically to PRAPJ. The important procurement lesson is to specify exactly which work the library covers and which remains with the journal. [O06]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald Publishing Services describes a diamond open-access partnership in which journal owners retain ownership and receive infrastructure, a publishing manager, ScholarOne configuration, guidance and performance reporting. It advertises a financial outlay agreed in advance, but the inspected page supplies no PRAPJ-specific quote or contractual entitlement. Treat it as a potential service model requiring scope, price and policy negotiation. Its existence supports the feasibility of buying specialist capacity; it does not demonstrate affordability for this project. [O05]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Compare three operating arrangements on the same basis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The following matrix contains PRAPJ decision criteria, not supplier promises. Each option must be evaluated against the same forecast workload and service boundary. A low hosting invoice cannot establish a low total cost when the journal must supply unpaid editorial administration, production and technical support."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Decision dimension", "Institutional OJS service", "Managed hosting service", "Publishing partnership"], ["Necessary sponsor evidence", "Written institutional eligibility, service scope and named budget owner", "Contract with a provider plus an internal journal owner", "Accepted journal proposal and negotiated publishing agreement"], ["Likely internal burden to test", "Editorial office, production and any work excluded by the library", "Editorial decisions, administration and production unless specifically purchased", "Editorial leadership, contributor development, policy oversight and excluded work"], ["Technical accountability", "Named library or IT service; clarify shared responsibilities", "Provider service manager and documented escalation", "Publishing partner and its subcontractors under agreed obligations"], ["Control to preserve", "Journal title, policies, editorial appointments, domain and records", "Same, with data portability and administrator access tested", "Ownership, editorial independence, brand use and termination rights written explicitly"], ["Main uncertainty", "Institutional eligibility, capacity and persistence of subsidy", "Complete service price and ability to manage provider dependency", "Total fee, policy constraints, approval timetable and exit terms"], ["Suitable condition", "A willing institution accepts durable responsibility", "Owner exists but has insufficient technical capacity", "Owner can fund bundled expertise and accepts the negotiated service model"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "OJS supports submission, review, production, publication, identifiers, metadata exchange, usage statistics and import/export. These are software capabilities, not proof that a particular installation is correctly configured, securely operated or accepted by an index. For PRAPJ, platform selection should therefore be followed by a workflow demonstration using synthetic manuscripts, rather than acceptance on the basis of a feature list alone. [O02]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PKP publishes tiered hosting plans with different support and configuration provisions. The financial analysis records the inspected price benchmarks and limitations; obtain a written proposal for the chosen scope. [O03]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PKP describes full installation backups on cancellation. Require a testable exit package, while using the financial chapter's service-boundary analysis to separate hosting, production, administration and preserved content. [O04]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Procurement questions that expose the real cost"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Request a three-year comparison with identical assumptions: 16 published items in the first operating year, the agreed submission pipeline, four contribution tracks, article-level web pages, downloadable articles, linked briefs and two curated collections. Ask each provider to identify inclusions, exclusions, setup charges, recurring charges, volume thresholds, taxes, currency, annual increases and termination charges. The comparison should include a closure or transfer scenario because even a successful pilot may change ownership or service provider."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Work package", "Evidence to request before selection", "Budget treatment"], ["Configuration and training", "Demonstration of each contribution route, anonymity controls and staff permissions", "One-time setup plus training for replacement staff"], ["Editorial administration", "Named tasks and hours for checking files, invitations, reminders and version records", "Internal paid time or explicitly purchased service"], ["Production", "Sample copyediting, typesetting, references, accessible tables and author-proof cycle", "Per-item rate plus exception handling"], ["Identifiers and metadata", "Registration responsibility, included fields, failed-deposit handling and maintenance", "Membership/service fee, registration charges and staff time"], ["Technical operation", "Supported versions, upgrade policy, backup restore evidence and incident escalation", "Recurring service plus out-of-scope work"], ["Preservation", "Agreement, deposit mechanism, covered formats and confirmation evidence", "Service charge, integration and monitoring"], ["Exit and continuity", "Export specimen, readable records, migration assistance and domain transfer", "Exit reserve and continuing archive expense"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The sponsor should require a sample export before purchase. It should include published files, article metadata, licence information, identifiers and relationship links, together with a separate controlled export of editorial history where contractually permitted. A set of public PDFs is not a complete operational transfer. Nor does possession of a database guarantee that the next provider can restore it. Test restoration, character encoding, file associations and permissions in a clean environment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use a weighted appraisal only after excluding options that fail essential conditions. Suggested non-negotiable conditions are an accountable owner, independent manuscript decisions, a usable export, a defined preservation route and affordable minimum service. Weighted preferences may then compare service coverage, accessibility support, staff burden, technical flexibility and three-year cost. Do not award an option a high affordability score simply because the institution has not yet charged for staff time; record the subsidy and its renewal conditions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Roles must survive the founding team"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The following responsibility matrix is proposed for PRAPJ. Accountable means the role answerable for the decision or outcome; responsible means the role doing the work. A single person may hold compatible roles at pilot scale, but the record must distinguish those roles. An author or conflicted editor cannot supply independent oversight of their own manuscript."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Activity", "Accountable", "Responsible", "Consulted or control"], ["Funding, service contract and continuation", "Authorised journal owner", "Sponsor delegate or business manager", "Advisory Board and service provider"], ["Editorial policy and reviewer standards", "Editor-in-Chief", "Research and Practice Editors", "Editorial Board; owner for institutional adoption"], ["Completeness and file classification", "Managing Editor", "Editorial office", "Author; academic decisions remain with editor"], ["Manuscript decision", "Independent handling editor within delegation", "Handling editor", "Independent reviewers; Editor-in-Chief where unconflicted"], ["Translation fidelity", "Translation Editor", "Brief author and translation assessor", "Source author or specialist where needed"], ["Rights and production release", "Owner's authorised publishing role", "Managing Editor and production provider", "Handling editor and corresponding author"], ["Security and recovery", "Named host service owner", "Technical service provider", "Managing Editor and institutional incident contact"], ["Appeal involving the Editor-in-Chief", "Independent publisher-designated handler", "Unconflicted appeal editor or panel", "Relevant evidence holders"], ["Preservation and transfer", "Journal owner", "Managing Editor and technical provider", "Archive provider; successor owner"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "At least two people should be able to perform essential administrative actions. Maintain a role-based account register, a documented process for removing access when people leave, and a monthly check of outstanding submissions. A student's personal email, laptop or cloud account should not be the sole route to author records or journal credentials. The owner should appoint a successor before the capstone team hands over and document how pending decisions will be managed during leave or departure."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Advisory Board should review aggregate performance, relevance and financial resilience. It should not gain access to confidential manuscripts merely because it advises the publisher. Editorial meetings should resolve policy and capacity issues rather than vote on acceptance quotas. Staff performance should combine timely handling, defensible decisions and constructive communication. A target of publishing 16 items must never override evidence or permissions requirements."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "A workflow with observable quality gates"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald's process benchmark distinguishes administrative checking, editorial assessment, reviewer invitations, assessment, decision and subsequent production. It also makes editorial judgement important where reports conflict. PRAPJ can adopt the process logic while retaining its own proposed targets and contribution routes. Emerald's published practices do not establish PRAPJ's actual turnaround time, and no current PRAPJ performance statistic exists. [O19]"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Gate", "Evidence before progression", "Failure response"], ["Complete submission", "Correct files, author approval, declarations, evidence access and article type", "Return with a precise list of missing items"], ["Independent handling", "Conflict check and assigned editor with appropriate authority", "Reassign or seek an external editor"], ["Evidence-ready manuscript", "A clear question or decision, proportionate method, traceable evidence and limits", "Decline, request preparation or agree a more suitable track"], ["Required assessment complete", "Reports from the adopted route and recorded expertise and independence", "Obtain missing assessment; do not substitute a deadline for review"], ["Reasoned decision", "Editor's explanation connects evidence, reports and required changes", "Clarify contradictory reports or obtain specialist advice"], ["Acceptance ready for production", "Accepted version and resolved material scholarly concerns", "Retain revision status until concerns are resolved"], ["Publication ready", "Rights, proof, authorship, declarations, accessible files and correct labels", "Hold publication while documenting outstanding actions"], ["Record maintained", "Tested links, metadata deposit, preservation confirmation and correction route", "Repair and record the action; notify affected parties where appropriate"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For research and evidence reviews, require the adopted minimum of two independent external reports. Cases normally use two reports spanning research and practice expertise. Briefs retain editorial translation assessment; challenges and notes retain editorial and domain assessment with escalation where substantive empirical claims require it. An editor should record why an item belongs in its chosen route. This protects the practical formats without implying that every item received the same assessment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The APM summary programme discussed in the market analysis provides an operating precedent. [O20] PRAPJ should test source fidelity, preservation of limitations and decision relevance in each brief; shorter prose alone does not establish distinctive value."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Capacity is constrained by accepted assignments and completed work"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The following is a transparent planning model, not an observed submission forecast. It retains the existing 16-item portfolio: four research articles, six cases, four briefs and two challenges or notes. Assume 24 research/case submissions, eight desk declines and 16 sent to external assessment; ten ultimately publish. Thirty-two first-round reports are required. Allow another 16 completed reports for revision reassessment, giving 48 reports annually. Content quality, rather than an acceptance quota, determines the actual outcome."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Separate invitation acceptance from completion after acceptance. If half of first-round invitations are accepted and 80% of accepted assignments produce a usable report, expected first-round invitations are 32 ÷ (0.50 × 0.80) = 80. This yields 40 accepted assignments and 32 completed reports. Sixteen reassessment reports require 20 accepted repeat assignments at the same completion rate. Across both rounds, 60 accepted assignments yield 48 reports. A pool of 20 reviewers accepting three assignments each has no remaining capacity margin under these assumptions."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Scenario", "Initial invitation acceptance", "Completion after acceptance", "Expected initial invitations rounded up", "Accepted assignments for 48 reports", "Pool at three accepted assignments each"], ["Strong engagement", "65%", "90%", "55", "54", "18"], ["Planning base", "50%", "80%", "80", "60", "20"], ["Recruitment stress", "35%", "80%", "115", "60", "20"], ["Completion stress", "50%", "65%", "99", "74", "25"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "These expected values are useful for budgeting, not guarantees. The first-round invitation column excludes repeat invitations for reassessment. The pool calculation assumes relevant expertise can be matched across manuscripts and that all invited people remain independent. Real requirements may be higher because expertise is uneven, contributors become conflicted, or repeat reviewers decline. The proposed launch condition is 25 active reviewers for the base workload, with an expertise map and documented assignment capacity. This limits planned use to 80% of expected report capacity, consistent with the financial model; 20 remains the theoretical minimum without a reserve."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed 320 annual Managing Editor hours can be decomposed into testable work packages: recurring administration 104; receipt checks 12; review coordination 32; production coordination 48; metadata and release checks 16; case-permission coordination 18; platform and preservation liaison 32; training and outreach administration 30; contingency 28. These assumptions sum to 320. Copyediting and typesetting are separate production work, while substantive editorial decisions and reviewer reports are separate contributions. This prevents the same hour being counted twice or disappearing inside a hosting fee."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An illustrative 72-hour academic handling allowance comprises 12 hours screening 24 submissions, 40 hours handling 16 externally assessed manuscripts, and 20 hours finalising ten accepted research/case manuscripts. At four hours per completed report, external review adds 192 hours; this excludes any uncompleted or partially completed review effort and is therefore a lower-bound exposure. Eighteen hours for briefs and notes and 72 for advisory/governance work reproduce the earlier 354-hour non-administrative estimate. These are workload assumptions to validate through pilot time logs; they are not evidence that named academics have donated the time."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Identity discoverability selection and metrics are different achievements"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An ISSN identifies a continuing publication and does not guarantee quality or validity. It is associated with a publication title. Different media can have separate ISSNs, brought together through an ISSN-L. PRAPJ should establish its intended title, publisher and media before requesting identifiers. A printed capstone mockup is not itself evidence of a registered print serial or an allocated identifier. [O09, O21]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Crossref registers metadata and persistent identifiers; it does not receive the article files as an archive. Members must maintain their records and update changed URLs. PRAPJ therefore needs article landing pages, a responsible depositor and a correction process for metadata. The presence of a DOI is not equivalent to selection by a citation index, and it does not remove the need for preservation. [O10]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Crossref charges distinguish organisational membership from registration of individual records. The financial chapter gives the current and announced price benchmarks. Select the applicable organisational arrangement before costing the service; a proposed journal budget alone does not establish a university's fee tier. [O11]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Google Scholar's inclusion guidance describes crawlable article pages, appropriate bibliographic metadata and access to article text or abstracts. Meeting technical recommendations supports discovery but does not create a contractual promise that every item will appear. PRAPJ should test actual search results after publication and fix metadata and access barriers, while avoiding any statement that its prototype is already indexed. [O18]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "DOAJ requires at least five research articles annually. New journals need more than one year of publishing history or at least ten open-access research articles. It requires two independent reviewers per article, subject to its arts/humanities exception, and limits research-paper endogeny to 25% under its specified calculation. Preservation and persistent identifiers are recommendations rather than basic requirements. [O01]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Clarify whether cases qualify and how editorially assessed briefs and notes fit the review criterion. Local labels do not establish eligibility. Apply after auditing actual content and policies. Preserve review labels; do not manufacture publications to satisfy a numerical threshold."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Scopus requires peer-reviewed content, a public review description, regular publication, a registered ISSN, publication history, English titles and abstracts, and an ethics statement. Selection also examines journal policy, content, standing, regularity and online availability; technical compliance alone does not guarantee admission. The inspected current page asks for publication history without specifying a universal two-year minimum, so the business case should not invent one. Coverage remains subject to re-evaluation. [O16]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Clarivate's official selection account describes 24 quality criteria and four impact criteria. Its current approach initially assesses new submissions for quality and adds selected journals to ESCI. Journal metrics follow separate collection and reporting arrangements; neither a DOI nor a hosted platform confers a Journal Impact Factor. The practical decision is to demonstrate sustained editorial quality first and investigate a future application when sufficient real evidence exists. No date, quartile or metric should appear as a guaranteed PRAPJ benefit. [O17]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For management reporting, maintain separate fields for identifier assigned, metadata registered, content discoverable, application submitted, selection confirmed and metric released. Each needs a dated evidence link. Use a second dashboard for practical usefulness: readers who can identify the decision and limitation, documented reuse in a real professional task, repeat practitioner participation and answered practice questions. Citation and download counts may complement these measures, but do not establish a causal improvement in project outcomes."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Preserve both the public record and the ability to operate"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PKP PN is a possible preservation route for qualifying OJS journals. Its stated entry conditions include an ISSN, at least one published article, a supported minimum OJS version, activation of the plugin and acceptance of its preservation agreement. Installing OJS is therefore insufficient evidence of participation. PRAPJ would need to inspect successful deposits and resolve failures rather than claiming preservation from a plugin being present. The page's minimum software version is an eligibility statement, not a recommendation to run an obsolete release. [O13]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "CLOCKSS is another possible arrangement. It receives content under preservation agreements and makes triggered content freely available when specified circumstances prevent continuing access. Publisher participation includes fees; the inspected material does not supply a PRAPJ quote. A dark archive should not be mistaken for ordinary website hosting or rapid operational recovery. Service selection must consider the trigger conditions, formats deposited and who maintains routine public access before a trigger. [O14]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Keepers Registry monitors archival arrangements reported by participating agencies. It can help verify which service holds which journal and reveal coverage gaps. It is a monitoring service, not the journal's backup provider or proof that every newly published item has already been ingested. PRAPJ should reconcile its publication inventory with deposit receipts and independently visible coverage where available. [O15]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ needs three distinct records: the public version of record and its corrections; a preservation copy with bibliographic metadata and rights; and restricted operational records such as decisions, reviews, permissions and correspondence. Give each a named custodian, retention decision, access rule and recovery procedure. Do not deposit consent forms or reviewer identities in the public archive. Before opening submissions, restore a synthetic submission and its attachments; before claiming preservation, confirm a real authorised publication deposit."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A linked brief should remain connected to its source if the host changes. Crossref supports typed relationships including derivation, basis and translation. A plain-language adaptation is not necessarily a translation into another language, so choose a relationship that describes the actual connection and verify it with the metadata provider. Add human-readable source links and correction links as well. Metadata supports navigation; the translation editor remains responsible for the brief's fidelity. [O12]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Supported contribution and multimedia responsibilities"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Practice Editor should own the proposed interview-to-manuscript route. An initial conversation is followed by a source and permissions check, an agreed outline, supported drafting, contributor verification and formal submission. The handling editor must distinguish author recollection from documentary evidence, record material discrepancies and retain enough context to assess transferability. Editorial assistance is disclosed and authorship follows the adopted contribution policy. Acceptance is assigned independently of the person who provided substantive writing assistance. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A multimedia lead or designated production editor should maintain a derivative-content register: source contribution and version, purpose, audience, transcript or caption file, permissions, factual approval, assessment label and release date. Every media item links back to its evidence source, and corrections to the source trigger a review of derivatives. Do not call a promotional interview or explainer peer reviewed merely because it discusses a reviewed article. Short student explanations should retain the limits of the parent evidence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The initial owner should explicitly choose the volume of assisted conversations and media work. The finance chapter provides a hypothetical optional extension rather than silently absorbing unlimited support into the core workload. Track hours spent on each supported outline and media item during the first cohort. If support exceeds capacity, agree a later slot or a simpler format before promising a publication date."}]}, {"id": "s8", "title": "7. Financial feasibility, resource capacity and long-term sustainability", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decision and evidence basis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The capstone project has no approved or allocated actual budget and the journal has no committed funding. All PRAPJ financial amounts in this chapter are hypothetical scenarios: costs, income receipts, paid effort, contributed effort, contingency, reserves and funding gaps. No figure authorises spending or reports actual expenditure. Public supplier prices are external benchmarks as of their recorded research dates, not PRAPJ budgets or quotations. The project lead confirmed the overall status on 8 October 2026. [L9]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The recommended journal model is diamond open access, supported by an accountable institution or coalition so that readers and authors can participate without a publishing charge. The financial analysis explains what a future publishing arrangement would need to sustain that offer. It makes labour, quality and continuity visible rather than assuming that a website and goodwill are sufficient. Formal publication is the intended outcome; host-specific resource commitments and service scope are implementation requirements to resolve before opening real submissions. None of the four interviews constitutes a financial commitment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The existing core scenarios remain useful for comparing scale and support. A new optional scenario below identifies the additional effort associated with James's interview-supported contribution and multimedia ideas. The calculations describe possible service designs, not approved activities or payments. In-kind provision can replace a cash line only when its scope and capacity are confirmed; it does not make the underlying work disappear. [L8, MODEL]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The 2021 OA Diamond Journals Study surveyed 1,619 journals and disclosed geographical and participation biases. It described reliance on volunteers, financial uncertainty and multiple forms of institutional support; approximately 60% depended on volunteers, 25% reported a loss and almost one-third did not know their financial status. These historical, heterogeneous results are not estimates for PRAPJ. They support making work and commitments visible. [F01]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The 2022 Diamond Open Access Action Plan connects shared infrastructure, editorial capability, governance, financial administration and institutional support. For PRAPJ, it supports developing a complete publishing service with named owners. It is a policy framework, not a funding offer. [F02]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "What public prices do and do not establish"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Current service information allows several costs to be checked without pretending that a complete journal can be purchased for a hosting fee. PKP’s current journal-hosting table lists annual Basic, Professional and Enterprise prices of $925, $1,650 and $3,000 respectively, and institutional hosting by quotation. Basic uses a shared installation with standard themes and plugins; more tailored configurations require other plans. The table displays dollar symbols without an explicit currency label in its accessible price headings, so procurement must confirm currency and scope. These list prices are preserved as published and are not converted to AUD or substituted into the model. [F05]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Crossref’s schedule, last updated 11 September 2026, lists annual membership of USD 275 for the USD 1,000–1 million publishing-revenue-or-expense tier and USD 1 per current journal-article registration in 2026. The relevant tier depends on the publishing activity of the member organisation, not simply the PRAPJ budget. For an eligible standalone member in that tier, 16 such records would imply USD 291 before optional services or intermediary charges. A host may already provide registration through a broader arrangement; that must be checked before buying another membership. [F03]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Crossref has announced that current journal-article registration falls to USD 0.95 from 1 January 2027. At unchanged illustrative membership, 16 registrations would therefore total USD 290.20. These small registration amounts show why the AUD 1,000 allowance must be understood as a bundle for identifiers, metadata work, preservation-related support and administration, rather than described as “DOI fees”. No exchange rate or applicable membership arrangement has been assumed. [F04]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A locally relevant route exists: the University of Sydney Library’s Sydney Open Journals page invites researchers from the University or affiliated organisations to enquire about starting an open-access journal or transferring an existing OJS journal. That is evidence of a service pathway worth evaluating, not evidence that PRAPJ qualifies, that hosting is free, or that the Library has accepted responsibility. The request for support must specify the continuing academic owner, audience, editorial capacity, files, service boundaries and succession arrangements. [F07]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "All prospective model values below are original planning assumptions [MODEL]. The model definitions appendix provides formulas and the monthly cash schedule."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Cost structure and boundaries"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The base first-year model retains the existing 16-item mix: four Research Articles, six Practitioner Cases, four linked Research-to-Practice Briefs and two Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes. The briefs are separate publications but are linked to four research contributions. Thus 16 outputs do not represent 16 independent research findings. Planning reports should show both the number of publications and the twelve contributions excluding linked briefs. Quality thresholds govern acceptance; the mix is a capacity design, not an acceptance quota."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["First-year cash component", "Calculation or scope assumption", "AUD"], ["Managing editor", "320 paid hours × $55", "17,600"], ["Production", "16 items × $350", "5,600"], ["Hosting, support and backups", "Annual service allowance", "3,000"], ["Identifiers, preservation and metadata", "Services and administration allowance", "1,000"], ["Accessibility and author support", "Annual allowance", "2,000"], ["Outreach and evaluation", "Annual allowance", "1,500"], ["Policy and agreements", "Annual establishment/maintenance allowance", "2,000"], ["Planned operating cash", "Sum before contingency", "32,700"], ["Contingency", "15% of planned operating cash", "4,905"], ["Hypothetical cash ceiling", "Operating cash plus contingency", "37,605"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This structure makes the largest controllable dependency visible: paid coordination represents 53.8% of the planned cash subtotal, compared with 9.2% for hosting. Selecting cheaper hosting cannot resolve an undefined editorial workload. Conversely, adding sophisticated website functions can create development and support obligations absent from a small publishing budget. The model assumes a standard supported workflow and the reuse of the supplied templates; custom integration, extensive redevelopment or a bespoke manuscript platform must be separately quoted."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The hourly amounts are cost assumptions rather than statements about salary entitlements or market pay. Before procurement, the host must identify the employment or contracting arrangement, taxes, employment on-costs, insurance, indirect charges and purchasing rules that apply. The present model excludes unconfirmed on-costs and institutional overhead; they are not zero-cost facts. A sensitivity adds 25% to paid coordination as an illustrative test, without presenting that percentage as a legal or institutional rate. The production allowance similarly requires specification of copyediting depth, page/word limits, table complexity, accessible PDF/HTML, metadata, corrections and rounds of author proof."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Validation is a separate one-off stage: 80 coordination hours at $55, plus $1,200 for test materials and specialist advice, totals $5,600 before contingency and $6,440 with 15% contingency. It is additional if paid. If the work is actually supplied within approved capstone effort, those hours should be recorded as an in-kind contribution and the cash requirement reduced accordingly. Completed student work is not assumed to recur after graduation. The $2,000 annual policy allowance remains in the operating model for establishment, review and specialist handling; quote-based estimates should split startup work from later maintenance."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Optional contributor-support and multimedia scenario"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "James's recommendations add value only if the journal can provide the support it describes. A hypothetical small extension comprises six assisted contribution conversations and four short derivative media items in Year 1. Assume three editorial hours for each supported conversation and its initial fact-checked outline, and six production hours per media item for planning, editing, source checks and captions/transcript. A 30–60-minute conversation is only one part of the effort. Final manuscript development, independent review and author work remain separate; complex cases may exceed these allowances. These hours are assumptions, not measured productivity. [L8, MODEL]"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Optional activity", "Hypothetical calculation", "Incremental AUD"], ["Six supported conversations and initial outlines", "6 × 3 hours × AUD 55", "990"], ["Four short source-linked media items", "4 × 6 hours × AUD 55", "1,320"], ["Incremental planned cash subtotal", "18 + 24 = 42 additional hours", "2,310"], ["Additional 15% contingency", "2,310 × 15%", "346.50"], ["Incremental hypothetical ceiling", "2,310 × 1.15", "2,656.50"], ["Core plus optional extension", "37,605 + 2,656.50", "40,261.50"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The extension is not included in the core AUD 37,605 scenario or in its three-year and monthly charts. Add it only for work demonstrably outside the core 320 managing-editor hours, AUD 2,000 accessibility/author-support allowance and normal per-item production scope. If existing allowances cover part of it, deduct that overlap before recalculating contingency. Equipment, platform storage, travel, external transcription, translation and extensive post-production are excluded and require separate scope decisions. No funds or providers are committed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The first implementation choice is whether to reallocate limited effort within the core service or add supported media after the initial text workflow is stable. Test one coherent content family and record both user response and staff time. This gives a future owner evidence for scale without promising a broad media service before capacity exists. If support is offered in kind, record the hours and service boundary; a zero cash line does not mean zero resource cost."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-5.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 5. First-year cash envelopes by service scenario, including contingency. These are planning assumptions in AUD."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Cash, in-kind work and economic resources"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A cash budget alone substantially understates the model’s dependence on professional contribution. In the base year, academic and practice handling is estimated at 24 externally reviewable submissions × three hours, or 72 hours. Forty-eight completed external reports at four hours each require 192 hours. Assessment of the four briefs and two challenges at three hours each adds 18 hours. Six advisory members contributing twelve hours each add 72 hours. The total is 354 contributed hours. At an assumed replacement value of $90 per hour, this equals $31,860. These amounts are neither fees offered to participants nor independently verified market prices."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Two totals must be distinguished. Planned operating resources, excluding contingency, are $32,700 cash plus $31,860 contributed expertise: $64,560. The resource envelope inclusive of cash contingency is $69,465. Describing the latter as actual or expected economic expenditure would imply that all contingency is spent. Both totals exclude author writing time, capstone work, partial effort on uncompleted reviews, and any currently unpriced host resource. They provide a transparent partial resource account, not a complete societal cost estimate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "In-kind support should be recorded by owner, task, hours or service scope, duration, and replacement consequence. If a library supplies hosting that genuinely displaces the $3,000 line, that line can be removed from the cash subtotal and recorded separately as institutional support. The 15% cash contingency would then also fall by $450 under the current rule. The journal has saved $3,450 of cash requirement, not eliminated the service’s resource use. The same task must never remain in both a supplier invoice and the donated-labour estimate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Review capacity constrains publication growth"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The base pipeline assumes 24 research/case submissions, eight desk declines and sixteen manuscripts entering external review. Ten of those are ultimately accepted: four research articles and six cases. Two reports per reviewed manuscript produce 32 initial completed reports. Eight manuscripts are assumed to require a further two reports, producing sixteen revision reports and 48 completed reports overall. Briefs and challenges follow their own assessment routes and do not inflate this external-review count. These are planning assumptions, not observed acceptance statistics."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use a consistent completion convention. If each reviewer accepts three assignments annually and 80% of accepted assignments yield a completed report, twenty reviewers supply an expected 48 reports: 20 × 3 × 0.8 = 48. That is the base need with no headroom. To secure 32 initial completed reports at 50% invitation acceptance and 80% completion, the expected invitation count is 32 ÷ (0.5 × 0.8) = 80. At 35% acceptance it becomes 115 after rounding up. The sixteen revision reports require another twenty accepted assignments at the same completion yield; this is not an additional set of forty new reviewers."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Completion assumptions do not guarantee suitably independent expertise. A roster may be numerically adequate while lacking a methods specialist or practice reviewer for a particular submission. A more resilient capacity design limits planned use to 80% of the expected report capacity. It would require twenty-five reviewers for 48 reports, compared with the bare minimum of twenty. The corresponding growth-year rosters are 32 and 38 for 60 and 72 reports. These are operational design targets, not evidence that enough reviewers will volunteer. Record named capacity and coverage before opening intake."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-6.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 6. Required reports compared with expected reviewer capacity. Assumed completion is distinct from planned utilisation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Three-year scenarios"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Years 1–3 are operating years beginning only after a pilot is authorised; they are not launch commitments for 2026–2028. The model applies a uniform 3% annual price escalation to cash rates and replacement values solely as a planning sensitivity, not an inflation forecast. Scenarios are alternatives and are not assigned probabilities. They retain a 15% cash contingency and no author, reader or advertising revenue. Each includes the same separate $6,440 validation ceiling for comparability."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Scenario", "Publication volumes Y1 / Y2 / Y3", "Cash ceilings Y1 / Y2 / Y3, AUD", "Three-year cash including validation"], ["Constrained service envelope", "12 / 12 / 12", "18,285 / 18,834 / 19,399", "62,957"], ["Controlled growth, conditional", "16 / 20 / 24", "37,605 / 42,997 / 48,679", "135,722"], ["Higher support and scale", "24 / 28 / 32", "74,405 / 83,863 / 93,821", "258,528"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The constrained case preserves the original low assumptions: 200 managing-editor hours at $45; $200 production per item; and $4,500 of annual non-production service allowances before escalation. It represents a thinner service envelope, not the same service delivered more efficiently. Its twelve-item mix is three research articles, four cases, three linked briefs and two challenges. It still depends on 36 completed external reports and 285 contributed hours annually. A low cash request is therefore not sufficient evidence of viability."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Controlled growth expands only after demonstrated use, suitable submissions, funding and capacity: sixteen items in Year 1, twenty in Year 2 and twenty-four in Year 3. Managing-editor time increases from 320 to 360 and 400 hours. The formula is 160 annual coordination hours plus ten hours per published item, used as an aggregate planning proxy; the pilot must replace it with observed effort by submission, report, revision and publication. Production remains $350 per item before escalation, and other service allowances total $9,500."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Controlled growth workload", "Y1", "Y2", "Y3"], ["Research / cases / briefs / challenges", "4 / 6 / 4 / 2", "5 / 8 / 5 / 2", "6 / 9 / 6 / 3"], ["Research/case submissions received", "24", "30", "36"], ["Sent to external review", "16", "20", "24"], ["Completed external reports", "48", "60", "72"], ["Minimum reviewer roster at model yield", "20", "25", "30"], ["Roster at 80% planned utilisation", "25", "32", "38"], ["Paid managing-editor hours", "320", "360", "400"], ["Contributed expertise hours", "354", "423", "495"], ["Replacement value of contributions, AUD", "31,860", "39,212", "47,263"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The higher-support scenario starts at the original high estimate of $74,405 for twenty-four items. Paid coordination rises from 500 to 560 and 620 hours at a starting rate of $65; production is initially $550 per item and other annual allowances total $19,000. This scenario deliberately combines more support with more volume. It is not a statistically derived upper bound. By Year 3 its 32 items require 96 completed reports and 636 contributed hours, before author effort or incomplete-review effort."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A constant sixteen-item control is useful because growth is optional. At 3% annual escalation, cash ceilings are $37,605, $38,733 and $39,895; three-year cash plus validation is $122,673. The controlled-growth pathway therefore requires approximately $13,048 more cash across three years for twelve additional publication items. That marginal difference should be judged against the value and staffing consequences of the expanded mix, not used to promise economies of scale or returns."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-7.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 7. Conditional three-year cash ceilings; operating years begin only after authorisation. Validation is shown in the chapter totals."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Cash timing and funding runway"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An annual award can cover the budget while arriving too late to pay bills. The illustrative first-year cashflow assumes hosting, identifier/metadata and policy allowances of $6,000 are paid in month 1; managing-editor cost is spread evenly; accessibility support is paid in months 3 and 9; outreach in months 6 and 12; and production of two items per month occurs in months 5–12. Planned cash expenditure is $32,700. The $4,905 contingency remains unspent in this schedule, rather than being automatically distributed across months."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Assume, only for illustration, an anchor pays $25,000 in month 1 and other supporters pay $12,605 in month 7. The schedule stays cash-positive and ends with $4,905. If the second payment slips to month 10, month 9 closes at minus $450, despite the same total annual funding. At least $450 of timely bridging liquidity is needed to avoid a negative balance; $5,355 would be needed to keep the entire contingency untouched at that low point. The bridge is a timing requirement, not another operating expense."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A launch decision should therefore examine monthly unrestricted cash, due invoices, contractual commitments and the credibility of payment dates. An expression of interest is not cash, and a grant restricted to outreach cannot automatically pay editorial wages. Before accepting additional manuscripts, the journal should be able to finish or responsibly transfer its existing obligations without relying on an unapproved renewal. The finance owner should show both an annual funding gap and a rolling thirteen-week cash view."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A separate working-capital reserve is a sponsor policy choice. As an explicit illustration, three months of first-year non-production planned cash equals $27,100 ÷ 4 = $6,775. If required in addition to the operating ceiling, initial funding including paid validation would be $50,820. This reserve is not an expense, not part of the three-year operating totals and not a standard imposed by an external source. Its final amount should follow the host’s payment terms and closeout obligations. It must not be counted again as spent contingency."}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-8.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 8. Illustrative monthly closing cash under different funding timings; no funding shown is committed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Funding architecture and concentration risk"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The initial proposition should be an institutional public-interest service, with an anchor providing accountable ownership and core coordination and supporting partners funding defined annual contributions or shared services. Any contribution should have a period, payment schedule, restrictions, renewal notice, acknowledgement rules and a clear boundary preventing influence over individual manuscript decisions. Funding and editorial authority need separate documentation even where one institution supplies both people and money."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A consortium is a credible design pattern, but its existence elsewhere is not evidence of commitments here. SCOAP3 describes collective institutional funding, a central contracting arrangement and a redirection of existing subscription resources to support publishing without charges to authors. Its field, installed relationships and existing expenditure base differ materially from a new project-management title. PRAPJ cannot assume that comparable subscription money is available to redirect. The transferable lesson is coordinated funding and accountability, not an immediately obtainable subsidy. [F09]"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Funding route", "Potential role", "Main condition or weakness for PRAPJ"], ["Host operating allocation", "Coordination, service ownership and continuity", "Needs an actual budget owner and a defined multi-year or renewable commitment"], ["Professional or scholarly partners", "Cash support, dissemination or documented service contributions", "Protect editorial independence; expressions of interest do not establish funds"], ["Library/shared-platform support", "Displaces specified infrastructure tasks", "Verify eligibility, scope, service levels and succession; it may exclude production"], ["Time-limited grant", "Validation, setup, accessibility or transition", "Separate one-off investment from a recurring salary and publishing obligation"], ["Donations or voluntary membership", "Supplementary unrestricted support", "Collection costs and uncertain renewal make it unsuitable as the sole launch assumption"], ["Sponsorship or events", "Possible supplementary activity", "No demand evidence; quantify staff effort, restrictions and conflicts before assuming surplus"], ["APCs or subscriptions", "Different funding model", "Excluded from the preferred diamond model; requires a separate mission and demand decision"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The example $25,000 anchor supplies 66.5% of the $37,605 ceiling. Losing half that support creates a $12,500 gap against the ceiling and a $7,595 gap even against planned cash before contingency. Eliminating only hosting and outreach would not close that planned-cost deficit. Response options are replacement support, a smaller service with fewer new obligations, a partner pathway, or managed cessation. An unannounced author charge is not a sound contingency."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "University read-and-publish agreements should not be mistaken for startup-journal finance. Sydney’s current guidance applies to eligible corresponding authors and covered journals, and states that the Library does not pay APC invoices issued directly to authors. This support mechanism does not establish a grant for PRAPJ’s editorial operation or an entitlement for a new title to join an agreement. [F10]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Sensitivity, alternatives and financial decision rules"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Change to base first-year assumption", "Change in cash ceiling, AUD", "Revised ceiling, AUD"], ["Managing-editor hourly assumption +20%", "+4,048", "41,653"], ["Production unit assumption +30%", "+1,932", "39,537"], ["Additional 80 coordination hours", "+5,060", "42,665"], ["Pay for half the modelled reviewer hours at $90/hour", "+9,936", "47,541"], ["Host replaces only the $3,000 hosting line", "−3,450", "34,155"], ["Illustrative 25% on-cost on paid coordination", "+5,060", "42,665"]]}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-9.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 9. First-year cash sensitivity to selected assumptions, relative to the AUD 37,605 base ceiling."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The largest tests concern labour and its availability. Paying for half the reviewer hours transfers $8,640 of replacement value from the in-kind account into planned cash and adds $1,296 of cash contingency. It does not create another 96 hours of review resource. Conversely, needing longer reports increases the economic workload even if reviewers remain unpaid. At six rather than four hours per completed report, reviewer time grows by 96 hours and its assumed replacement value by $8,640; paid coordination could also increase if the more demanding manuscripts require additional handling."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Two counterfactuals should be evaluated on comparable scope. A twelve-brief digest illustrates a smaller service: 160 coordination hours at $55, twelve production units at $350, and the same $9,500 service allowances give $22,500 planned cash or a $25,875 ceiling. Thirty-six hours of translation assessment are separately valued at $3,240. This is an original conservative planning alternative, not a quotation; it provides interpretation of existing evidence but does not offer the same original research/case publication route or independent research-review system."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For a partner collection, leave the external service price unknown. If PRAPJ retains 160 coordination hours, $1,500 outreach and a $2,000 agreement allowance, its local subtotal is $12,300. Let Q represent a fully scoped partner quotation for the delegated services. The cash ceiling is 1.15 × ($12,300 + Q). Cash parity with the base journal occurs at Q = $20,400, before tax and omitted obligations. This threshold is useful for negotiation and comparison, but does not establish that any publisher will offer that price, accept the project or supply equivalent review, rights, access and continuity terms."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Financial approval should depend on scope-matched quotations, a fundable host, realistic reviewer/editor capacity and evidence that the chosen option addresses a distinct need. No monetary return on investment is calculated: the project has neither a validated willingness-to-pay model nor attributable benefits capable of supporting a defensible return estimate. The renewal question is whether documented reader and contributor value, fair assessment and a maintainable publication record justify the continuing resource commitment relative to the alternatives."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Continuity and the procurement questions that matter"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PKP’s FAQ describes an archived hosting option at USD 450 annually and explains that its preservation network does not preserve the editorial/review history needed to reconstruct an active journal. This distinction means the host must determine who retains confidential workflow records, exports, account control and published content if the pilot ends. An archive fee is a useful benchmark, not the total price of PRAPJ closure. [F06]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before a pilot decision, obtain written answers on: the legal publisher; service inclusions; paid and donated roles; production scope; applicable taxes and on-costs; currency and renewal pricing; backup restoration; export of public and confidential records; identifier maintenance; preservation eligibility; incident responsibility; continuity if an editor leaves; funding restrictions; and the cost of stopping. Update the model with actual quotes, preserve the original assumptions for comparison and record who accepts residual gaps. The finance plan should then be reviewed monthly in operation and formally before any growth or renewal decision."}]}, {"id": "s9", "title": "8. Publication validation, benefits and readiness", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Validation should test behaviour and feasibility"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Validation should refine PRAPJ for formal publication: confirm which content helps readers, make the contribution route practical, test evidence and permissions, calibrate assessment, and establish the resources needed to deliver reliably. Comparison with existing resources identifies where the journal experience can improve. The four completed stakeholder interviews inform this design; the reader-test protocol below is proposed future work, with no results or commitments represented as collected."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Recruit ten practitioners from at least three organisations and two sectors, including people who rarely read journals. Focus initially on experienced and mid-career professionals, with a mix of sponsor/PMO, consulting and delivery roles rather than recruitment entirely from academic contacts. Include a small secondary check with students or emerging professionals for derivative explanations without conflating the two audience needs. Record role, sector, usual information sources and potential prior connection to the project. This is a formative sample chosen to expose problems; it will not estimate population preferences with statistical precision."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use two comparable evidence tasks, for example a risk-assumption decision and a lessons-transfer decision. Give participants a PRAPJ specimen and an appropriately sourced existing article or summary dealing with comparable subject matter. Do not compare an unusually polished new specimen with an irrelevant or deliberately difficult alternative. Counterbalance reading order where practical. Clearly label synthetic specimen content; it can test comprehension and navigation, but cannot establish the usefulness of real empirical findings. A later test with cleared real content is needed before launch."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Ask each participant to identify the decision the item could inform, describe the main evidence, state one material limitation and explain what additional information they would need before applying it. Record unaided responses and elapsed time. Only then ask for preference, perceived credibility and likelihood of returning. Invite criticism and ask when the item would be unhelpful. Avoid coaching the reader toward the answer or introducing a positive project description before the comprehension task."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Interview and multimedia test improvements"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Retain an open closing question in every future interview: 'Is there anything we have not discussed that you think would benefit PRAPJ or be important for the project?' James's response to that question introduced multimedia as a material design issue. Ask about video and other formats explicitly in future tests, while leaving room for unanticipated needs. Record contrary views and distinguish a participant's suggestion from a team decision. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Test the same substantive lesson in a written brief and a short media explanation, keeping the evidence and limitations comparable. Ask participants which format helps them understand the objective and conditions of use, whether captions/transcripts are useful, where they would encounter it, and when they would choose the full article. Measure comprehension and completion before preference. Format appeal is not evidence of better learning or improved project results. The test should also record production time and accessibility effort so that preferences can inform a feasible publication programme."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For the supported-contribution route, invite a feasible practice example and record the initial conversation, records required, confidentiality questions, outline approvals and editing time. Capture whether the contributor understands their authorship responsibility and the independent assessment route. A positive reaction to being interviewed does not establish willingness to approve or publish the resulting article. Treat James's 30–60-minute example as a possible conversation length, not a full publication-service promise."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Proposed test measures and decision rule"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Test", "Proposed formative threshold", "Interpretation and limit"], ["Decision and limitation comprehension", "At least 7 of 10 readers identify both within five minutes on each task", "A practical usability gate, not proof of general effectiveness"], ["Comparative preference", "At least 6 of 10 prefer the PRAPJ approach after both comparisons; one judgement per participant", "Review reasons and role differences; a narrow majority is weak evidence"], ["Concrete contribution pipeline", "Twelve expressions of interest with topic, evidence source and date, including at least four research and four case prospects", "A lead is not a submission or a permission approval"], ["Permission feasibility", "At least two cases have documented routes to sufficient evidence and owner clearance", "Necessary seed supply; not a full year's accepted content"], ["Reviewer readiness", "For the core PRAPJ scenario: 25 active, subject-mapped reviewers committing three accepted assignments annually", "Proposed launch gate at 80% planned utilisation of expected report capacity; twenty is the theoretical minimum without a reserve"], ["Publication ownership and resources", "Named continuing owner and verified cash or cost-replacing service capacity for the adopted scope", "No budget or funding is currently available; all financial figures remain hypothetical"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Agree a correct-answer rubric before testing. Score comprehension separately for each task, and collect one comparative preference per participant after both tasks. The thresholds are proposed management rules, not results or established industry benchmarks. Record the denominator and all failures. Seven successful readers out of ten does not mean that 70% of the market will benefit. With such a small purposive sample, one or two participants can change the apparent percentage materially. If the concept passes narrowly, review the specific difficulties and retest the affected design; do not convert the percentage into a revenue forecast."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The contributor test should require a one-page outline: the decision or question, context, available records, observed outcome, author involvement, confidentiality constraints, permission owner and realistic drafting date. Classify prospects as interested, evidence-feasible, permission-feasible, draft-ready and submitted. Keep permission feasibility distinct from permission granted. A pipeline dominated by enthusiastic but inaccessible cases is a warning about the model, not a healthy backlog."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The reviewer test should ask about specific subjects, conflicts, willingness to assess both methods and application, assignments per year and current availability. A short calibration exercise using a synthetic manuscript can reveal disagreement over evidence sufficiency and review labels. Record whether the process produces a reasoned editorial decision and how much time it takes. Do not use real confidential manuscripts without an authorised handling arrangement."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Benefits register"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Benefit and intended beneficiary", "Indicator and collection method", "Proposed owner", "Main attribution limit"], ["Clearer interpretation for professional readers", "Correct decision and limitation identification in a structured test", "Research and evaluation lead", "Comprehension does not demonstrate later use"], ["Better-supported professional decisions", "Voluntary follow-up example with source link and, where possible, a redacted decision artefact", "Practice Editor", "Multiple information sources and organisational factors contribute"], ["More assessable practice evidence", "Cases progressing from outline to cleared, review-ready manuscript", "Practice Editor", "Editorial support may reveal that some cases are unsuitable"], ["Practice-informed research questions", "Questions developed into a documented research or review proposal", "Research Editor", "Subsequent research may occur outside PRAPJ"], ["Fair, predictable contribution experience", "Stage timings, reasons for delay and confidential author feedback", "Managing Editor", "Fast processing alone is not a quality measure"], ["Sustainable institutional capability", "Documented succession, cost coverage and tested records transfer", "Journal owner", "Annual subsidy may remain essential"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Separate output, outcome and longer-term impact. An output is a published contribution. An early outcome is that a reader correctly understands and uses its evidence. A longer-term impact might be stronger project learning or decisions, but causal attribution will usually be weak in a small pilot. Use contribution narratives with their limitations rather than attributing financial savings to article counts."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Operating dashboard and measurement definitions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before first publication, capture a baseline from the concept test and the actual pipeline. For each subsequent month, record submissions by track, complete submissions, desk decisions, manuscripts under review, accepted assignments, usable reports, accepted articles, published items and waiting time by stage. Separate author revision time from editorial handling time. Report both the median and the longest unresolved waits when volumes are small; a single average can conceal a stuck manuscript."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed service measures remain targets: at least 80% of completeness checks within five working days and scope decisions within ten working days; at least 70% of accepted reviewer assignments completed by the agreed date. Track overdue assignments separately from all eventual completions so the 80% modelled completion yield is not confused with the on-time target. A report received late may count as completed but not on time."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "At six operating months, seek at least five of eight follow-up participants able to describe a concrete use or well-reasoned decision not to apply an item. At twelve months, seek eight of twelve. Count and explain missing responses rather than replacing them only with enthusiastic readers. These are small learning samples, not representative estimates. Include examples of misunderstood or unhelpful content and the resulting editorial change."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For reach, report article views, full-text accesses and repeat visits using the host's documented definitions and available privacy controls. State bot filtering and the limits of identifying unique users. A brief and its parent article should be reported separately as formats and together as one evidence family when assessing overall contribution. Do not add downloads, page views and email impressions into a single invented impact total."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "At twelve months, assess the 16-item planning volume alongside evidence quality, permissions, readership learning, actual cost, volunteer burden and funded renewal. Fewer high-quality items may be preferable to meeting a quota through weak or unsuitable content. Conversely, a high output count with a departing editor and no funding is not sustainable success. The continuation decision should record which benefits occurred, which did not and what changes are affordable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Usefulness, return and recommendation measures"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "James distinguishes producing a feature from demonstrating its benefit. Extend the dashboard beyond article counts with a short optional response after reading or viewing: rate usefulness from one to five; identify the decision it helped clarify; state one condition or limitation; and indicate whether the reader would return or recommend the item. Report the distribution and number of responses, the invitation denominator where available, missing responses and how participants were recruited. Do not present an average of a few enthusiastic responses as a market-wide satisfaction score. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed management learning targets, to be agreed before collection, are: at least ten substantive usefulness responses across each initial collection, a median usefulness rating of at least four out of five among respondents, and documented reasons for low ratings. These are hypothetical targets rather than industry benchmarks or observed outcomes. Report whether the same people respond repeatedly. Return intention and recommendation intention remain self-reports; actual repeat visits require the host's privacy-appropriate measurement and a stated observation window."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "At six and twelve months, combine these signals with the existing comprehension and use follow-ups. Seek permission before publishing any testimonial, identify the content and context, and retain critical feedback alongside positive examples. A useful decision not to apply a finding can be evidence of responsible learning. Do not use media views to claim adoption or add views of an article, brief and video as though they were independent people or independent studies."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Publication-readiness gates"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Gate", "Required evidence", "Decision and responsible authority"], ["G0 Capstone acceptance", "Business case, board TORs/shortlists, guides/templates and both mockups checked", "Team and sponsor accept or request revisions to preparation outputs"], ["G1 Publication direction and implementation ownership", "PRAPJ mission, priority readers and continuing accountable lead agreed", "Sponsor confirms the formal-publication programme and authorised preparatory work"], ["G2 Service arrangement and first-content plan", "Publisher/host scope, contribution support, initial pipeline, resource assumptions and policy responsibilities", "Proposed owner selects the support arrangement and practical first-year scope"], ["G3 Opening submissions", "Adopted policies, available resources, staffed handling, permissions workflow and rehearsal; core scenario proposes 25 subject-mapped reviewers", "Authorised publishing owner approves the real submission opening and public statements"], ["G4 Six-month service review", "Content quality, contributor experience, reader usefulness, workload and resource outlook", "Owner and editors improve the service and adjust commissioning or optional formats"], ["G5 Twelve-month publication review", "Benefit evidence, quality record, actual operating accounts, succession and next-period resources", "Owner approves the next publication programme and continuity arrangements"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "These gates organise the intended launch; they do not imply that publication or funding has already been authorised. The project currently has no actual budget, and financial scenarios remain hypothetical until a future authorised owner makes and records a resource decision. Formal adoption of the journal's policies and operating commitments is separate from accepting the capstone files."}]}, {"id": "s10", "title": "9. Delivery plan, accountability and handover", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Work to the capstone deadline"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The capstone deadline is 20 November 2026. The four interview dates below are confirmed completed events; subsequent activity windows are planning proposals from the 8 October update and depend on team availability and access. Interview completion does not establish that unrelated tasks in the source schedule are complete. A revised Microsoft Project XML schedule accompanies the package, with source assumptions and inherited progress explicitly distinguished from verified interview facts."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Date or period", "Status and work", "Reviewable output and dependency"], ["31 August 2026", "Completed interview: Paul Rodman", "Written interview summary available"], ["3 September 2026", "Completed interview: Louis Taborda", "Written support account added 10 October;interview date unchanged [L12]"], ["24 September 2026", "Completed interview: Michael Hopmere", "Written interview summary available; identity/date confirmed"], ["8 October 2026", "Completed interview: James Bawtree", "Detailed written summary available; integrate practical design insights"], ["9–16 October", "Proposed: validate priority reader and contributor experience; seek host scope information where authorised", "Test notes, contribution outlines and documented service requirements"], ["17–30 October", "Proposed: consolidate formal-publication proposition and implementation responsibilities", "Sponsor presentation narrative, resource scenarios and readiness record"], ["31 October–6 November", "Proposed: check all documents, website copy and both mockups together", "Consistency record and physical print proof from the editable Word specimen"], ["7–13 November", "Proposed: independent team review and sponsor feedback", "Corrected package with unresolved operational decisions recorded"], ["14–19 November", "Proposed: final references, formatting, version freeze and handover rehearsal", "Final editable files, responsibility record and submission copy"], ["20 November 2026", "Confirmed submission deadline", "Capstone submission confirmation and retained archive"]]}, {"type": "image", "src": "assets/doc-01-figure-10.png", "alt": "Figure from Business Case; read the surrounding source text for context."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Caption", "text": "Figure 10. Confirmed interview dates and proposed remaining capstone work to 20 November 2026."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The key delivery sequence runs through evidence review, the assumptions and decision record, consistency across the publication materials, and team/sponsor review. The website team's work can proceed with approved copy and the mockup while publishing ownership remains under discussion, provided unconfirmed appointments, fees and submission-opening claims remain clearly held for release."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Team responsibilities proposed from the charter"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The charter provides the initial division of work, and the supplied schedule records a later 17 September allocation. Jinchi Han coordinates integration; Tingting Yu maintains controls; Xinyao Wang leads governance; Ningyue Gao leads engagement; Juan Fan leads quality assurance. The later schedule assigns Jinghao Liu to planning and analysis, including the WBS, schedule and feasibility, and Ningxin Xu to research and documentation, including website, author and publication files. Use that later allocation when reconciling the earlier charter, and confirm the current team assignment before treating it as an instruction. Lydia Wen is the project sponsor. Capstone responsibilities do not automatically become permanent journal appointments. [L5, L11]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Integration should maintain a single decision log containing the decision, rationale, source, affected documents, owner and date. Research should retain source access limitations, not simply a bibliography. Publication development should ensure that the Guide for Authors, website copy and mockups use the same tracks, limits and review labels. Quality assurance should check the user journey from selecting a track to completing the correct template and understanding the assessment process."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Acceptance and change control"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The sponsor review should ask whether the report makes a persuasive, evidence-based case for formal publication, explains the reader and contributor offer, exposes hypothetical financial assumptions and sets an achievable implementation programme. It should not ask reviewers to accept a conclusion simply because the document is long. The companion governance review should test whether decision rights are clear and candidates remain tentative. The publication-material review should test usability and consistency, including the difference between a demonstration and a real submission service."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For each requested change, record whether it alters scope, policy, cost, evidence or presentation. A changed contribution type may affect the guide, templates, review workflow, cost model, website copy and mockups simultaneously. A corrected source URL may affect only the reference register. The integration lead should identify these dependencies before approving a version freeze. Do not quietly make divergent copies of the same policy for different audiences."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Operational handover after the capstone"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A continuing journal needs an owner beyond the student team. Handover should include the adopted policies, current role and access register, supplier and funding agreements, outstanding manuscript status, rights records, metadata/export instructions, preservation evidence, complaints route and annual review calendar. Confidential records should transfer only through the authorised host's controlled process. Public website copy and blank templates do not need the same access level as reviewer identities or complaint records."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Demonstrate the handover through a synthetic manuscript: locate the applicable policy, assign an unconflicted editor, record the assessment route, produce a reasoned decision, create a publication-ready record and retrieve it after an export/restore exercise. Then demonstrate an editor's departure and an appeal. These exercises reveal whether responsibility is actually transferable, rather than merely documented."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Confirm a continuing owner before the student team hands over operational responsibilities. If that appointment is still pending at the capstone deadline, submit the complete preparation package and record the named authority responsible for completing the transition. Keep the formal-publication objective in the handover record, with a clear outstanding-action list. Students should not inherit indefinite obligations to maintain a public site, confidential manuscripts or author expectations by default."}]}, {"id": "s11", "title": "10. Risk, resilience and the publication recommendation", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Risk approach"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The register below is a qualitative planning assessment. Priority reflects the potential to invalidate the model or damage trust, not a calculated probability or expected monetary loss. These are prospective operating risks because the journal has not yet begun publishing. Each risk needs an observable trigger, a response and an accountable role. Contingency is not a substitute for a response plan, and the 15% financial allowance is not evidence that every risk has been costed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Risk and priority", "Early warning or trigger", "Proposed response and owner"], ["Insufficient distinct reader value — high", "Readers prefer existing resources or cannot name a useful decision", "Improve problem focus, formats and distribution; sponsor and Practice Editor"], ["Weak permission-ready case supply — high", "Many expressions of interest but no assessable cleared evidence", "Offer bounded case support; reduce commissioning; Practice Editor"], ["Unfunded continuing work — high", "Endorsement without confirmed cash or cost-replacing service scope", "Resolve resources and adjust first-year scope before opening submissions; journal owner"], ["Reviewer exhaustion or mismatch — high", "Repeat declines, uncovered methods/topics or overdue reports", "Recruit reserve, adjust intake and report workload; Editor-in-Chief"], ["Sponsor or author conflict — high", "Pressure to accept, suppress findings or choose a favourable reviewer", "Independent handling and recorded escalation; owner and unconflicted editor"], ["Misleading practical translation — high", "Brief's recommended action exceeds source findings", "Require claim-to-source checks; correct linked outputs; Translation Editor"], ["Confidentiality or rights failure — high", "Unclear permission or identifiable sensitive project material", "Hold release, seek authorised clearance or decline; publishing role"], ["Key-person departure — high", "Only one person holds credentials or decision knowledge", "Shared role coverage and rehearsed handover; owner"], ["Production scope underestimated — medium/high", "Extra rounds, complex figures or accessibility repairs exceed allowance", "Requote exceptions and prioritise quality; Managing Editor"], ["Metadata or preservation gap — high", "Failed deposits, broken DOI targets or no deposit confirmation", "Assign monitoring and repair; host and Managing Editor"], ["Delayed funding receipt — high", "Annual commitment exists but cash arrives after costs fall due", "Secure upfront working capital or defer commitments; finance owner"], ["Indexing expectations outrun eligibility — medium/high", "Authors assume immediate DOAJ, Scopus or metric status", "Publish accurate status and staged criteria; Editor-in-Chief"], ["Biased contributor network — medium/high", "Founding institution dominates authorship and review", "Broaden commissioning and independent review; Editorial Board"], ["Weak evidence of benefit — medium", "Good traffic but few credible examples of comprehension or use", "Redesign evaluation and content; advisory/evaluation lead"], ["Uncontrolled expansion — medium/high", "Extra formats, events or data services added without resources", "Change request with owner, effort and funding; sponsor"], ["Capstone deadline pressure — medium", "Validation results are unavailable close to submission", "Record gaps and transfer next steps; integration lead"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Resilience through controlled scale"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The first response to overload should be to control intake and commitments, not to weaken review or silently extend every deadline. Editors can close a special call, limit commissioning, defer a collection or publish fewer items while preserving standards. A documented reserve reviewer pool and deputy roles reduce fragility, but cannot substitute for a realistic scope. The workload model should be refreshed after the first real cohort because assumed hours, revision rates and completion yields may be wrong."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "There are at least three viable continuity outcomes: renew the independent journal; transfer or partner while preserving the published record and rights; or stop new submissions and maintain access to existing content. Each can be responsible. An unmanaged disappearance is the outcome to avoid. Closing intake should trigger author communication, decisions about pending manuscripts, funded archive/identifier maintenance and an accurate public notice under the owner's authority."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Assumptions requiring active ownership"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Assumption", "Current status", "Evidence needed before commitment"], ["Readers value this bundle over an existing alternative", "Design proposition informed by three interview summaries, Louis’s expanded confirmed-support account and comparator research", "Comparative task results and reasons for preference"], ["Sixteen items is a manageable first-year portfolio", "Planning volume", "Actual submission mix, editor time and production scope"], ["Cases can be shared with sufficient detail", "Unproven beyond supplied discussion", "Cleared evidence routes and real outlines"], ["Qualified reviewers complete 80% of accepted assignments", "Modelling assumption", "Recorded cohort performance and reserve availability"], ["AUD 55/hour and other unit costs reflect the host's cost", "Scenario input", "Fully specified quotes and applicable on-costs/taxes"], ["Diamond funding can continue beyond the first year", "Preferred model, no commitments", "Written funding duration, renewal and exit terms"], ["A host can supply the required service", "Candidate arrangement", "Named ownership and a signed service boundary"], ["The mixed portfolio can meet a chosen directory's criteria", "Unresolved", "Current criteria review and classification of actual articles"], ["The capstone team can transfer responsibility cleanly", "Proposed plan", "Named recipient and completed handover exercise"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Sponsor implementation register"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The recommended decision is to endorse PRAPJ's establishment as a formally published journal and progress the implementation programme. Record the rationale, accountable person and date for each required arrangement: authorised publisher and host; continuing operating ownership; priority professional audience; initial content programme; resource requirements and actual commitments when available; appointment and complaints authority; four-track policy and service targets; rights, licence and access model; reviewer and production capacity; contributor support and optional media; public submission opening; and continuity responsibilities. The journal direction is clear; unconfirmed operating decisions remain visibly open."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ is worth publishing because it can give credible research and documented professional experience a consistent, practical route into project decisions, while giving practitioners a fair route into the published knowledge base. Michael and Paul identify the value and barriers of that exchange; James shows how to make participation and use more concrete; Louis’s confirmed support reinforces the connection between research, professional judgement and education, as explained in the expanded account. [L12, W31] The proposed evidence, review, context and contribution standards turn that promise into a reviewable publication model."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The strongest invitation to readers and contributors is a specific, trustworthy offer: useful problems, transparent evidence, clear limits, supported preparation and independent editorial decisions. The next work is to make that service ready for formal publication. Accepting the capstone confirms that the preparation deliverables have been reviewed; opening the journal confirms that the authorised owner can fulfil its publishing commitments. All current budget figures remain hypothetical, with no approved project budget or committed journal funding."}]}, {"id": "s12", "title": "Appendix A. Publication design and benchmark decisions", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "What is borrowed and what remains a PRAPJ choice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The recommended publisher resources are useful because they expose the complete publication journey: choosing an outlet, preparing files, passing editorial checks, obtaining assessment, revising, producing a final version and maintaining the published record. Emerald's publishing entry pages and peer-review hub were read as reference material for that journey. They provide external benchmarks and do not establish a publishing relationship. The project has developed original PRAPJ policies and templates from the relevant needs, while retaining the supplied guide's contribution structure. [E1–E3]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald's linked principles and review-model resources make fairness, confidentiality, appropriate expertise and clear assessment arrangements visible. The bias guidance and review-preparation material help identify the questions a reviewer should answer and the risks of judging an author or institution instead of the evidence. PRAPJ applies those concerns through conflict checks, explicit assessment criteria and reasoned decisions. Its proposed review routes and time targets still require editorial adoption and operational testing. [E4, E5, E7, E8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The IJPM site provides a useful content architecture: journal scope and identity; author and publishing guidance; editorial information; article discovery and reading; and routes into submission. Its author guide demonstrates that detailed file, ethics and publication requirements belong in a stable author resource. PRAPJ's website team can use this functional pattern while using the original content supplied in files 05–06. No private IJPM submission workflow was tested, and no screen, publisher identity or journal metric has been copied into a claim about PRAPJ. [J1, J2]"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Benchmark issue", "Proposed PRAPJ design", "Reason and decision still required"], ["Scope and audience", "A clear account of project decisions, evidence and practitioner contribution", "Readers and authors should know the journal's purpose before selecting a track"], ["Author preparation", "A separate Guide for Authors and reusable manuscript/private-file templates", "Supports consistency without placing the whole guide inside the Business Case"], ["Research assessment", "At least two independent external reports for Research Articles and Evidence Reviews", "Provides a clear proposed minimum; reviewer capacity and policy adoption remain necessary"], ["Practice assessment", "Normally two case reviewers spanning practice and research; distinct labels for briefs and notes", "Matches evidence type while preventing blanket peer-review claims"], ["Practical interpretation", "Source-linked briefs with explicit decision relevance and transfer limits", "Addresses comprehension without treating the brief as a second independent study"], ["Editorial decisions", "Editor-owned, reasoned decisions; independent handling of conflicts and appeals", "Reviewer recommendations are evidence for the decision, not a vote or an acceptance guarantee"], ["Production and release", "Rights, proof, metadata, accessible output and accurate public status", "Acceptance alone is not permission to release an incomplete publication record"], ["Post-publication responsibility", "Corrections linked to affected articles and briefs, with an owner for records", "Practical summaries can become misleading if their source changes"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Preserve a coherent contribution model"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The expanded business case does not replace the supplied guide with another publisher's word limits. Research Articles remain 6,000–9,000 words, with Evidence Reviews as a research subtype at 5,000–8,000. Practitioner Cases remain 3,000–5,000, Research-to-Practice Briefs 1,200–2,000, and Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes 1,500–2,500. Invited Editorials or Debates at 1,500–3,000 are supplementary; teaching cases remain deferred. James's proposed videos and micro-content are supplementary delivery forms linked to the core evidence, not extra manuscript tracks. Their volume, review labels, permissions and workload require an explicit operating decision. [L8] [L3, L4]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research abstracts remain 200–300 words; other core contributions use a 150–200-word summary. Core items include a 100–150-word decision-relevance statement and four to seven keywords. The guide's word-count convention includes main text, footnotes, table text, captions and published appendices; it excludes the title page, abstract/summary, keywords, relevance statement, highlights, declarations, references and separate supplements. No borrowed per-figure word allowance is added. These are proposed PRAPJ requirements, not universal journal standards. [L3]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The linked-brief proposal introduces additional work and should be costed as such. Authors of accepted research prepare a linked brief with translation support, but a research article ready for publication should not wait indefinitely for its brief. The published relationship and dates should remain explicit. Where a brief interprets external research, the editor must also consider rights, source access and whether the summary represents the findings accurately."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Policy differences that need an explicit adoption decision"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald's author and publication-ethics resources address authorship, permissions, integrity and the use of AI tools. PRAPJ's current proposed author approach permits disclosed and verified preparation assistance and differs from Emerald's restrictions on generating manuscript prose. The PRAPJ review process separately prohibits generative-AI assessment or the creation/editing of confidential review reports and prohibits uploading confidential review material. A future service agreement requires a policy comparison; it does not silently replace the proposed PRAPJ guide. [E9, E11]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed appeal window is 30 calendar days from the decision, including desk decisions. A handler independent of the disputed decision should acknowledge within five working days, aim to resolve within 30 calendar days and issue progress updates every 20 working days while unresolved. These are proposed service targets, not legal entitlements or measured performance. Minor and major revisions use proposed 30- and 60-calendar-day targets; extensions may be agreed. Publisher-specific deadlines from another journal are not automatically imported."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Diamond open access, author-retained copyright, a non-exclusive publishing grant and CC BY 4.0 remain proposals requiring the host's adopted agreement and accurate treatment of third-party material. CC BY 4.0 permits sharing and adaptation, including commercial use, subject to attribution, licence information and indication of changes. It does not remove privacy or other rights. The business case recommends deciding these terms before submissions open rather than resolving them after authors have invested in a manuscript. [P2]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Accessibility and the website team's handoff"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The content handoff should include readable headings, plain link labels, meaningful article metadata, text equivalents for figures, accessible tables and clear notices about review status. WCAG 2.2 provides a technical accessibility benchmark for web implementation; a polished static mockup is not a conformance assessment. The website team should test the actual implementation with keyboard navigation, assistive technology and realistic long article content. [P3]"}]}, {"id": "s13", "title": "Appendix B. Model definitions and reproducibility", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Financial model identity"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[MODEL] denotes the original PRAPJ planning model prepared for this report. The capstone has no approved or allocated actual budget and no journal funds are committed. All PRAPJ amounts labelled AUD, including costs, hypothetical receipts, allowances and labour valuations, are assumptions rather than quotes, allocations or actual expenditure. Year 1 starts when the journal begins its authorised operating programme; Years 2 and 3 are conditional future operating years. The equations below allow a reviewer to reproduce the calculations without relying on an unexplained chart. Tables round to whole dollars; calculations retain decimals until the final presentation step."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Quantity", "Formula and interpretation"], ["Planned cash", "Managing-editor hours × rate + items × production unit cost + specified service allowances"], ["Cash ceiling", "Planned cash × 1.15; the 15% allowance is a scenario contingency, not forecast spending"], ["Annual escalation", "Year n unit-cost multiplier = 1.03 raised to n−1; this is a sensitivity assumption"], ["Contributed expertise", "Handling + completed review + translation/note assessment + advisory hours"], ["Resource cost before contingency", "Planned cash + contributed hours × assumed replacement rate"], ["Resource envelope", "Cash ceiling + contributed expertise value; excludes author effort and unpriced resources"], ["Funding gap", "Remove verified displaced cash costs, recalculate contingency, then subtract confirmed eligible cash; include validation/reserve only if chosen"], ["Initial invitations", "Required first-round reports ÷ (invitation acceptance × post-acceptance completion), rounded up"], ["Bare reviewer roster", "Reports ÷ (accepted assignments per person × completion yield), rounded up"], ["Resilient reviewer roster", "Reports ÷ (assignments × completion yield × 80% planned utilisation), rounded up"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Completion yield and planned utilisation are different. Completion yield estimates how many accepted assignments produce usable reports. Planned utilisation limits how much of the resulting expected capacity is committed, leaving some reserve. At three accepted assignments, 80% completion and 80% planned utilisation, each reviewer supports 1.92 planned completed reports. Forty-eight reports therefore require 25 reviewers. The model assumes compatible expertise and independence; it cannot guarantee either."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Base cost-driver detail"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The base fixed service allowances total AUD 9,500: hosting 3,000; identifiers/preservation/metadata 1,000; accessibility/author support 2,000; outreach/evaluation 1,500; and policy/agreements 2,000. At 16 items, 320 managing-editor hours at AUD 55 and production at AUD 350 give 17,600 + 5,600 + 9,500 = 32,700. Contingency adds 4,905 for the 37,605 ceiling. The 320 hours include operating slack for uneven work; the separate cash contingency protects the budget. Procurement should define the intended cover to avoid charging twice for the same risk."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Controlled growth uses 160 fixed coordination hours plus ten hours per published item: 320, 360 and 400 hours for 16, 20 and 24 items. This is an aggregate proxy, not a claim that accepted articles are the only source of work. The operating model separately decomposes the base hours by task. Replace the proxy when time logs show how submissions, revisions and production exceptions actually drive effort."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The constrained case assumes 200 hours at AUD 45, production at AUD 200 and AUD 4,500 in services. The high-support case starts with 500 hours at AUD 65, production at AUD 550 and AUD 19,000 in services, then adds 60 coordination hours for each four-item annual increase. These scenarios change service depth as well as output. They should not be plotted or interpreted as a confidence interval around one fixed service."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The one-off paid validation calculation is (80 × 55 + 1,200) × 1.15 = AUD 6,440. No scenario assumes fee, subscription or advertising revenue. At preparation, confirmed funding is zero, so the modelled gap equals the full chosen requirement. If a host confirms support later, record its scope and duration before deducting it."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Optional extension calculation"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The James-informed support/media extension assumes six conversations plus initial outlines at three hours each and four short media items at six hours each. At the hypothetical AUD 55 rate, (6 × 3 + 4 × 6) × 55 = AUD 2,310. A 15% contingency adds AUD 346.50, giving AUD 2,656.50. Core plus extension is AUD 40,261.50. These are additional hours only to the extent they are not already covered by the core 320 hours, existing author/accessibility support or normal production allowance. This extension is not inserted into the unchanged core monthly or three-year charts; it is a separate scenario and has no funding commitment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Monthly base cash schedule"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This table demonstrates liquidity, not agreed payment terms. It starts with zero cash, uses the planned AUD 32,700 expenditure before contingency and assumes illustrative income of AUD 25,000 in month 1 plus AUD 12,605 in month 7. The stress column delays the second receipt to month 10. A negative balance shows the need for timing changes or bridging liquidity; it is not permission to overdraw."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Operating month", "Planned cash cost", "Closing cash: receipt M7", "Closing cash: receipt M10"], ["1", "7,467", "17,533", "17,533"], ["2", "1,467", "16,067", "16,067"], ["3", "2,467", "13,600", "13,600"], ["4", "1,467", "12,133", "12,133"], ["5", "2,167", "9,967", "9,967"], ["6", "2,917", "7,050", "7,050"], ["7", "2,167", "17,488", "4,883"], ["8", "2,167", "15,322", "2,717"], ["9", "3,167", "12,155", "-450"], ["10", "2,167", "9,988", "9,988"], ["11", "2,167", "7,822", "7,822"], ["12", "2,917", "4,905", "4,905"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The finance chapter and charts use the same core data. Paid validation, reserves, unconfirmed taxes/on-costs and closure charges are excluded here. A future adopted budget must resolve these exclusions, regardless of the illustrative balance."}]}, {"id": "s14", "title": "Appendix C. Source register", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Supplied project records"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L1. PRAPJ Interview.docx. Michael Hopmere interview summary, interview 24 September 2026; identity and date confirmed by the project lead on 8 October 2026. Relevant sections: Quick recap; Project Management Research-Practice Gap; Practitioner Journal Pilot Planning; Journal Evaluation Metrics Discussion. Preparation chatter excluded. The project lead confirmed the identity; public attribution permission remains separate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L2. Paul Rodman Industry Interview Summary, 31 August 2026, approximately 28 minutes. File: Paul_Rodman_Interview_Summary_English.docx. Q1–Q7 and sections 3–6 used; no recording or transcript independently inspected."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L3. PRAPJ_Guide_for_Authors.docx. Supplied proposed author policy; no adoption date. Basis for contribution types, lengths, disclosures, files and the linked-brief model."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L4. PRAPJ_About_Publish_and_Website_Content_Proposal.docx. Supplied proposed public copy. Missing licence passage and incomplete trailing text identified for revision."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L5. PMGT5850_2026S2_Team DOMINUS_Journal Business Case.pdf. Preliminary charter and blueprint, 15 slides. Pages 9–14 define scope, success, constraints and gates; page 15 contains the preliminary WBS and timetable. The confirmed 20 November deadline supersedes that timetable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L6. Screenshot at 12.29.51 on 25 September 2026. Project proposal and Success Measures. Used as the deliverable acceptance basis, not proof of market size."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L7. Screenshot at 12.31.39 on 25 September 2026. Dr Hopmere's message recommending Emerald publishing and peer-review resources. It does not constitute a publishing partnership."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L8. PRAPJ_James_Bawtree_Interview_Summary_EN_Updated.pdf. James Bawtree interview, 8 October 2026; eight-page detailed summary of a recorded interview. Full supplied summary examined. It paraphrases the transcript and notes transcription errors; direct public quotation requires recording checks and permission."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L9. Project lead confirmations in the project conversation, 8 October 2026: four interview identities/dates; no approved or allocated actual project budget; all financial figures hypothetical; no committed journal funding; formal-publication intention; no PDF deliverables. These instructions supersede inconsistent earlier planning assumptions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L10. Original project-lead account of Louis Taborda's interview, conducted 3 September 2026, supplied 8 October. It recorded general support for establishing PRAPJ. Retained as the original evidence trail and supplemented by L12"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L11. Supplied PMGT5850_2026S2_TeamD1_Project_ Journal Business Case.mpp. The file contains Microsoft Project XML despite its extension. Original schedule and cost fields are retained in a source audit; interview dates and the 20 November deadline are updated in a correctly named editable XML deliverable. Other inherited progress is not independently verified."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L12. Louis Taborda support account, prepared for the Business Case on 10 October 2026 following the project lead's report that Louis had confirmed his support. The 600-word third-person text combines that reported supportive position, the public biographical context in W31 and explanatory analysis of PRAPJ's potential value."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "L13. Project lead confirmation in the project conversation, 10 October 2026: the sponsor has signed the Project Charter and PMP; final deliverables have not yet been accepted. Final presentation and capstone deadline: 20 November 2026. Signed planning documents do not constitute operational launch authorisation or final acceptance."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Public biographical context"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "W31. University of Sydney (18 March 2024). Community of expert educators to transform student experience"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Official historical profile inspected 10 October 2026. It documents Louis Taborda’s earlier mathematics and computer-science teaching, IT consultancy, project-management teaching, experiential-learning research and industry-linked student projects. Used only for professional and educational context; it does not establish University support for PRAPJ, a current appointment, or PRAPJ-specific interview views."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Scholarly, journal, operating and funding sources"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M01. Winter, M.; Smith, C.; Morris, P.; Cicmil, S. (2006). Directions for future research in project management: The main findings of a UK government-funded research network"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.1016/j.ijproman.2006.08.009. Primary author-affiliated institutional abstract and bibliographic record inspected. Agenda-setting synthesis, not a representative demand study or an evaluation of a new journal. No claim to full-paper review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M02. Cicmil, S.; Williams, T.; Thomas, J.; Hodgson, D. (2006). Rethinking Project Management: Researching the actuality of projects"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.1016/j.ijproman.2006.08.006. Primary institutional abstract and metadata inspected. Linked publisher-version PDF retrieval was blocked. Methodological/conceptual argument is not an outcome estimate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M03. Chaves, M. S. (2021). Narrowing the research-practice gap in project management"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.5585/gep.v12i3.17227. Full 24-page published paper retrieved from the author's institutional repository; abstract, introduction, recommendations and conclusion inspected. Position paper, not a controlled test. Publisher landing page returned an access error. Recommendations are not measured effects."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M04. Van de Ven, A. H.; Johnson, P. E. (2006). Knowledge for theory and practice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.5465/amr.2006.22527385. Primary author-affiliated abstract and bibliographic record inspected, supplemented by publisher-indexed abstract. Conceptual argument; no causal effect size or journal-market evidence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M05. Mohrman, S. A.; Gibson, C. B.; Mohrman, A. M., Jr. (2001 (institutional working paper: 2000)). Doing Research That Is Useful to Practice: A Model and Empirical Exploration"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.5465/3069461. Publisher-indexed abstract and USC research-centre record inspected. Crossref publisher-deposited metadata confirms Academy of Management Journal 44(2), 357-375, April 2001; platform's later online date is not treated as original publication year. Perceived usefulness is not a causal project-performance outcome."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M06. Božič, K.; Bachkirov, A. A.; Černe, M. (2022). Towards better understanding and narrowing of the science-practice gap: A practitioner-centered approach to management knowledge creation"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.1016/j.emj.2021.09.006. Primary author-affiliated abstract and bibliographic record plus publisher-indexed abstract/introduction inspected. Qualitative sample does not estimate Australian project-management demand or population prevalence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M07. Makin, S. (2021). The research-practice gap as a pragmatic knowledge boundary"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.1016/j.infoandorg.2020.100334. Publisher-indexed introduction and substantial section excerpts inspected; direct page opening returned 403. Crossref publisher-deposited record verified author and Information and Organization 31(2), 100334, June 2021. Single embedded case; no general effect size inferred."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M08. Bansal, P.; Bertels, S.; Ewart, T.; MacConnachie, P.; O'Brien, J. (2012). Bridging the research-practice gap"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.5465/amp.2011.0140. Primary author-affiliated abstract and bibliographic record inspected, with the originating network's account as corroboration. Reflective institutional experience is not proof that an independent PRAPJ journal is the best vehicle."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M09. Locatelli, G.; Ika, L.; Drouin, N.; Müller, R.; Huemann, M.; Söderlund, J.; Geraldi, J.; Clegg, S. (2023). A Manifesto for project management research"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.1111/emre.12568. Publisher-indexed substantive text, issue metadata and author-affiliated published-version records inspected. Agenda-setting perspective, not a representative reader study. Authors' editorial positions are historical disclosure, not evidence of current appointments or PRAPJ support."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M10. Tkachenko, O.; Hahn, H. J.; Peterson, S. L. (2017). Research-Practice Gap in Applied Fields: An Integrative Literature Review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "DOI: 10.1177/1534484317707562. Primary author-affiliated abstract and bibliographic record inspected. Detailed themes, study counts and effect claims are not reproduced because full review methods/results were not inspected. General applied-field evidence requires contextual interpretation for PRAPJ."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M11. Association for Project Management (Undated current webpage). International Journal of Project Management (IJPM)"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Full accessible official association page inspected. Publisher homepage returned 403 in this research pass. This association page is not treated as an exhaustive inventory of current article types, policies, prices or metrics."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M12, O20. Association for Project Management (Undated current webpage). APM Research Summary Series"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Full accessible programme page inspected. The page establishes service design and APM's rationale, not quantified impact, unmet market size or demand for a competing service."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M13, J4. Emerald Publishing (Undated current webpage). International Journal of Managing Projects in Business"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Publisher-indexed page text and official journal-finder description inspected; direct fetch returned 403. Promotional positioning does not independently demonstrate practical impact. Historical metrics and acceptance-rate figures are not used for market share or PRAPJ forecasting."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M14, J5. Project Management Institute (Undated current webpage). Project Management Journal"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Full accessible official PMI page inspected. The page's reader and citation metrics are not used because comparability and denominator details were not established. Membership benefit does not mean every article is inaccessible to non-members."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M15, J6. UTS ePRESS (Archive covers 2016-2018; migration notice refers to 2020). Project Management Research and Practice: UTS archive and migration notice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Full accessible official archive homepage inspected. This is a historical comparator; the archive cannot establish current output, ownership, operational health, closure or failure. Current linked site was not successfully assessed in the wider project review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M16. University of Malaya (Undated current webpage). Journal of Project Management Practice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Official university indexed page text inspected; direct opening timed out. Scope describes the intended offering, not proven practitioner uptake. No author charge or throughput claim is inferred."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M17. International Project Management Association (Undated current webpage). IPMA Journals: Project Leadership and Society"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Full accessible official association page inspected. Direct publisher page returned 403. Earlier launch announcements and expired fee-waiver promotions were not treated as current policies; no APC amount or turnaround promise is used."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "M18. Inderscience Publishers (Undated current webpage). International Journal of Project Organisation and Management"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Accessible official publisher page inspected. The topics and publication model are positioning evidence; article-level practical utility and market share are unmeasured. Optional open access must not be described as a wholly open-access journal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O01. Directory of Open Access Journals (n.d.). Guide to applying Version 2.9"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Live criteria may change. Mixed-format treatment and whether specific practitioner cases qualify require clarification; no PRAPJ application or acceptance exists."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O02, P1. Public Knowledge Project (n.d.). Open Journal Systems"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Product description establishes capabilities, not configuration quality, platform operation, indexing admission or a service contract."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O03, F05. Public Knowledge Project (n.d.). OJS Journal Hosting"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "The inspected plan table did not explicitly state the currency. Do not treat the dollar figures as AUD; verify currency, taxes and all inclusions through a written quote."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O04, F06. Public Knowledge Project (n.d.). Hosting and Services FAQ"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Vendor description is not a negotiated service agreement. Full backup usability, migration assistance, retention and service responsibilities need testing and confirmation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O05, E10. Emerald Publishing (n.d.). Emerald Publishing Services"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Marketing/service description, not a PRAPJ-specific offer. No price, acceptance, institutional affiliation or guaranteed indexing inferred."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O06. University of Edinburgh Library Edinburgh Diamond (2024). Roles and Responsibilities"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Published 21 August 2024. This is an example only; no inference that PRAPJ qualifies for Edinburgh support or that another university supplies equivalent terms."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O07. Committee on Publication Ethics (n.d.). Our core practices"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Direct PDF fetch failed. Primary-source indexed PDF text was retrieved and read; verify the currently operative document when adopting policy. Not a claim of membership or certification."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O08. COPE DOAJ OASPA and WAME (2022). Principles of Transparency and Best Practice in Scholarly Publishing Version 4"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Published 15 September 2022. Best-practice principles are distinguished from a statutory licence and from individual services’ formal admission criteria."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O09. ISSN International Centre (n.d.). What is an ISSN"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Specific identifier applications and media/title treatment must be confirmed with the responsible ISSN centre. No number has been allocated to PRAPJ."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O10. Crossref (n.d.). Content Registration"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "A DOI is not evidence of citation-index selection or preservation. Actual membership and deposit arrangements are unconfirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O11, F03. Crossref (n.d.). Fees"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Live US-dollar fees observed 25 September 2026; subject to change. Sponsored membership may include additional sponsor service charges; no AUD conversion or contractual quote is inferred."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O12. Crossref (n.d.). Relationships"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "The correct relation for a plain-language brief depends on the actual content relationship and provider implementation; no automatic semantic mapping is claimed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O13, F08. Public Knowledge Project (n.d.). PKP Preservation Network"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "The minimum version listed on the page is not a recommendation to run an old release. Actual journal acceptance and successful preservation deposits remain unverified."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O14. CLOCKSS (n.d.). FAQs"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "No PRAPJ price or agreement exists. Service description does not replace ordinary hosting, backup recovery or confirmation of coverage."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O15. ISSN International Centre (n.d.). Keepers Registry"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "It is not an archive or backup provider; published title coverage is not proof that every new item has been deposited."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O16. Elsevier (n.d.). Scopus content policy and selection"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "The current inspected page requires publication history without stating a universal two-year minimum. Readiness does not guarantee inclusion or a particular timetable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O17. Clarivate (n.d.). Journal evaluation process and selection criteria"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Direct retrieval returned 403; the official page’s detailed indexed search extract was read. Recheck before any application. No guaranteed collection placement, metric or date."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O18. Google Scholar (n.d.). Inclusion Guidelines for Webmasters"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Technical readiness does not guarantee discovery of every item. Prototype content is not evidence of actual indexing."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O19, E6. Emerald Publishing (n.d.). The journal peer review process"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Publisher-specific practices do not establish PRAPJ policy or actual turnaround statistics. Existing PRAPJ routes and targets remain proposals."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "O21. ISSN International Centre (n.d.). The ISSN-L for publications on multiple media"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "This does not establish that a capstone print specimen is an independently registered print publication."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "F01. Science Europe / cOAlition S commissioned consortium (2021-03-09). The OA Diamond Journals Study: recommendations and findings"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Material inspected: Recommendations printed pp. 25 and 32 (PDF pp. 27 and 34); Findings overview and methodology printed pp. 7–8 and 13–14 (PDF pp. 45–46 and 51–52). Historical multi-country survey; not a representative Australian PM-journal cost estimate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "F02. Science Europe, cOAlition S, OPERAS and ANR (2022-03). Action Plan for Diamond Open Access"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Material inspected: Printed pp.3–6: efficiency, capacity, governance and sustainability. Policy recommendations, not an available PRAPJ funding award."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "F04. Crossref (2026). Fee update: lowering and removing DOI registration fees in January 2027"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Material inspected: Table: current journal-article fee USD 1.00; January 2027 USD 0.95. Announced future registration price; membership arrangements separately assessed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "F07. University of Sydney Library (Current webpage). Sydney Open Journals"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Material inspected: Starting a journal; institutional or affiliated researchers; OJS platform. Support pathway only; no PRAPJ eligibility, acceptance, cost or service commitment established."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "F09. SCOAP3 (Current webpage). Frequently Asked Questions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Material inspected: Business model, central CERN arrangement and pooled subscription resources. Different discipline and installed funding base; no claim of transferable funds or exact current membership counts."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "F10. University of Sydney Library (Current webpage). Read and publish agreements"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Material inspected: Author eligibility, covered journals and treatment of direct APC invoices. Author-publication support is not evidence of financing for a new journal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "R01. HM Treasury (2025). Publishing business cases: guidance for departments"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Five-case structure used as an analytical aid. UK public-spending rules are not applied to this capstone."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "R02. Project Management Institute (Current webpage). Ways to earn PDUs"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "Official reading and informal-learning categories inspected; no PRAPJ accreditation or individual credit award is inferred."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E1. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). Publishing with us"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E2. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). Publish in a journal"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E3. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). Peer review resource hub"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E4. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). Peer review pillars and principles"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E5. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). Understanding different peer review processes"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E7. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). How to manage bias in peer review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E8. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). How to begin your review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E9. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). Publication ethics"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "E11. Emerald Publishing (Current webpage). Author policies and guidelines"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "J1. Elsevier / International Journal of Project Management (Current webpage). IJPM homepage and live journal navigation"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "J2. Elsevier / International Journal of Project Management (Current webpage). IJPM Guide for Authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "J3. Elsevier / International Journal of Project Management (Current webpage). IJPM Research Summary Series"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "J7. University of Malaya (Current webpage). University of Malaya Journal of Project Management Practice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "P2. Creative Commons (Current webpage). Creative Commons Attribution 4 point 0 licence deed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference", "text": "P3. World Wide Web Consortium (Current webpage). W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 2 point 2"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Reference note", "text": "See the reading-coverage chapter. Used as a benchmark, not a PRAPJ approval or performance claim."}]}], "words": 20542}, {"id": "02", "title": "Boards & Governance", "filename": "02_PRAPJ_Board_TOR_and_Candidate_Shortlists.docx", "sha256": "fb4e7aa0b41c351a5aead1411c4cb4257ffed7c94d0bf3b130b3c68883ecec3c", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "PRAPJ Board Terms of Reference and Candidate Shortlists"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "DOMINUS | Version 1.4 | 10 October 2026 | Proposed for sponsor and publisher adoption"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This document defines separate Advisory and Editorial Boards, protects editorial decisions and provides prospective invitees for discussion. No person listed has been appointed by this package. The journal owner and legal publishing entity must be confirmed before appointments are issued. References to a publisher below describe the future authorised entity."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Formal publication of PRAPJ is the intended outcome. No actual capstone budget has been approved or allocated, and no journal funding is committed. Financial estimates remain hypothetical planning assumptions; appointments and service commitments require authorised resources."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Advisory Board Terms of Reference"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Purpose. Advise on the journal's mission, practical relevance, audience, partnerships and sustainability. The board helps the publisher evaluate the programme while preserving the Editor-in-Chief's authority over individual manuscripts."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Responsibilities. Review the annual strategy, funding assumptions, audience evidence, risk register and continuity arrangements. Prioritise experienced and mid-career professionals while considering a later student pathway. Test contributor support and optional media against capacity and reader usefulness. Examine aggregate service and integrity indicators without directing manuscript decisions or accessing confidential reviewer identities."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Composition. Propose six voting members, including a chair, at least two experienced practitioners, at least two research representatives and a member with publishing or scholarly-communications experience. A professional-body representative may fill one of these seats. Seek varied sectors, regions and career perspectives; no institution should hold a voting majority. The Editor-in-Chief and Managing Editor attend as non-voting participants when appropriate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Appointment. The publisher appoints members after sponsor-supported nomination, conflict assessment and written acceptance. Use two-year terms, renewable once, with staggered expiry after the pilot. Pilot appointments receive a review at 12 months. Members act in a personal advisory capacity unless institutional representation has been explicitly authorised."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Meetings and decisions. Meet quarterly, with extraordinary meetings for material funding or continuity risks. Quorum is more than half of current voting members, including the chair or agreed deputy, and at least one practitioner and one research representative. Seek consensus; otherwise record a simple majority of eligible votes. A tie goes to the publisher as an unresolved recommendation, not a chair's editorial veto."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Conduct and conflicts. Maintain confidentiality and disclose interests on appointment and at each relevant agenda item. A conflicted member leaves the discussion and vote; recalculate quorum excluding that member. If quorum fails, defer or seek additional independent advice. Members cannot use confidential information to promote their own work or business."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Accountability. The secretary records attendance, recommendations, dissent, recusals, owners and due dates. Send minutes to members for correction within ten working days. The chair submits an annual advice report covering strategy, risks, participation and funding continuity. The publisher records its response to material recommendations."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Review and exit. Review the TOR at month 6 and annually. Members may resign with 30 days' notice where feasible. The publisher may remove a member for sustained non-participation, undeclared conflict or serious misconduct after written reasons and an opportunity to respond. Disagreement with an individual editorial decision is not grounds for removal. Expenses or remuneration require a separate agreement; none is offered here."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "Editorial Board Terms of Reference", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Purpose and authority"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Maintain the quality, integrity and usefulness of PRAPJ's content. The Editor-in-Chief has final responsibility for manuscript decisions, exercised personally or through an independent delegated editor. A board meeting does not vote on ordinary manuscript acceptance. The publisher supports lawful operations and integrity procedures without selling or directing editorial outcomes."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Composition and appointments"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Propose an Editor-in-Chief, a deputy, a Research Editor, a Practice Editor, a Translation Editor and additional methods or subject editors as workload requires. One person may cover two compatible roles during the pilot if time and conflicts permit; independent backup is mandatory. The Managing Editor coordinates records and production. Recruit a separate external-reviewer pool; board membership does not establish independent external review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The publisher appoints the Editor-in-Chief through documented assessment of editorial judgement, research quality, practitioner understanding, independence and time commitment. The Editor-in-Chief recommends other editors to the publisher. Use two-year renewable terms, with a 12-month pilot performance review. Confirm responsibilities and workload individually; acceptance of an interview invitation does not constitute an appointment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Responsibilities"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Editor-in-Chief maintains policy, appoints manuscript handlers, resolves conflicting recommendations, monitors quality and oversees corrections. The deputy covers absences and independently handles conflicts involving the Editor-in-Chief. Research and Practice Editors assess fit, commission appropriate reviews and recommend or make decisions within a written delegation. The Translation Editor checks fidelity, decision relevance and limitations in briefs. Methods advice is commissioned when needed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Managing Editor checks completeness, access permissions, deadlines, versions, production files and metadata. This role may return incomplete files but may not convert an administrative check into a scholarly acceptance. Guest editors work under the same standards, with the Editor-in-Chief retaining oversight of a collection."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Practice Editor oversees any interview-supported contribution route, including evidence traceability and contributor approval. The Translation Editor checks optional media against its written source and material limitations. A person who substantially helped prepare an item must not supply its independent review or control its acceptance."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Review and independence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research Articles and Evidence Reviews need at least two independent external reports. Practitioner Cases normally receive two external reports covering practice and evidence quality. Briefs receive editorial translation review. Challenges and Evidence Notes receive editorial and domain assessment, escalating to external review where the claim requires. Public labels must state the route actually used."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Editors declare relationships, employment, funding, collaboration and competitive interests that may affect impartiality. An implicated editor loses access to confidential handling records for that manuscript, apart from information available to an ordinary author. The independent handler and rationale are recorded. If no suitable internal handler exists, the publisher arranges an external editor without choosing the outcome."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Meetings and performance"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Hold monthly operational reviews during the pilot. Policy recommendations need a quorum of more than half the appointed voting editors, including the Editor-in-Chief or deputy and both research and practice expertise. Seek consensus, then majority; unresolved policy issues go to the publisher. Publish policy changes with an effective date and treatment of manuscripts already in process."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Evaluate editors on reasoned decisions, constructive handling, timeliness, confidentiality and contribution to capacity. Do not reward acceptance quotas or citation inflation. Review membership after repeated failure to carry out agreed duties. Give reasons, an opportunity to respond and a succession plan before removal. Annual reporting should disclose aggregate workflow results, material policy changes and corrective actions without exposing confidential cases."}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "Decision rights and conflict handling", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The publisher holds institutional and contractual responsibilities; the sponsor authorises project direction and resources within its authority. Neither role determines the scholarly outcome of an individual manuscript. The final institutional appointment and financial delegations remain to be confirmed."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Decision", "Accountable decision maker", "Advice and record"], ["Pilot funding and continuation", "Authorised publisher or sponsor within delegated authority", "Advisory Board recommendation and cost register"], ["Editor-in-Chief appointment", "Publisher", "Documented selection, conflicts and written acceptance"], ["Other editor appointments", "Publisher", "Editor-in-Chief recommendation and capacity assessment"], ["Individual manuscript outcome", "Editor-in-Chief or independent delegated editor", "Reports, reasons, conflicts and decision letter"], ["Journal editorial policy", "Editor-in-Chief for editorial standards; publisher for institutional adoption", "Board consultation and version register"], ["Commercial sponsorship", "Publisher", "Advisory input; no manuscript decision rights granted"], ["Appeal outcome", "Independent appeal editor or panel", "Evidence, process assessment and written reasons"], ["Correction or retraction", "Independent responsible editor with publisher support", "Assessment, author response and linked public notice"], ["Confidentiality incident", "Host incident owner", "Editor notification, containment and institutional procedure"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Operational conflict test"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An Advisory Board member submits a case about a sponsor-funded project. The office records the relationships on the private title page and in the conflict log. The Editor-in-Chief assigns an independent handler and excludes implicated advisers and sponsors from the review record. Reviewers are checked for institutional, commercial and collaboration links. The public article discloses relevant funding and author roles if accepted; funding does not influence the decision."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "If the Editor-in-Chief is an author, the deputy or an external editor controls the process and communicates the decision. If an appeal challenges that handler, a different independent person considers it. Record who appointed each handler, the reason, access restrictions and the final decision. An external reviewer cannot be counted as independent merely because their name is unknown to the author."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Appeals complaints and integrity"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed service standard: an author may appeal within 30 calendar days of the decision, stating a factual or procedural ground and supporting evidence. Acknowledge within five working days and aim for an independent response within 30 calendar days; update the author every 20 working days if delayed. Consider one appeal per decision unless material new evidence justifies reopening. Permit procedural appeals after desk decisions as well as external review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Separate an appeal about a decision from a complaint about conduct or integrity. An independent publisher-designated contact handles complaints involving the Editor-in-Chief. Preserve records, assess evidence, invite responses and obtain specialist advice when needed. Correct the record visibly where conclusions or published information materially change. Protect privacy in identity-related corrections. Contacts, record-retention periods and institutional escalation must be adopted before launch."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Reference basis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Original PRAPJ proposals informed by L3, L5, E4, E7 and J2 in the Business Case source register. Timing and composition require adoption; no publisher affiliation is implied."}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "Prospective Advisory Board invitees", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This is a prospective shortlist for sponsor review. All people listed are unappointed; availability, willingness, conflicts and permission to publish a profile must be checked. Professional fit is a proposed assessment from the cited evidence, not an organisational endorsement. Keep this shortlist off the public editorial-board page."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Paul Rodman"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed contribution: practitioner adviser on infrastructure decisions, case evidence and audience usefulness. Paul Rodman's interview was conducted on 31 August 2026. The supplied summary identifies him as Principal at Flagstaff Consulting Group, consistent with the profile inspected on 25 September. Reconfirm his preferred affiliation before invitation. [L2, C1]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Priority: initial sponsor discussion because an interview relationship already exists. Confirmation needed: interest in an advisory role, personal versus organisational capacity, available time, employer permission where relevant, and conflicts involving cases. The interview does not record acceptance of a board seat or a commitment of organisational funding."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "James Bawtree"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed contribution: practitioner adviser on professional usefulness, supported contributions and media testing. His 8 October 2026 interview emphasises project objectives, evidence triangulation, context, writing support and observable user value. This supports a role discussion; it does not establish board interest or organisational endorsement. [L8]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Confirmation needed: preferred title and affiliation, willingness, available time, conflicts, personal or organisational capacity, and whether an advisory role or bounded content review best suits his contribution. No appointment or financial commitment was recorded."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Professor Jennifer Whyte"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed contribution: research-practice strategy and major-project leadership. A University of Sydney short-course profile describes her professorship in the School of Project Management and work on systems integration, digital information and major projects. The preliminary charter also presents her as an illustrative academic contact. [L5, C2]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Priority: strategic academic candidate, subject to sponsor access. Confirmation needed: current role and responsibilities, time, institutional capacity, existing editorial relationships and willingness. Do not imply that a personal appointment commits the University to hosting or underwriting PRAPJ."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Professor Ofer Zwikael"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed contribution: benefits, sponsorship and business-case quality. ANU's official profile identifies project-management scholarship, benefits research and experience working with industry. This could help the board judge whether the publication improves decision relevance. [C3]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Priority: external perspective after the host and scope are clearer. Confirmation needed: availability, editorial commitments, conflicts and appointment terms. Existing journal relationships may require explicit management; they do not automatically exclude consideration."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Seats still to identify"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed six-member board still needs publishing or library expertise and balanced research, practice and professional-community coverage. The shortlist is a pool for selection, not a list of assigned seats. Ask the host and sponsor to resolve gaps before appointments."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Source links and verification"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Earlier public-profile checks date to 25 September 2026; W31 is a historical biography accessed on 10 October. Reconfirm current roles before outreach through official institutional channels, without reproducing personal telephone numbers in the handover package."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "C1. Flagstaff Consulting Group profile of Paul Rodman"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "C2. University of Sydney facilitator profile of Jennifer Whyte"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "C3. ANU profile of Ofer Zwikael"}]}, {"id": "s4", "title": "Prospective Editorial Board invitees", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "No Editor-in-Chief is nominated automatically. That appointment needs a separate assessment of editorial judgement, independence, capacity and willingness. The following candidates could be considered for specified functions; the roles are proposals only."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Dr Michael Hopmere"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed contribution: Practice or Translation Editor, or an external-reviewer role if an editorial appointment is unsuitable. The user confirms that the interview with Michael Hopmere took place on 24 September 2026. The supplied minutes record interest in reviewing, writing and possible editorial participation, with attention to practical cases and timely decisions. [L1, L9]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Priority: initial sponsor discussion. Confirm preferred title, current affiliation, the scope of the expressed interest and time commitment. The user has confirmed the identity; an editorial appointment still requires written acceptance. If appointed as editor, do not count his report as an independent external review of an item he handles."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Louis Taborda"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Prospective contact for an editorial-role discussion. Following the 3 September 2026 interview, the project lead reports that Louis confirmed support for establishing PRAPJ. The expanded third-person support account supplements the earlier general-support record. It combines that reported position with background analysis; it is not a verbatim transcript or independently signed endorsement. [L9, L10, L12]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Sydney's 18 March 2024 account documents teaching, IT consultancy and experiential learning involving real projects and industry. This background supports exploring an educational or practice contribution; current role and editorial fit still require confirmation. [W31]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before shortlisting for a defined appointment, confirm current title and affiliation, editorial judgement, independence, workload and willingness. The support account does not establish agreement to particular journal policies or formats, a board appointment, a submission or a funding or hosting commitment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Professor Shankar Sankaran"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed contribution: research or methods editor for organisational project management and practice-oriented inquiry. UTS's public Robotics Institute people page lists him with project-management and sociotechnical expertise. Its institutional account of a project-governance initiative describes work with practitioners. [C4]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Priority: methods and governance candidate. Confirm current title, editorial obligations, review capacity and conflicts. His experience may suit advisory work instead; select one clearly defined primary role to avoid ambiguous accountability."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Associate Professor Julien Pollack"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed contribution: research or practice editor for organisational change and complex projects. The University of Sydney's John Grill Institute profile describes practical delivery experience followed by research on project-management practice, systems thinking and change. The charter includes him among illustrative academic contacts. [L5, C5]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Priority: candidate with a documented practice-research connection. The profile is an institutional source but is not a current appointment confirmation; verify preferred title, affiliation, availability and existing commitments before invitation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Selection and invitation sequence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "First screen demonstrated subject expertise and integrity; then test independent judgement, contribution to practitioner relevance, capacity and coverage of methods, sectors and regions. Do not infer demographic characteristics from names or photographs. The current shortlist is Australia-focused and incomplete for a mature international board."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The sponsor reviews the shortlist; the authorised publisher confirms roles; approved invitations specify mandate, term, expected workload, confidentiality, conflicts and whether the role is unpaid. Obtain written acceptance and profile approval before announcement. Record status as shortlisted, approved to approach, invited, declined, accepted or appointed, with dates and evidence. No outreach was sent during preparation of this package."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Source links and appointment record"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "C4. UTS Robotics Institute people page"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "C4a. UTS project governance initiative"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "C5. John Grill Institute profile of Julien Pollack"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use template T11 for an invitation and acceptance record. A profile link supports professional relevance only; it never records consent to serve."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Interview and profile evidence update"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "L8. James Bawtree interview summary, 8 October 2026; check quotations against the recording and obtain publication approval. L9. Project-lead confirmation on 8 October of the four interview dates, Michael Hopmere's identity and project status. L10. Original project-lead account of Louis's general support, retained for provenance and supplemented by L12."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "L12. Expanded third-person Louis support account and project-lead confirmation supplied on 10 October 2026. Prepared from reported confirmation of support and documented background; the confirmation date and method were not supplied. No original transcript or independent signed record was supplied, and the analytical expansion does not establish that every sentence was spoken or separately confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "W31. University of Sydney, 18 March 2024. Community of expert educators to transform student experience. Accessed 10 October 2026. Historical professional background, not a current appointment confirmation or institutional endorsement of PRAPJ."}]}], "words": 2620}, {"id": "03", "title": "Guide for Authors", "filename": "03_PRAPJ_Guide_for_Authors.docx", "sha256": "92fa61857e6c69ace13aa0da4418a29f2237fa375ace0229ce4c240bce3ef650", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "PRAPJ Guide for Authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Version 1.3 | 8 October 2026 | Proposed for editorial adoption"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This guide sets out a complete proposed author workflow for the Project Research and Practice Journal (PRAPJ)."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ is in development. The requirements below are drafted for editorial adoption and are not an announcement that submissions are open. The publisher, editorial appointments, submission channel, opening date, access arrangements and publishing agreement must be confirmed before this guide becomes operative. Appendix B records the decisions still required."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "Before you begin", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ is intended for formal publication as a journal connecting credible project research with professional decisions and documented practice experience. The initial reader focus is experienced and mid-career project professionals. Researchers, educators, students and emerging professionals also benefit, with selected introductory explanations envisaged as a later derivative pathway. Choose a format that matches your evidence and claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A strong submission identifies its project context, explains how its evidence was obtained, distinguishes observations from interpretation and states the limits of application. Clear professional relevance does not remove the need for sound evidence. Equally, a well-documented Practitioner Case need not claim the same type of theoretical novelty as a Research Article."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Prepare in five steps"}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Check the aims, scope and format requirements in Sections 1–3."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Review the editorial, integrity and disclosure requirements in Sections 4–7."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Prepare your manuscript, separate title page and supporting files using Sections 8–11."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Complete the checklist in Section 12 and use only the official submission channel once announced."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Use Section 13 for revision, publication, sharing and updates to the published record."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Section", "What you will find"], ["1–3. Contribution fit", "Scope, article types, lengths and evidence requirements"], ["4. Review", "Assessment routes, decisions, independence and appeals"], ["5–7. Integrity", "Authorship, interests, ethics, confidentiality and AI"], ["8–11. Preparation", "Files, summaries, structure, artwork, data and references"], ["12–13. Submission and publication", "Checklist, revisions, agreements, proofs and sharing"], ["Appendix A", "Declaration prompts, cover-letter prompts and brief outline"], ["Appendix B", "Editorial adoption register and reference basis"]]}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "1 Aims scope and contribution formats", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ welcomes work on projects, programs, portfolios, project-based organisations and the wider settings in which project activity takes place. Topics include governance, strategy, sponsorship, benefits, leadership, teams, stakeholders, planning, control, uncertainty, procurement, capability, digital change, sustainability and project education."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Contributions may concern any relevant sector or region. Explain what is distinctive about the setting and which findings may have relevance elsewhere. Purely technical optimisation, consultancy promotion or organisational description requires a substantive project-management contribution to fall within scope."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Article types and proposed lengths"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Contribution", "Main-text length", "Assessment route"], ["Research Article", "6,000–9,000 words", "At least two independent external reviews; double-anonymous"], ["Evidence Review: research-track subtype", "5,000–8,000 words", "Research Article route, with review-method expertise"], ["Practitioner Case", "3,000–5,000 words", "Normally two external reviewers with combined practice and research expertise"], ["Research-to-Practice Brief", "1,200–2,000 words", "Editorial translation review"], ["Practice Challenge or Evidence Note", "1,500–2,500 words", "Editorial screening and domain assessment"], ["Invited Editorial or Debate", "1,500–3,000 words", "Editorial assessment and additional review where needed"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The four core tracks are research, practitioner cases, briefs, and challenges or evidence notes. Evidence Reviews sit within the research track. Invited Editorials and Debates are supplementary formats. A separate Teaching Case track is deferred during the proposed pilot; research about project education remains within scope."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Counting words"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Count the main text, footnotes, table text, captions and appendices intended to appear with the article. Exclude the title page, abstract or summary, keywords, decision-relevance statement, highlights, declarations, references and separately supplied supplementary files. Figures do not receive an automatic word allowance. Essential methods, evidence and limitations must remain in the main article."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Report the count and article type on the title page. Explain any requested exception in the cover letter. A longer submission should demonstrate why the additional material is necessary; acceptance of an exception is an editorial decision."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Required summary elements"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research Articles and Evidence Reviews require a 200–300-word abstract. Other substantive formats require a 150–200-word summary. All four core tracks require a separate 100–150-word decision-relevance statement and 4–7 keywords. For an Invited Editorial or Debate, the commissioning editor will confirm the summary elements needed."}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "2 Requirements for research and practitioner cases", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Research Articles"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "State the research question, its importance and the contribution to project knowledge. Situate the study in relevant literature and explain the conceptual or theoretical reasoning appropriate to the design. Qualitative, quantitative, mixed-method, conceptual and methodological contributions are eligible when their approach supports the contribution claimed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For empirical research, report the setting, unit of analysis, recruitment or sampling, data sources, timing, measures and analytical procedures. Explain how ethical and confidentiality issues were managed. Present findings with evidence sufficient to evaluate the interpretation. Discuss uncertainty, rival explanations, methodological limitations and implications for both scholarship and project decisions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Conceptual work should define its constructs, explain its reasoning and identify how the argument could inform empirical inquiry or professional judgement. A methodological article should demonstrate the method’s value, assumptions and limitations in a relevant project setting."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Evidence Reviews"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Identify the review question and justify the design, whether systematic, scoping, integrative, realist or another defensible approach. Report information sources, search dates, search strategy, eligibility criteria, selection procedures, extraction, appraisal and synthesis as appropriate to that design. Explain the treatment of grey literature, multiple reports from the same study and potentially conflicting findings."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Provide reproducible search details and a selection record where applicable. Identify limitations of coverage and evidence quality. A review must offer an informative synthesis, not simply count publications or reproduce abstracts. Claims of effectiveness or recommended action must reflect the strength and relevance of the underlying studies."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Practitioner Cases"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Describe a real project or programme challenge and provide a credible account of decisions, actions and outcomes. Suggested structure: context; challenge and objectives; evidence sources; options and decision rationale; intervention; outcomes; critical reflection; transfer conditions; practical implications."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Report the project objective, stage, relevant scale, organisational setting, stakeholder roles and observation period. Explain the authors' involvement and evidence access. Where available, compare records, schedules, measures and participant accounts; explain discrepancies and missing evidence. Distinguish contemporaneous records from recollection and give baselines, comparison periods and limitations for performance claims."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A case may report failure, mixed results or an intervention still in progress. State whether outcomes are observed, estimated or expected. Consider alternative explanations and avoid attributing all change to one intervention without supporting evidence. A purely hypothetical scenario must not be presented as an observed case."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Transferability depends on comparable objectives and material conditions, not identical industry labels alone. Explain what must be similar, what may differ and what a reader should verify before applying the lesson. Generalisation or removal of names does not replace required consent, organisational permission or rights clearance."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Interview supported contributions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A supported route may be offered when editorial capacity is approved. The contributor and editor first agree the topic, evidence access, confidentiality, recording consent and intended outputs. An exploratory conversation may help prepare an outline; it is not a completed submission or a promised service. Use T13 to record the process."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The contributor checks the resulting account, evidence, quotations and proposed generalisations and approves the submitted version. Credit writing and analytical assistance accurately under the authorship criteria. Record unresolved factual disagreements; never manufacture evidence to complete an account. An independent editor handles assessment, with the ordinary contribution standards and review route unchanged."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Obtain permission for organisational material and address participant confidentiality. Explain what a reader would need to reproduce or adapt the approach, where it may not work and which conclusions remain provisional. Commercial affiliation is permissible when disclosed; promotional claims are not evidence."}]}, {"id": "s4", "title": "3 Briefs practice questions and translation quality", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Research to Practice Briefs"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A brief interprets identifiable research for a specific professional decision and project objective. Explain the problem, source evidence, findings, possible actions and conditions of use. Cite and link each source, identify its publication and review status, and distinguish a single study from a synthesis. Use concrete problem-led explanations without implying that one setting establishes a universal solution."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Separate what the research found from your interpretation and from suggested action. Do not turn an association into a causal claim or omit limitations that would change a professional decision. Explain unfamiliar methods in plain language while retaining material uncertainty."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Under the proposed publication model, authors of accepted Research Articles, including Evidence Reviews, prepare a linked brief of 1,200–2,000 words with editorial guidance. The translation editor assesses it separately. The research article may proceed to publication while the brief completes its checks; the items will be linked when both are available. A brief based on externally published work is eligible if the source can be assessed and reuse rights are respected."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A Practice Challenge identifies a consequential problem that existing evidence does not adequately resolve. Describe its context, the decisions affected, what is already known, what remains uncertain and the research questions or evidence needed. An unresolved question should not be presented as a validated solution."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An Evidence Note reports a focused observation, preliminary finding or concise body of evidence. Describe provenance, methods and limitations in proportion to the claim. Label preliminary or exploratory work clearly. The editor may require a Research Article or Practitioner Case route when the claim calls for fuller methods or external review. This format does not provide a shortcut around those requirements."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Invited Editorials and Debates"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Present a clear argument, engage fairly with relevant evidence and distinguish interpretation from established findings. Disclose relevant interests. Invitations define a topic or format and do not guarantee publication."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Translation quality criteria"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Criterion", "Question the submission should answer"], ["Traceability", "Can readers identify the evidence behind each material claim?"], ["Decision relevance", "Which professional decision could this evidence inform?"], ["Boundaries", "In what settings or circumstances may it apply?"], ["Uncertainty", "What is unknown, contested or weakly supported?"], ["Actionability", "Are suggested actions feasible and supported by the evidence?"], ["Accessibility", "Can the intended professional audience understand the argument?"], ["Commercial neutrality", "Are claims independent of product promotion and declared interests?"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "These criteria support editorial judgement; they do not imply that practical recommendations are guaranteed to succeed."}]}, {"id": "s5", "title": "4 Editorial assessment and peer review", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Initial assessment"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The editorial office checks completeness, format and disclosures. An editor assesses fit, contribution, evidence, integrity and readiness for review. A submission may be returned for missing information, offered a more suitable format, sent for review or declined without external review. Invitations and favourable preliminary enquiries do not constitute acceptance."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Review routes and anonymity"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research Articles and Evidence Reviews receive at least two independent external reviews under a proposed double-anonymous process. Authors and reviewers are not routinely told one another’s identities. Specialist assessment may be sought when necessary."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Practitioner Cases normally receive two external reviews whose combined expertise covers relevant practice and research evidence. Double-anonymous handling is the proposed default. Where the organisation or public project makes author identity readily inferable, the editor will discuss feasible anonymisation and any alternative handling with the authors before review. Anonymity cannot be guaranteed for distinctive cases or publicly available preprints."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Briefs receive editorial translation review. Practice Challenges and Evidence Notes receive editorial screening and domain assessment, with additional external review when the claim requires it. Editorials and Debates receive editorial assessment and any necessary specialist review. Published items should identify the process used; editorial review must not be described as independent research peer review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Independence and confidentiality"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Editors and reviewers must disclose relevant conflicts and decline or be recused where impartial assessment is compromised. Manuscripts from an editor, adviser, sponsor or close collaborator must be handled by an independent editor. Sponsorship and institutional affiliation have no role in acceptance decisions. Unpublished submissions and review correspondence must remain confidential."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Guest editors operate within the same standards. The Editor-in-Chief retains oversight of collections and appoints an independent handler for guest-editor submissions or conflicts. Authors may suggest qualified reviewers or explain exclusions; editors decide appointments and verify independence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decisions and revisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Decisions may include rejection, revision or acceptance. An editor weighs the reviews and the submission as a whole; a favourable reviewer report alone does not establish acceptance. For revision, provide a response to every substantive comment and identify changes by section and page or line. Explain respectfully where a requested change was not made. Revised work may return to reviewers."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Appeals and complaints"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An appeal should identify a specific factual error, procedural concern or material misunderstanding and provide supporting evidence. The proposed deadline is 30 calendar days from the decision, including a desk decision. A handler independent of the disputed decision should acknowledge the appeal within five working days and aim to resolve it within 30 calendar days, with progress updates every 20 working days while unresolved. These are proposed service targets, not guaranteed turnaround. One appeal is normally permitted unless material new evidence emerges. Complaints about conduct or integrity follow a separate route outside the implicated editor. Confirm official contacts before launch."}]}, {"id": "s6", "title": "5 Originality authorship and interests", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Submission declaration"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Submission confirms that all authors approve the manuscript and its submission, that the work is not simultaneously under consideration elsewhere, and that prior dissemination and related manuscripts have been disclosed. Authors must identify reused text, figures, data and overlapping analyses, cite the source and explain the new contribution. Fabrication, falsification, plagiarism and concealment of material overlap are unacceptable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A thesis, preprint, abstract, conference presentation or earlier proceedings paper should be disclosed. Eligibility depends on overlap, rights and the added contribution; there is no automatic percentage rule. Supply an earlier version when requested. A secondary publication or translation requires prior editorial agreement and the necessary permissions and attribution."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Authorship and responsibility"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authorship requires a substantial intellectual contribution to the work; participation in drafting or substantive revision; approval of the submitted version; and acceptance of responsibility for the contribution and for helping resolve questions about the work. All who meet the criteria should be included, and honorary, purchased or undisclosed authorship is prohibited."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The corresponding author coordinates communication and confirms that the author list, affiliations, contributions and declarations are accurate. All authors should review the final version. Contributors who do not meet authorship criteria may be acknowledged with their permission. AI systems cannot be authors."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contributor roles"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Provide a CRediT contribution statement where applicable. Use relevant roles accurately: Conceptualization; Data curation; Formal analysis; Funding acquisition; Investigation; Methodology; Project administration; Resources; Software; Supervision; Validation; Visualization; Writing – original draft; Writing – review and editing. Multiple people may share a role. CRediT describes contributions and does not independently determine authorship."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Changes to the author list"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before acceptance, request a change through the editor, explain the reason and provide written agreement from all affected authors, including anyone added or removed. Changes after acceptance require exceptional justification and editorial review; a published change may need a correction. An unresolved authorship dispute may pause consideration while the relevant parties or institutions address it."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Funding and competing interests"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "List financial and in-kind support, grant identifiers where applicable and each funder’s role in design, access, analysis, interpretation, writing and publication decisions. State when the funder had no role."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Disclose financial, professional, personal or organisational relationships that could reasonably be perceived to influence the work: employment, consultancy, ownership, patents, paid advocacy, sponsor involvement or evaluation of an author’s own service. A conflict does not automatically preclude publication. If none exist, state that explicitly. Do not use a generic declaration that contradicts the author’s actual role in a case."}]}, {"id": "s7", "title": "6 Research ethics permissions and confidentiality", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Research involving people"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research using interviews, surveys, observation, experiments or identifiable records must comply with applicable ethical requirements. Report the approving body, decision or reference number and relevant consent arrangements. Where formal review was not required or an exemption was granted, explain the basis and identify the responsible institution or process. Do not invent an approval or claim that all project-management research is exempt."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Describe how participation was voluntary, how consent was obtained where required and how participants’ privacy was protected. Address dependencies such as a manager recruiting direct reports. If consent was waived, explain the authorised basis. Approval to conduct research does not automatically permit publishing identifiable quotations, images or records."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Organisational cases and access"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Explain whether the work is a research study, an authorised practice account or an analysis of public material. Clarify the author’s relationship to the organisation and how the evidence was accessed. Obtain permission to use confidential documents, client information, project records and third-party images. Record what the permission covers and retain supporting evidence for editorial inspection."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An organisation’s permission does not replace participant consent or other required ethical review. Conversely, participant consent does not override contractual or intellectual-property restrictions. The submission should explain any review by a sponsor or organisation and whether it constrained access, reporting or the decision to publish."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Anonymisation and restricted information"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use pseudonyms and remove unnecessary identifying details where appropriate. Explain material disguising or aggregation without revealing the protected information. Do not silently change observations or fabricate substitute data. If numerical transformation is essential to protect confidentiality, explain the method and its effect on interpretation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Consider combinations of details that may identify an organisation or person even when names are removed. Apply the same checks to appendices, supplementary files, metadata and repository records. Keep confidential consent forms and identity keys out of public supplements; provide confirmation or securely requested evidence instead."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Data and permissions limits"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A data availability statement must state restrictions honestly. Use de-identified data, controlled access, metadata or a justified non-sharing statement where open release is inappropriate. Do not promise access that your consent, contract or institution does not permit."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For copyrighted material, identify the rights holder and obtain permission covering the intended publication and licence unless an applicable licence or other valid basis already permits use. Supply appropriate credits. Notify the editor if a condition would conflict with the proposed open licence. Authors remain responsible for securing the necessary permissions before publication."}]}, {"id": "s8", "title": "7 AI use reporting integrity and inclusive language", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Use of generative AI by authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authors remain responsible for accuracy, originality, citations, interpretation and the protection of confidential information. AI-generated text must be checked against reliable sources. Do not cite a tool’s output as evidence for a factual or scientific claim without verifying the underlying source. Do not upload restricted research data, personal information or confidential third-party material to a service without authority and appropriate protections."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Disclose substantive generative-AI use in drafting, translation, analysis, coding or image creation. Identify the tool, version or access date where relevant, purpose, affected parts and human checks. Routine spelling, grammar and reference-formatting tools need not be declared unless they generated or materially transformed content. A declaration does not excuse unsupported claims or prohibited use."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "When AI is part of the research method, describe the model or service, relevant settings, prompts or procedures, dates, evaluation and validation sufficiently to assess the study. Explain reproducibility limits where systems change or are not publicly accessible. Put methodological details in Methods, with fuller protocols in a supplement if needed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Images and research evidence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Do not generate or alter images, recordings, quotations or data to represent observations that did not occur. Image adjustments must preserve information and be described when material. Retain original files. Combining panels, cropping or enhancing contrast must not mislead readers about the evidence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed policy excludes AI-generated or AI-altered evidentiary images unless that use is an explicitly described part of the research being evaluated. A clearly labelled conceptual illustration may be considered with disclosure, rights clearance and editorial agreement. It must not be presented as an observed project, participant or research result."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Confidential review material"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authors must not upload confidential review reports or editorial correspondence to generative-AI services. Editors and reviewers must not use generative AI to assess manuscripts, create or edit confidential review reports, or upload confidential review material. Seek permission before sharing review material with a colleague. Author preparation assistance is governed separately above."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Inclusive and precise reporting"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use respectful language and avoid assumptions about a reader’s or participant’s gender, ethnicity, disability or professional status. Describe relevant characteristics with justified terminology rather than stereotypes. Distinguish sex and gender where relevant, explain how categories were collected and discuss limitations of the analysis. Report demographic detail only where it serves the research and respects privacy."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Define specialised terms and abbreviations. Use one English spelling convention consistently. Use accurate institutional names and neutral descriptions of geographic areas. Maps should identify necessary study locations without implying an editorial position on disputed territorial or jurisdictional claims."}]}, {"id": "s9", "title": "8 Manuscript files and anonymous preparation", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "General format"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Prepare an editable .docx manuscript in a single column, using a readable 11- or 12-point font, double line spacing and margins of approximately 2.5 cm. Add page numbers and, where feasible, continuous line numbering. Use heading styles and editable text, equations and tables. A PDF may accompany the manuscript for reference but does not replace the editable file."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "LaTeX and other source formats should be accepted only if supported by the confirmed submission and production system. Until that system is announced, .docx is the proposed standard. Keep the structure simple; elaborate journal page design is unnecessary at submission."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Separate title page"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Include the following information in a file withheld from anonymous reviewers:"}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Article title, contribution type and main-text word count."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Each author’s preferred name and institutional or professional affiliation, including country or region; identify an independent affiliation accurately where relevant."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Corresponding author’s email and appropriate contact details; ORCID identifiers where available."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Acknowledgements, full funding details, competing interests and CRediT contributions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Relevant ethics and permission information that would identify authors or their institutions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Prior dissemination, related submissions and links needed for editorial assessment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Anonymous manuscript"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For formats using anonymous review, omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements and direct identifiers. Refer to your own published work in the third person and cite it normally. Do not remove essential evidence or literature merely because you authored it."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Retain the substantive ethics, funding and interest information needed to assess the study while replacing identifying particulars with a clear review placeholder where necessary. Supply the complete declaration privately on the title page. For example, identify that approval was obtained and the type of body, while temporarily withholding its name and reference number if these reveal the author. Restore all required details before publication."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Check document properties, comments, tracked-change authors, filenames, repository links and supplementary files for identifiers. Use an anonymous repository view where the repository supports one; otherwise explain the issue to the editor and provide access for editorial assessment. Disclose linked preprints privately and recognise that public versions can make identity inferable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Final and supplementary files"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Number files clearly and refer to each supplement in the manuscript. Keep identifying or confidential editorial documents separate from publishable material. At revision, provide a clean manuscript and a marked copy if requested, taking care that the marked copy does not reveal author identity during continued anonymous review."}]}, {"id": "s10", "title": "9 Titles summaries and article structure", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Title abstract and keywords"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use a concise, informative title that reflects the evidence and setting without promising more than the study establishes. Avoid unexplained abbreviations and promotional claims."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For Research Articles and Evidence Reviews, provide a 200–300-word abstract covering purpose, approach, principal findings, contribution and material limitations. Use an informative narrative or short labelled sections consistently. Avoid citations where possible and define essential abbreviations. For other substantive formats, provide a 150–200-word summary explaining the problem, evidence, central message and limits."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Supply 4–7 searchable keywords for every core-track submission. Choose terms that identify the project topic, setting or approach; do not rely solely on words already prominent in the title."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decision relevance statement"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Every core-track submission requires a separate 100–150-word statement for a professional reader. Identify the user, project objective, decision, contribution and conditions or limitations of use. Explain which contextual similarities matter and what the reader should check before applying the learning. A research gap alone is not a practical decision; avoid unsupported promises of savings, performance or universal applicability."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Highlights and graphical abstracts"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Highlights are optional in this proposal: provide 3–5 short bullets, each no more than 25 words, reporting the contribution and practical meaning. They should not repeat the abstract or introduce a new claim."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A graphical abstract is optional and should communicate the main relationship or finding legibly. Supply an accessible text description and an editable or high-quality source file. Use only material for which you have the necessary rights. No platform-specific dimensions are imposed before production requirements are confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Suggested structure"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Format", "Main sections to consider"], ["Research Article", "Introduction; literature and conceptual basis; methods; findings; discussion; practical implications; limitations; conclusion"], ["Evidence Review", "Review question; conceptual background; review methods; synthesis; evidence quality and gaps; implications; limitations; conclusion"], ["Practitioner Case", "Context; challenge; evidence sources; options and decisions; intervention; outcomes; reflection; transfer conditions"], ["Research-to-Practice Brief", "Decision context; evidence base; key findings; practical interpretation; possible actions; boundaries and uncertainty"], ["Challenge or Evidence Note", "Problem or question; setting; evidence and approach; focused findings or uncertainty; implications; next steps"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Adapt the structure to the design without omitting information needed to evaluate the work. Number major sections consistently. Define abbreviations on first use, keep footnotes limited and use appendices only for material that supports rather than substitutes for the central argument."}]}, {"id": "s11", "title": "10 Tables figures and supplementary material", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Tables and numerical reporting"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Supply tables as editable text, number them consecutively and cite each in the manuscript. Give each a descriptive title, clear column headings and notes defining abbreviations, units, sample sizes and missing values. Avoid duplicating the same evidence extensively in both tables and figures."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Distinguish observed, estimated, forecast and simulated values. State currencies, price dates, inflation assumptions and exchange-rate bases when they matter to project costs. Define time periods, denominators and measurement procedures. Use consistent units and appropriate precision. For statistical results, report effect sizes and uncertainty where applicable and explain material departures from the planned analysis."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Figures and artwork"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use clear labels that remain legible when reduced. Number figures in order and provide captions describing their content, sources and material limitations. Supply original editable charts or vector PDF/SVG where possible, together with a review-friendly version embedded in the manuscript."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For raster artwork, the proposed preparation target is at least 300 dpi at intended display size for photographs and 600 dpi for mixed text and image artwork; use vector output or approximately 1,000 dpi for fine line drawings. Enlarging a low-resolution image does not restore missing detail. Final production may request an alternative format that preserves quality."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Explain relevant image processing and distinguish separate panels or composite images. Do not use a screenshot when the original table, chart or source file is available. Ensure that external material carries the required permission and credit."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Accessibility"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use adequate contrast and do not rely on colour alone to distinguish categories. Add meaningful alt text or a separate accessible description explaining the figure’s purpose and key information. Keep text selectable wherever possible. Include legends, labels and units that are understandable without returning repeatedly to the main text."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Supplementary files and video"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Provide a title, short description and file number for each supplement. Cite it in the article and ensure it can be understood independently. Suitable materials include search strategies, instruments, coding frameworks, additional analyses, protocols and reproducibility instructions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Optional video, audio or short explanations may supplement a written contribution when the platform and editorial capacity support them. They are not a fifth core track or a substitute for assessable written evidence. Identify the intended audience, related article, source version and exact assessment label. Provide accurate captions and a transcript for spoken video; supply a transcript for audio, with descriptions of material visual information where needed. Supported formats and file limits remain to be confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before release, obtain speaker and contributor approval for the final item and clear recording, publication and third-party rights. Check statements against the written source and retain material limitations, source links, date and correction links. Student-facing extracts must not lose qualifications through simplification. Use T14 for the release record. Supplementary material follows applicable ethics and review checks; keep private consent records out of public files. Substantive changes after acceptance require editorial assessment."}]}, {"id": "s12", "title": "11 Research data reproducibility and references", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Data and supporting materials"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Share the data, code, instruments and protocols needed to understand the findings when ethical, contractual and legal conditions permit. Prefer an appropriate trusted repository with a persistent identifier and a clear version. Deposit materials in time for review where feasible and provide access instructions. Cite datasets and software in the manuscript as well as in the availability statement."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Explain processing, variable definitions, dependencies, software versions and execution steps where relevant. Identify proprietary tools and access limits. If materials cannot be shared, specify the reason and what can be provided instead. A restriction is not a reason to omit the account of how evidence was produced."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Data availability statement"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Every substantive article must include a statement describing where its supporting materials can be accessed, or why access is limited. Distinguish open, controlled and unavailable materials. If no dataset was generated or analysed, state that when accurate. A claim of access on reasonable request should identify an actual decision process and must be consistent with consent and institutional authority."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Disclose preregistration or a published protocol where applicable, and explain material deviations. Label exploratory analyses accurately. Research transparency includes null or conflicting results that materially affect interpretation; selective reporting must not create a misleading account."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Citations and reference style"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use an accurate, consistent author–date style at initial submission. The proposed final style is author–date, with references alphabetised by author and then year. Use “et al.” for three or more authors in text, distinguish same-author same-year works with letters and include page numbers for quotations. Verify every source and ensure that every in-text citation matches a reference entry and vice versa."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Provide authors, year, title and the source information needed to locate the work. Include verified DOIs as persistent links where available; do not invent one. Identify preprints, datasets and software as such. Give an access date for changing web content. Cite original sources for substantive claims and avoid excessive self-citation or unrelated citations suggested solely to increase metrics."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Reference patterns"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Journal article: Author, A. A., & Author, B. B. (Year). Article title. Journal Title, volume(issue), page range or article number. DOI link."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Book: Author, A. A. (Year). Book title (edition, if relevant). Publisher."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dataset or software: Creator. (Year). Title (version) [Data set or Software]. Repository or publisher. Persistent identifier."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Web source: Organisation or Author. (Date). Page title. Website. URL. Accessed date where relevant."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "These are field patterns, not real sources. Preserve the verified metadata of the actual works cited."}]}, {"id": "s13", "title": "12 Submission checklist and cover letter", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Submission channel"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The submission opening date and official portal are to be announced. Once open, enter metadata that matches the manuscript and use the designated file categories. Do not send a manuscript or payment to an unverified address. Editorial enquiries and technical support details will be published with the portal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Required submission package"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["File or information", "What to provide"], ["Cover letter", "Contribution, fit, format, prior dissemination and material editorial issues"], ["Title page", "Authors, affiliations, contact, word count and complete identifying declarations"], ["Main manuscript", "Editable .docx, anonymised where required, with summaries, references and review-appropriate declarations"], ["Figures and tables", "Legible embedded versions and source files where required"], ["Supplementary material", "Labelled files cited in the article and cleared for review and potential publication"], ["Data information", "Availability statement, repository links or restrictions and review access"], ["Permissions and disclosures", "Relevant confirmations or documents supplied privately when requested"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Final checks"}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "The contribution fits PRAPJ and uses the correct article type and proposed length."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "The abstract or summary, decision-relevance statement and keywords meet the format requirements."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "The question, methods or evidence sources, findings, limitations and practical implications are clear."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "All authors approved submission; affiliations, contributions, interests and funding are accurate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Ethics, consent, organisational permissions and third-party rights have been addressed where applicable."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Prior versions, related manuscripts and material AI use are disclosed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Anonymous files, metadata, repository access and supplementary files have been checked."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Citations, references, figures, tables, units and numerical claims have been verified."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Files are readable, editable where required and free of confidential material intended only for the editor."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Cover letter prompts"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Keep the letter concise, normally within one page. State the manuscript title and proposed format. Explain the problem, principal contribution and why PRAPJ’s research-practice audience would benefit. Identify the professional decision informed by the work and any format exception requested."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Confirm author approval and exclusive consideration only if true. Disclose preprints, conference versions, overlapping work, important restrictions and relevant editorial conflicts. For a collection, name the collection and any invitation. Reviewer suggestions or exclusions should explain expertise or a relevant conflict; authors must not nominate fabricated identities or people with undisclosed close relationships."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Suggested subject classifications are governance and strategy; leadership and people; planning and control; risk and complexity; procurement; benefits and value; digital change; sustainability; and learning and education. Select the closest available terms when the portal taxonomy is confirmed."}]}, {"id": "s14", "title": "13 Revision publication and the published record", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Revision and acceptance"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Return a clean revised manuscript, any requested marked copy and a point-by-point response. Use the deadline in the editorial letter or request an extension with a reason. For each comment, provide the response, location of the change and any supporting evidence. Explain disagreements substantively. Recheck author details, disclosures, references and data links after revision."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Only a formal editorial acceptance establishes acceptance. Production may still require complete source files, permissions and an agreed publishing licence. Do not make substantive post-acceptance changes without editorial agreement. Any author-list change follows section 5."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Access charges and rights"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The preferred model is diamond open access, with no submission fee, article publishing charge or reader paywall, subject to confirmed support and adoption. The final fee policy will be stated before submissions open. Authors would retain copyright and grant the publisher non-exclusive rights needed to publish, identify and preserve the version of record. A host-approved publishing agreement is required before release."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "CC BY 4.0 is the proposed publication licence, subject to publisher adoption and author agreement. It permits sharing and adaptation, including commercial use, with attribution, a licence link and identification of changes. Third-party material may carry separate terms, and privacy or other rights may still constrain reuse. No licence or publishing agreement is executed by this draft."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Proofs publication and sharing"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The corresponding author checks proofs for factual accuracy, author details, tables, figures, references and production errors and returns corrections within the period specified by production. Major new analysis or a changed conclusion requires editorial assessment. The journal has no verified processing history; this proposal does not promise a fixed review or publication time."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Under the proposed immediate open-access model, the journal would impose no additional embargo on sharing the licensed published article. Identify the version, retain credits and licence information, and link to the current publication record. Update preprints and repositories with the final citation when available. Promote findings accurately and retain material limitations. Linked briefs complete their own editorial checks and are connected to source articles when ready."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Withdrawal and changes after publication"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Request withdrawal before publication through the editorial office and explain the reason; do not assume silence confirms withdrawal. Alert the editor promptly to an error or integrity concern. Corrections, expressions of concern or retractions should follow an evidence-based assessment, allow affected parties to respond where appropriate, and be linked visibly to the article. Substantive published changes should not occur silently. Any exceptional removal must preserve an explanatory record to the extent appropriate. Complaints and appeals follow the independent handling described in section 4."}]}, {"id": "s15", "title": "Appendix A Author preparation prompts", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Declaration templates"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Adapt these prompts to the actual work. Delete alternatives that do not apply. Bracketed fields are instructions to authors and must be completed before submission. Use the anonymous-review approach in section 8 while providing full details privately to the editor."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Funding: This work was supported by [funder and grant identifier]. The funder’s role was [describe role]. The funder had no involvement in [specify stages, only if true]. If accurate: This work received no specific financial support. Disclose relevant in-kind support separately."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Competing interests: [Author] is [relationship] with [organisation], whose [service, project or interest] is discussed in this article. [Describe any related role in data access, analysis or reporting]. If accurate for all authors: The authors declare no competing interests relevant to this work."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Ethics and consent: The study was [approved or exempted] by [body], reference [identifier/date]. Participants [describe consent and privacy arrangements]. If formal review was not required, explain the basis and the responsible institutional or other applicable process. Do not use an exemption statement merely because approval was not sought."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Case permissions: Permission to use [records, quotations or images] for publication was obtained from [authorised party]. [Describe confidentiality protections and limits]. State the author’s role in the project or organisation and any review of the account by that organisation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Data available openly: The [data/code/materials] supporting this work are available in [repository], [persistent identifier], version [version], under [applicable terms]."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Data restricted: The [materials] cannot be released openly because [specific ethical, confidentiality or contractual reason]. [Describe a permitted access route or alternative materials, if available]. Do not promise access that cannot be provided."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "No new dataset: No new dataset was generated or analysed for this [conceptual article/commentary]. The sources supporting the argument are cited in the article. Use only when accurate."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "AI declaration: During preparation of this work, the authors used [tool, version/access date] for [purpose and affected content]. The authors [describe checks and revisions] and take responsibility for the submitted work. AI used as a research method is also described in [Methods section]."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Contributor statement: [Name]: [relevant CRediT roles]. [Name]: [roles]. All authors approved the submitted version and accept the authorship responsibilities described in this guide."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Acknowledgements: We thank [person or organisation] for [contribution]. Obtain permission from named individuals. Identify substantive language, analytical or writing assistance that does not qualify for authorship."}]}, {"id": "s16", "title": "Appendix A continued Brief and revision tools", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Research to Practice Brief outline"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use this outline within the proposed 1,200–2,000-word main-text range. Include the separate 150–200-word summary, 100–150-word decision-relevance statement and 4–7 keywords required for the format."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Component", "Questions to answer"], ["Title and decision context", "Who needs to decide what, and why is this question consequential?"], ["Source evidence", "Which study or studies inform the brief? What is their publication and review status?"], ["What was examined", "What settings, participants, methods and period underpin the evidence?"], ["Main findings", "What did the source establish, with what uncertainty or variation?"], ["Practical interpretation", "How could the findings inform judgement, and what is interpretation rather than a source finding?"], ["Possible actions", "Which actions or questions follow from the evidence? What resources or prerequisites are needed?"], ["Boundaries and limitations", "Where might the evidence not transfer? What remains uncertain or untested?"], ["Sources and declarations", "Are citations, permissions, interests and material assistance complete?"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Avoid giving a universal prescription from a single setting. A useful action may be to collect better evidence or test an assumption before committing to an intervention. Do not invent an implementation result to make the brief appear more practical."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Response to reviewers"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Repeat or accurately identify each editorial or reviewer comment. Separate the response from the revised manuscript text. A concise response record can use the following fields:"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Author entry"], ["Comment identifier", "Editor / Reviewer number and comment number"], ["Issue raised", "The question, concern or requested change"], ["Response", "What was changed and why; or a reasoned explanation if no change was made"], ["Manuscript location", "Section, page and line in the revised version"], ["Supporting material", "Any new analysis, citation, table or supplementary file"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Address overlapping comments consistently and identify any consequential change to the abstract, decision-relevance statement or linked brief. New analyses should be described transparently rather than presented as if they had always been planned."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Author readiness check"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Read the manuscript once from the perspective of a researcher assessing evidence and once from the perspective of a professional deciding whether the findings apply. Both readers should be able to locate the central question, evidence, implications and limitations. Neither should need to infer what was observed, what was interpreted and what remains proposed."}]}, {"id": "s17", "title": "Appendix B Editorial adoption and source note", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This appendix is for the founding editorial team. Remove it from the public guide after recording the adopted decisions and replacing the pre-launch status text. Until then, the guide is a reviewable policy proposal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decisions required before the guide takes effect"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Decision area", "Action required"], ["Publication authority and contacts", "Confirm publisher, host, Editor-in-Chief, editorial office, support and independent complaints contacts"], ["Access and agreement", "Adopt the access and fee policy, author copyright, proposed CC BY 4.0 licence, third-party treatment and host-approved publishing agreement before release."], ["Contribution specifications", "Adopt the consolidated lengths, summaries, keywords, count rules and pilot taxonomy"], ["Review arrangements", "Confirm reviewer capacity, double-anonymous default, case-identifiability handling and accurate review labels"], ["Linked briefs", "Confirm author responsibility, translation-editor capacity and publication without waiting for the linked brief"], ["Integrity procedures", "Adopt authorship, ethics, data, AI, permissions, appeals and published-record policies; assign responsible handlers"], ["Submission and production", "Confirm portal, dates, file support, media limits, proof procedures, identifiers and preservation"], ["Policy maintenance", "Add approval and effective dates, version owner, review cycle and treatment of submissions already in process"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Policy reconciliation and source basis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This revision retains the four core tracks, lengths, summaries, word-count rules and assessment routes. It adds goal-led transferability, evidence comparison, a capacity-dependent supported contribution route and optional accessible media controls informed by James Bawtree's 8 October interview. These are proposals for adoption, not confirmed staffing or services. No actual capstone budget is approved or allocated, and no journal funding is committed; resource models are hypothetical."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Adopt the staffing boundary and consent/approval process for supported contributions and optional media before offering them. T13 and T14 provide records for these choices. Confirm whether a later directory application is compatible with the actual article classifications and assessment routes; the guide does not assert indexing eligibility."}]}, {"id": "s18", "title": "Editorial source and release note", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This version builds on the supplied PRAPJ draft and the interview-led business case. Emerald and IJPM were used as process and content benchmarks, not as policies automatically binding PRAPJ. Source pages were reviewed on 25 September 2026; the Business Case records full reading coverage and limitations. Approval and effective dates remain pending."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald publishing process"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald peer review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald publication ethics"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "IJPM Guide for Authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The author AI proposal differs from Emerald's prose-generation rules; reconcile policies before signing a publishing agreement. Reviewer confidentiality rules remain separate."}]}], "words": 6340}, {"id": "04", "title": "Review & Editorial Procedures", "filename": "04_PRAPJ_Reviewer_Guide_and_Editorial_Procedures.docx", "sha256": "568f20a5b6aab7fedad4458beb84edc68a2301a468420370c9db1ec359ee71da", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "PRAPJ Reviewer Guide and Editorial Procedures"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Version 1.3 | 8 October 2026 | Proposed operating guide | Submissions are not open"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This guide explains how editors and reviewers would assess PRAPJ submissions, document decisions and protect confidentiality. Read it alongside the Guide for Authors and Board Terms of Reference. The author guide controls contribution specifications; this document controls proposed handling steps. All service targets require adoption and capacity testing."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Before accepting an invitation"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Check expertise, time and independence. Declare relevant institutional, employment, financial, supervisory, collaboration or competitive relationships before accessing a manuscript. Decline when you cannot give an impartial assessment or meet an agreed deadline. Suggest alternatives only when you know their relevant expertise; editors verify identity and independence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Treat the manuscript, supplementary files and correspondence as confidential throughout and after the review. Do not contact authors, pass files to a colleague, use unpublished ideas, or run files through external tools without authorised arrangements. A co-reviewer needs prior editorial approval and acknowledgement in the private record. Do not upload confidential review material to generative-AI services or use generative AI to assess it."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Routes and reviewer assignments"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Contribution", "Assessment route", "Primary question"], ["Research Article or Evidence Review", "At least two independent external reviewers; double-anonymous default", "Does the method and evidence support a worthwhile contribution?"], ["Practitioner Case", "Normally two external reviewers whose combined expertise covers practice and research", "Is the account credible and are transfer conditions clear?"], ["Research-to-Practice Brief", "Editorial translation review", "Does the interpretation accurately reflect identifiable source evidence?"], ["Challenge or Evidence Note", "Editorial screening and domain assessment; escalate when required", "Is the question or limited claim appropriately evidenced and labelled?"], ["Invited Editorial or Debate", "Editorial assessment and specialist advice where needed", "Is the argument evidence-informed and fairly presented?"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An identifiable case or preprint may make anonymity incomplete. The editor documents feasible treatment and discusses a change of review model with the author before proceeding. Never promise that anonymisation eliminates all bias. Editorial assessment must not be labelled as independent research peer review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Structure of a useful report"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Summarise the contribution fairly; identify strengths; separate major concerns from minor improvements; connect each concern to a manuscript location and its effect on the claim. Suggest a feasible remedy where possible. Distinguish an essential correction from a preference. Use respectful, specific language and avoid requiring citations merely to promote yourself or the journal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use T07 for research and case reports and T08 for translation checks. Recommendations inform an editor's decision; they do not constitute acceptance. Report suspected integrity problems privately to the editor with evidence rather than accusing authors in public comments."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "Assessment criteria and decisions", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Research and evidence reviews"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Evaluate the question, contribution and relationship to relevant literature. Assess whether sampling, measurement, data sources and analysis suit the question. Check whether conclusions distinguish association from causation, acknowledge uncertainty and consider plausible alternatives. For evidence reviews, inspect search dates and sources, inclusion criteria, appraisal and synthesis in relation to the claimed design."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Check practical implications against the findings. A paper can be useful without claiming a universal solution. Identify missing evidence that changes the conclusion; do not ask for an entirely different study as a routine revision. Statistical or methodological concerns beyond your expertise should prompt specialist assessment."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Practitioner cases"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Assess the project objective, context, decision, alternatives, author involvement and evidence provenance. Compare available records, schedules, measures and participant accounts and inspect discrepancies. Separate recollection from contemporaneous evidence and intended from observed outcomes. Check baselines and periods. Mixed outcomes can be useful; theoretical novelty is not required for a credible case."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Ask which objectives and conditions must be comparable for another project to use the lesson, including across sectors. Check permissions, participant protections and interests. Removing names does not waive consent or organisational permission, and anonymisation must not invent replacement observations. Return unsupported promotional claims for evidence or decline them."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Translation review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Open the source research and identify its review status. Map important claims to specific findings. Separate source results, the brief author's interpretation and suggested action. Retain limitations, uncertainty and contextual boundaries. A linked brief may be published after its source article; it receives its own review label and version record."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Supported contributions and media"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For an interview-supported item, inspect T13 for agreed scope, evidence access, consent, credited assistance and contributor approval. The interviewer or substantive writing assistant must not provide the independent assessment of their own work. Identify limitations when records cannot corroborate recollection; apply the ordinary track requirements."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For optional media, inspect T14 and compare the final script, captions or transcript with the source. Verify speaker approval, reuse rights, context, limitations, audience level and the actual review label. A promotional or instructional video must not inherit a research peer-review label simply by linking to a reviewed article. Link corrections across the article and its derivatives."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decision categories"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Decision", "Use and required explanation"], ["Return for completion", "Missing files or essential declarations; specify what is absent and how to resubmit"], ["Decline without external review", "Scope, contribution, evidence readiness or integrity concern prevents progression; give a reason"], ["Major revision", "Substantial but potentially addressable changes; identify essential issues and reassessment route"], ["Minor revision", "Limited corrections; state who will verify them"], ["Accept", "Relevant quality and review requirements satisfied; complete rights and production checks before release"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Handling disagreement"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Editors read the reports and underlying manuscript rather than counting votes. Seek clarification or specialist advice where reports conflict on a material issue. Record the reasoning, including any departure from a recommendation. An apparent ethics problem triggers an evidence-based inquiry and author response, not an automatic misconduct finding."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed reviewer form ratings are Meets, Partly meets, Does not meet and Not applicable, with reasons. There is no automatic pass score. A serious validity or integrity defect cannot be offset by strong presentation ratings."}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "Editorial workflow and service targets", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Targets below are proposed internal operating standards, not published PRAPJ performance statistics or guarantees. Record calendar timestamps for reporting even where the service target uses working days."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Stage", "Owner and target", "Required record or control"], ["Receipt and completeness", "Office within 5 working days", "Manuscript ID, version, files, metadata and missing items"], ["Scope and evidence screening", "Assigned editor within 10 working days of complete receipt", "Route, fit, integrity checks, decision and reasons"], ["Reviewer invitation", "Editor; seek replies within 7 calendar days", "Expertise, independence and accept or decline log"], ["Review", "Reviewer; normally 28 calendar days after acceptance", "Agreed due date, report and any extension"], ["Follow-up", "Office at due date; editor after 7 overdue days", "Reminder, revised date or replacement decision"], ["Decision after reports", "Editor within 10 working days where feasible", "Considered reports and reasoned letter"], ["Author revision", "Proposed 30 days minor or 60 days major, varied by editor", "Clean and marked files plus point-by-point response"], ["Production and proof", "Office and author; proposed 5 working days for author proof", "Source completeness, rights, metadata and author signoff"], ["Release", "Responsible editor and production owner", "Final files, accurate label, licence, persistent link and archive"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Standard sequence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Receive → check files → assign independent editor → screen → commission appropriate assessment → decide → revise as needed → accept → clear rights → copyedit and typeset → obtain proof corrections → verify metadata and release. An administrative status of accepted is not a substitute for the signed publishing agreement or permissions check."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For a research manuscript, do not move from review to acceptance until the independent-report requirement is fulfilled. For a brief, store source links and the translation check. For a revision, preserve each version and the response to comments. For a withdrawn paper, confirm withdrawal in writing and retain the decision trail according to adopted retention rules."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Publication readiness"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Verify title, authors, affiliations, summary, decision-relevance statement, keywords, declarations, references, licence, third-party credits, accessible figures, article type and actual review label. Register an identifier only through an authorised service and check that it resolves. Link related research and briefs in both directions. A DOI does not establish indexing or quality recognition."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Training and calibration"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before launch, two editors independently screen the same sample case and research manuscript, then compare reasons. Ask novice reviewers to discuss a non-confidential training example with an editor. Record inconsistencies and revise guidance before processing real submissions. Monitor timeliness alongside report usefulness and fairness rather than setting pressure to accept a fixed number of papers."}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "Integrity continuity and reporting", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Appeals and complaints"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use the independent route in the TOR. Authors have a proposed 30 calendar days to identify a factual or procedural concern. Acknowledge within five working days and aim to respond within 30 calendar days, with updates every 20 working days if delayed. An implicated editor must not assess their own appeal. Publish official contacts before opening submissions; no invented address appears in this draft."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For suspected misconduct, preserve the record, identify an independent handler, assess the evidence and invite an author response. Seek institutional or specialist input when required. Correct misleading published information through a linked notice; keep a clear version history. An expression of concern or retraction needs a reasoned process. Do not silently replace substantive findings."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Records and access"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The host sets retention periods and a lawful privacy notice before collecting real submissions. Limit access to assigned roles; restrict title pages, permissions evidence and identity keys from anonymous reviewers. Log access changes, conflicts, decisions, file versions and release actions. Store confidential consent documents separately from material intended for publication."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Test a backup restore and a complete export of published files, metadata and policy versions before launch. If funding ends, stop taking new obligations, notify affected contributors through authorised channels, agree a completion or withdrawal route and preserve the published record. A named operational successor must receive credentials through the host's approved process, not through a public handover document."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Dashboard definitions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Report unique submissions by track; proportion returned incomplete; desk declines; manuscripts sent to review; invitation responses; completed reports; overdue assignments; revision cycles; accepted and published items; median and range of each elapsed stage. Show the period and denominator and avoid small-cell reporting that reveals confidential identities."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Separate invitation acceptance, eventual completion after acceptance and completion by the agreed date. Distinguish these from planned reviewer utilisation. Record staff time for supported interviews, drafting, contributor checks, media production and corrections so the service can be limited to available capacity."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Record outputs, views and downloads separately from evidence of use. For optional media, distinguish a view from meaningful consumption using a stated measure. Record voluntary usefulness ratings from one to five, free-text explanations, repeat use and recommendations with periods and denominators. Feedback is not proof of causal project improvement; obtain permission before publishing a testimonial."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Policy differences found in benchmarking"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald's reviewer-process page prohibits AI assistance in reviewing and also describes review-report confidentiality. Its broader ethics page permits declared copy-editing of review reports while still prohibiting manuscript uploads and AI evaluation. PRAPJ should not merge these into an apparently identical rule. The proposed pilot takes the simpler position of no generative-AI use with confidential review material; authors' disclosed, verified AI assistance is governed separately in the author guide. [E6, E9]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "References and document control"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "E4. Emerald peer review pillars and principles"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "E6. Emerald journal peer review process"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "E7. Emerald guidance on bias"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "E9. Emerald publication ethics"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Policy owner: proposed Editor-in-Chief. Approval and effective date: pending. Review after six pilot months and annually. T07–T10 and T12 support handling; T13 and T14 support interview-derived contributions and media. James Bawtree's supplied 8 October 2026 interview summary informed the new practical checks. All operating resources and financial scenarios remain uncommitted."}]}], "words": 1568}, {"id": "05", "title": "About & Publish", "filename": "05_PRAPJ_About_and_Publish.docx", "sha256": "057d314de55e6e7661e5b543f98369c9bfc9256cddeac832b929f0979d74407b", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "PRAPJ About and Publish"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Version 1.3 | 8 October 2026 | Proposed for editorial adoption"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Website copy for the team building the PRAPJ site. Editorial notes and unconfirmed information must be resolved before public release. Design and implementation are handled separately."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "About", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Aims and scope"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Project Research and Practice Journal (PRAPJ) is being developed for formal publication as an international journal connecting project research with professional practice. Its initial focus is experienced and mid-career project professionals who need credible evidence for real decisions and can contribute practical knowledge. Researchers and educators are part of this exchange; selected accessible explanations may later support students and emerging professionals."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ’s scope encompasses projects, programs, portfolios, project-based organisations and the settings in which they operate. It welcomes contributions that explain how projects are conceived, governed, delivered and evaluated, and how their consequences affect organisations, communities and society."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Relevant topics include governance and controls; credible and deliverable business cases; skilled resources; dependencies across projects and organisational silos; portfolio prioritisation; benefits and value; leadership and collaboration; risk, uncertainty and complexity; procurement; learning; AI and digital change; and sustainability. Contributions should connect a problem to a project objective and assessable evidence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Contributions may address infrastructure, construction, technology, manufacturing, healthcare, public administration or other project settings. Explain the objective, evidence sources and material context. Learning may transfer across industries when objectives and conditions are comparable; identify what the reader must verify and where the lesson may fail."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ’s proposed content model combines Research Articles, Practitioner Cases, Research-to-Practice Briefs, and Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes. Research is evaluated for its contribution, methods and evidence. Practice contributions are assessed for the credibility of their account and the conditions under which their lessons may transfer. Translation content connects practical implications to identifiable source evidence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ values transparent methods, proportionate claims and clear limitations. Negative findings, unsuccessful interventions and mixed outcomes can make useful contributions when they support credible learning. Promotional stories, unsupported opinion and technical studies with no substantive project-management contribution are outside the proposed scope."}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "About editorial leadership and governance", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Editorial board"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ’s editorial structure is designed to bring scholarly expertise and professional judgement into the evaluation of project knowledge. Confirmed editors and board members will be listed with their responsibilities, affiliations, areas of expertise and country or region."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The Editor-in-Chief will lead the editorial programme and hold responsibility for manuscript decisions under the adopted policies. Research editors will oversee research contributions. Practice and Translation Editors will oversee practitioner evidence and the fidelity of research-to-practice content. The editorial office will coordinate submission records, communications and production."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "An Advisory Board will support strategy, community engagement and the long-term development of the journal. Advisory membership will be distinguished from responsibility for evaluating individual submissions."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Public profile field", "Information to publish after appointment is accepted"], ["Name and role", "Full preferred name and exact editorial appointment"], ["Affiliation", "Approved institutional or professional affiliation"], ["Expertise", "Research themes, methods or areas of professional practice"], ["Location", "Country or region associated with the appointment"], ["Biography", "Short professional biography and verified profile link"], ["Term and responsibilities", "Appointment period and principal responsibilities"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Editorial independence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed governance model gives editorial decisions to the Editor-in-Chief or an independent delegated editor. Sponsors and advisers support the mission and resources of the journal without directing acceptance or rejection of individual manuscripts. Submissions from editors, advisers or people with relevant relationships will receive independent handling."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Review expertise"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research Articles and Evidence Reviews require at least two independent external reports under the proposed double-anonymous model. Practitioner Cases normally receive two reports covering practice and research evidence. Briefs receive editorial translation review; Practice Challenges and Evidence Notes receive editorial and domain assessment with further review when necessary. Each item will identify its actual assessment route."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Institutional and community relationships"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ’s business case identifies the PMDOK community as a potential source of research, cases and professional questions. The nature of any formal partnership, institutional hosting or professional-body affiliation will be stated once confirmed. Invitations arising from a conference or community event will remain subject to the relevant editorial and review standards."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Editorial office"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Editorial and technical contact details will be published when the responsible roles and submission channel are confirmed. Prospective contributors should use only the contact information displayed on the official journal website."}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "About journal insights", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Journal information"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Information", "PRAPJ content"], ["Full title", "Project Research and Practice Journal"], ["Abbreviation", "PRAPJ"], ["Subject areas", "Project management, project organising, governance, leadership, organisational learning and research-practice exchange"], ["Intended readership", "Primary: experienced and mid-career project professionals. Also researchers and educators; selected student explanations may follow."], ["Language", "English under the proposed author requirements"], ["Publication format", "Digital-first model proposed"], ["Core content", "Research Articles; Practitioner Cases; Research-to-Practice Briefs; Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes"], ["Access and charges", "Diamond open access is the preferred model, subject to confirmed support and adoption"], ["Publication frequency", "Rolling publication and two curated issue collections annually are proposed for the pilot"], ["Publisher and host", "To be confirmed"], ["Online and print ISSN", "To be announced following registration of the relevant publication formats"], ["DOI and preservation", "Service arrangements to be confirmed"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Publishing timeline"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ has no verified publication history from which to report processing statistics. Information on submission assessment, review and publication will be added. Review duration will vary with article type, reviewer availability and the extent of revision."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For future reporting, the journal should distinguish submission to initial decision; submission to the first decision following external review; submission to acceptance; and acceptance to online publication. Published figures should identify the reporting period and the relevant article population."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Abstracting and indexing"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Indexing information will identify services that have independently confirmed inclusion. Planned applications, metadata registration and a DOI do not establish inclusion in a citation database. Indexing coverage will be updated as the journal develops."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Impact and use"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Citation indicators will be reported only when independently assigned. PRAPJ also intends to examine how its content is used in professional and educational settings, including engagement with briefs and documented examples of application. Such measures will be distinguished from citation-based indicators and will not imply that use caused a particular project outcome."}]}, {"id": "s4", "title": "About news and community content", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "News"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Follow the development of PRAPJ, including editorial appointments, publication plans and opportunities for research-practice exchange. News items will identify their publication date and link to the relevant information or call."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Suggested initial news item: Introducing the PRAPJ journal concept. PRAPJ is developing a publication model that combines research, documented practice and accessible evidence briefs. Its focus is on the quality of evidence, the practical decisions it informs and the professional questions that can shape future research. Details of the pilot and contribution opportunities will be announced as arrangements are confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Announcements"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Official announcements will communicate approved changes to journal policies, submission arrangements and publication schedules. Each announcement will state what has changed, when the change takes effect and which contributors or submissions are affected. Information on recognition or indexing will be published only after confirmation by the relevant organisation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Videos"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ is exploring optional video interviews, presentations and short explanations alongside written contributions. Availability depends on approved editorial and production capacity. Each item would identify its speakers, sources, related article, version and actual review status, retain limitations, and provide captions and a transcript for spoken video. Contributor approval and rights clearance are required; source corrections must remain linked."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Awards"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Information on any future PRAPJ awards will be announced after the award categories, eligibility, assessment criteria and selection process have been approved. Recognition should reflect demonstrable contribution, with declared conflicts and independent assessment. There are no award recipients to announce in this content proposal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Author testimonials"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Author experiences will be shared when contributors have completed the publishing process and agreed to publication of their comments. Testimonials will identify the contributor, relevant article and date where permission allows. Statements will reflect the contributor’s experience and will not promise an equivalent outcome for other authors."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Calls for papers and collections"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Formal calls will identify the purpose of the collection, topics, eligible contribution types, guest editors, submission dates and review arrangements. Special collections will remain subject to the journal’s ordinary integrity and editorial standards. A proposed collection should include a clear rationale, intended audience, contribution pipeline, reviewer capacity and conflict-management arrangements."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A collection of new submissions should be distinguished from a thematic selection of previously published articles. Invitation, conference participation or an award will not guarantee acceptance."}]}, {"id": "s5", "title": "About the Research Summary Series", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Research evidence for practical decisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ’s proposed Research Summary Series will help project professionals understand the practical meaning of research. Its principal format is the Research-to-Practice Brief: a structured interpretation of identifiable evidence that explains the decision context, findings, possible actions and limits of application."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Each brief will link to its source and make clear whether it interprets one study or synthesises several sources. Readers should be able to distinguish source findings from editorial interpretation and suggested action. Briefs will not claim new research results unless these have been evaluated through the appropriate research publication route."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "What readers will find"}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "The project decision or problem addressed by the evidence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "The study setting, approach and principal findings in accessible language."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "What the evidence may change in professional judgement or practice."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Conditions, prerequisites and limitations that affect application."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "Focused questions or actions supported by the evidence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "List Bullet", "text": "A citation and persistent link to the underlying research, where available."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Selection and preparation"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed series will prioritise evidence relevant to identifiable professional decisions. Selection will consider the strength of the source, the importance of the problem and the ability to explain practical meaning without overstating the findings."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Under the proposed author model, accepted Research Articles, including evidence reviews, will support a linked brief of 1,200–2,000 words. Authors will prepare the first version with editorial guidance; the translation editor will review fidelity, clarity and applicability. The source article may proceed to publication once ready while a brief completes its separate editorial checks. The two items will be linked when both are available."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A brief based on research published elsewhere may be considered when the source is sufficiently accessible for assessment and all reuse rights are respected. It must cite the original study, avoid reproducing protected material without permission, and describe the source’s publication and review status."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Quality and review status"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research-to-Practice Briefs will receive editorial translation review. This checks traceability, accuracy, decision relevance, uncertainty, transfer conditions, accessibility and commercial neutrality. The brief’s review label will distinguish this process from independent peer review of a Research Article."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Series launch"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The series will open with approved content following confirmation of the journal’s editorial and publishing arrangements. Every item will identify its authors or credited contributors, date, source research and version. Related videos or interviews may supplement a brief when permissions and editorial checks are complete."}]}, {"id": "s6", "title": "Publish with PRAPJ", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Find the right contribution format"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ’s proposed publication routes support original research, credible practice evidence and communication between these communities. Select the format that matches the evidence and purpose of your work. Explain both what readers can learn and the limits of that learning."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Contribution", "Proposed length", "Principal requirement"], ["Research Article", "6,000–9,000 words", "Defensible contribution, transparent method and evidence, and practical implications"], ["Evidence Review within the research track", "5,000–8,000 words", "Transparent search, selection, appraisal and synthesis appropriate to the review design"], ["Practitioner Case", "3,000–5,000 words", "Context, challenge, intervention, outcome evidence and limits of transfer"], ["Research-to-Practice Brief", "1,200–2,000 words", "Traceable interpretation of source research for a practical decision"], ["Practice Challenge or Evidence Note", "1,500–2,500 words", "Documented problem or focused evidence, with unresolved questions clearly identified"], ["Invited Editorial or Debate", "1,500–3,000 words", "Evidence-informed argument with transparent status and declared interests"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The length calculation counts the main text, footnotes, table text, captions and appendices intended to appear with the article. It excludes the title page, abstract, keywords, decision-relevance statement, highlights, declarations, references and separately supplied supplementary files. Figures have no automatic word allowance. Essential methods and evidence must remain in the main article. Teaching cases are deferred in the proposed pilot taxonomy."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Guide for Authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The separate PRAPJ Guide for Authors provides proposed requirements for article structure, authorship, declarations, research ethics, AI use, anonymisation, figures, data, references, submission and publication. It includes a submission checklist and reusable declaration and cover-letter prompts."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research Articles and evidence reviews use a 200–300-word abstract; other substantive formats use a 150–200-word summary. All core tracks include a 100–150-word decision-relevance statement and 4–7 keywords. These specifications form one proposed editorial standard across the website and author guide."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Submit your article"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Submission opening date: to be announced. Submission portal: to be confirmed. Prospective authors should consult the approved Guide for Authors and the official submission page before sending files. Conference-derived work must disclose the earlier version and explain the substantive new contribution."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Review and decisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research and practice evidence are assessed through routes suited to their purpose. Editorial decisions consider contribution, evidence, integrity, clarity and fit. Invitations, positive pre-submission feedback and requests for revision do not constitute acceptance. The adopted review process and complaints route will be displayed before submissions open."}]}, {"id": "s7", "title": "Publish open access information", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Introduction"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ’s preferred model is diamond open access: articles would be freely available to readers, with no submission or article publishing charge to authors. This model depends on sufficient institutional or sponsor support for editorial and publishing operations. The final access and fee policy will be published before submissions open."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed pilot is digital-first. Articles would be published as they become ready, with two curated issue collections each year. The launch date and operating arrangements remain to be confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Open access licence and user rights"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "CC BY 4.0 is the proposed publication licence, subject to publisher adoption and author agreement. It permits sharing and adaptation, including commercial use, with attribution, a licence link and identification of changes. Third-party material may carry separate terms, and privacy or other rights may still constrain reuse. No licence or publishing agreement is executed by this draft."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Copyright and publishing agreement"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed arrangement is for authors to retain copyright and grant the publisher the rights needed to publish, preserve and identify the version of record. This arrangement and the selected licence require confirmation in the author agreement. Authors must have the authority to grant the relevant rights, including any necessary agreement from their institution or other rights holders."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Article publishing charges"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Charge or service", "Proposed PRAPJ position"], ["Submission fee", "No fee under the preferred diamond model; adoption pending"], ["Article publishing charge", "No APC under the preferred diamond model; adoption pending"], ["Reader access", "No reader paywall under the preferred model"], ["Online colour or supplementary files", "No separate mandatory charge proposed"], ["Optional printing or external editing", "No service or price has been established"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Pricing transparency"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Any future charge must be stated clearly before an author makes a submission or orders an optional service. The journal will distinguish compulsory fees from optional services and state any relevant waiver arrangements. No fee, sponsorship or purchase will buy favourable editorial treatment. Authors should not send payment in response to an unverified request."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Institutional agreements and funding"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Confirmed publishing support or agreements will be described with their scope and duration. No institutional eligibility, fee discount or funding arrangement should be inferred from an author’s affiliation until an agreement has been confirmed. Authors remain responsible for identifying any funder requirements concerning licences, repositories or reporting."}]}, {"id": "s8", "title": "Publish sharing and author support", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Self archiving"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Under the proposed immediate open-access model, the journal would impose no additional embargo on sharing an article published under its confirmed open licence. Authors should use the article’s citation and persistent link, identify the version shared and preserve licence and third-party credit information. Earlier manuscript versions should link to the final article when it becomes available."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Preprints are distinct from accepted manuscripts and the version of record. The proposed policy permits preprints with disclosure at submission. A preprint must not be labelled as peer-reviewed journal publication. Copyright, consent and confidentiality obligations continue to apply to all versions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Responsible sharing"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Share a persistent article link where possible. Describe findings accurately and retain relevant limitations when preparing social posts, presentations, teaching materials or summaries. Permission to reuse an article does not imply endorsement of a product, service or interpretation by its authors or the journal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authors preparing derivative briefs or educational materials should identify the source article, acknowledge adaptation and check the licence applicable to each component. Published corrections or retractions should remain visible through links to the current publication record."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Author resources and support"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The PRAPJ Guide for Authors explains the proposed editorial requirements and includes practical preparation tools. Available resources should be labelled by version so that prospective authors can use a consistent set of instructions."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Resource", "Purpose"], ["Guide for Authors", "Scope, contribution formats, preparation rules and editorial policies"], ["Submission checklist", "Final check of files, declarations, permissions and metadata"], ["Research-to-Practice Brief outline", "Convert research findings into a traceable practical interpretation"], ["Declaration prompts", "Report funding, interests, ethics, data and material AI use accurately"], ["Revision response format", "Address reviewer and editor comments with traceable changes"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Language and accessibility"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Write in clear English and use one spelling convention consistently. Explain specialist terms and make the practical meaning of evidence easy to locate. Text, tables, figures and video should be accessible to readers using different devices or assistive technologies."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ is exploring a supported contribution route in which an editor helps a practitioner develop an evidence-based account from a structured conversation. Scope and availability remain to be confirmed. Contributors would verify the account and approve submission; assistance would be credited and ordinary independent assessment retained. An interview or editing support would not guarantee publication. External editing remains the author's responsibility."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contact details and technical assistance"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Editorial enquiries, technical support and complaints contacts will be published once the responsible teams and channels are confirmed. The website will distinguish a submission enquiry from a formal appeal or integrity concern. No manuscript transfer service or external submission platform is implied by this proposal."}]}, {"id": "s9", "title": "Editorial source and release note", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This version incorporates the supplied PRAPJ draft and interviews through 8 October 2026, including James Bawtree's recommendations on experienced professional readers, supported contributions, context and media. Emerald and IJPM remain process benchmarks inspected on 25 September. Formal publication is intended; appointments, resources and adoption remain pending. No actual capstone budget is approved or allocated and no journal funding is committed. Keep this internal note off public pages."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald publishing process"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald peer review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Emerald publication ethics"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "IJPM Guide for Authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0"}]}], "words": 2658}, {"id": "06", "title": "Website Content Handoff", "filename": "06_PRAPJ_Website_Content_Handoff.docx", "sha256": "91fff9aa111ace682df3fbb770331c84ebb1bfd71bb907146016f183a971434c", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "PRAPJ Website Content Handoff"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Version 1.3 | 8 October 2026 | English copy for the website team"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This handoff supplies website text. The separate About and Publish document contains the longer journal and publishing pages; the Guide for Authors supplies the authoritative proposed manuscript requirements. The accompanying digital mockup illustrates how this copy can appear in a journal website. It is a static design prototype. The website team remains responsible for the operational site and submission system."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Using the copy"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Text labelled Public copy can be transferred into the relevant page after editorial approval. Bracketed fields are editorial replacement instructions, not publishable text. Items labelled Internal note must remain outside the public website. Do not replace unknown facts with plausible names, dates, identifiers or performance figures."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Content ownership and release"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Content", "Proposed approver", "Release condition"], ["Home and journal identity", "Sponsor and founding Editor-in-Chief", "Journal name and development status confirmed"], ["Aims scope and article types", "Editor-in-Chief", "Same taxonomy and limits as the author guide"], ["Board profiles", "Appointing publisher and individual member", "Written acceptance and approved profile"], ["Fees licence and publishing agreement", "Host or publisher", "Adopted terms and support confirmed"], ["Review appeals and integrity pages", "Editor-in-Chief and independent complaints handler", "Roles and contact routes appointed"], ["Privacy contact and accessibility information", "Host and website owner", "Actual organisation and service arrangements supplied"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Companion content map"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "About and Publish contains aims and scope; governance; journal information; review routes; research summaries; publishing formats; access and fees; sharing; news; and author support. The Guide for Authors contains the detailed requirements. This handoff adds homepage blocks, concise status text, article and issue content fields, practical labels and publication controls."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The machine-readable companion, PRAPJ_Website_Copy.json, contains only the short public-copy blocks in this handoff, keyed by page and element. It is a content source for the website team. The separate prototype README maps demonstrated interactions to future implementation requirements. Longer approved copy can be copied from the two companion Word documents."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Current state"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: PRAPJ is in development. Submissions are not yet open. Editorial appointments, publishing arrangements and the submission channel will be announced after confirmation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: Retain this development status until the sponsor and publisher authorise a change. Do not display candidate names as confirmed board members, display fabricated metrics or imply that a submission control is operational."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "Homepage copy", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Journal identity"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: Project Research and Practice Journal"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Short name: PRAPJ"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Page title: Project Research and Practice Journal | Research and practice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Search description: PRAPJ is being developed for formal publication, connecting credible project evidence with practical decisions for experienced project professionals."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Opening section"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Headline: Evidence for project decisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: PRAPJ is being developed for formal publication as a journal connecting credible research with project decisions. Our initial focus is experienced and mid-career professionals who use evidence and contribute practical knowledge. Research findings, documented experience and unresolved questions inform this exchange."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Supporting copy: Find the project objective, decision, evidence and conditions that help you judge whether a lesson applies in your setting."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Status: Journal in development. Submissions are not yet open."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Primary link label: About the journal"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Secondary link label: Prepare a contribution"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Four contribution routes"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research Articles: Develop and test knowledge about projects with transparent methods, evidence and practical implications. Evidence Reviews form part of this research track."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Practitioner Cases: Explain a real project decision or intervention, its evidence, observed outcomes and the limits of transferring its lessons."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research-to-Practice Briefs: Understand what identifiable research may mean for a professional decision, with clear source links and limits of application."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes: Bring a documented problem, focused evidence or an unresolved professional question into research-practice discussion."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Research and practice exchange"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: Useful evidence can start with a research question or a problem encountered in practice. PRAPJ's proposed model supports both directions: research that informs judgement, and practice experience that helps define what needs to be investigated."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Publication area before launch"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Heading: Publications will appear here"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: PRAPJ has not yet released a journal collection. Publication information will be added after the editorial and publishing arrangements are confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Author resource area"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Heading: Start with the Guide for Authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: Choose the contribution format that matches your evidence. The proposed guide explains structure, review routes, declarations, permissions and preparation requirements."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Supported contribution copy: Interview-supported contributions and optional video or short explanations are being explored. Availability and requirements will be confirmed before these services are offered."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Link labels: Read the Guide for Authors | View submission checklist | Explore research briefs"}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "Articles collections and search copy", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Articles page before launch"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Heading: Articles"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: No PRAPJ articles have been published. Accepted work will appear here after review, production and publication checks are complete."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: Use demonstration records only in an explicitly labelled design environment. Do not publish the print specimen as an accepted journal article."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Collections page before launch"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Heading: Issues and collections"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: The proposed pilot will publish articles when they are ready and gather them into two curated collections each year. The first collection and publication dates will be announced after confirmation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Practice briefs page"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Heading: Research-to-Practice Briefs"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: Briefs explain the practical meaning of identifiable research for a project objective and decision. Each describes source evidence, possible actions and transfer conditions. Editorial translation review checks accuracy and clarity; it is distinct from independent research peer review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Empty state: No PRAPJ briefs have been published yet."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Article content fields for the editorial team"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field", "Text or evidence to supply"], ["Title and authors", "Accepted title, approved author order and affiliations"], ["Type and review status", "Actual article type and assessment completed"], ["Summary and decision relevance", "Accepted text identifying user, project objective, decision and transfer conditions within the author-guide limits"], ["Publication information", "Actual publication date, version, identifier and collection where assigned"], ["Full text and download", "Matching approved accessible versions; use DOCX for the current preparation package"], ["Sources and related brief", "Verified source links, related brief and any approved media; retain source version and correction links"], ["Data declarations and rights", "Approved statements, adopted licence and component exceptions"], ["Updates", "Correction, retraction or other notice linked to the current record"], ["Optional media", "Title, audience, source article/version, speakers and credits, actual review label, captions/transcript, approvals and correction link"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Labels and empty states"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Reading labels: Abstract | Summary | Decision relevance | Full text | References | Data and materials | Declarations | Related brief | Publication history | Corrections and updates"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Action labels: Read article | Download article | Cite this article | Read the source research | View related brief"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Search label: Search PRAPJ articles"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Search prompt: Search by title, topic or author"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "No results: No articles match your search. Try fewer terms or a broader topic."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Service unavailable: This content is temporarily unavailable. Please try again later or use the confirmed support contact."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: Do not display a DOI, review status or date until the editorial record supports it."}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "Publish and submission status copy", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Publish landing page"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Headline: Prepare a contribution to PRAPJ"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: Choose the format that matches your evidence and purpose. Explain the project decision, how the evidence was obtained, what readers can learn and where the findings may not apply. Read the proposed Guide for Authors before preparing your files."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Current submission notice: Submissions are not yet open. The official portal and opening date will be announced once editorial and publishing arrangements are confirmed. Please do not send manuscripts or payments to an unverified address."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Resource labels: Guide for Authors | Article types and lengths | Submission checklist | Manuscript templates | Review process | Access and charges"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Author guide introduction"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: This guide explains PRAPJ's proposed requirements for contribution formats, manuscript preparation, evidence, authorship, ethics, permissions, data and review. The guide remains subject to editorial adoption. Use the version shown on this page when preparing your work."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Version label: Proposed guide version 1.3 | 8 October 2026"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Review process introduction"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: PRAPJ proposes assessment routes suited to each contribution. Research Articles receive at least two independent external reviews. Practitioner Cases normally receive two external reviews covering practice and research evidence. Briefs receive editorial translation review. Practice Challenges and Evidence Notes receive editorial and domain assessment, with further review when their claims require it."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Independence copy: Sponsors and advisers will not determine individual manuscript decisions. Submissions involving an editor or a relevant relationship will be assigned independent handling."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Fees and rights notice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: Diamond open access is the preferred model, subject to confirmed support and adoption. Under that model, there would be no submission fee, article publishing charge or reader paywall. Final fees, licence terms and publishing arrangements will be stated before submissions open."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Appeals and concerns"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: The independent contact for appeals, complaints and research-integrity concerns will be published before submissions open. Please retain the manuscript identifier and explain the factual or procedural issue clearly when contacting the journal."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Copy to hold until launch approval"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: The website team must not publish the following text until a real service exists and its terms are adopted."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Approved launch notice template: Submissions are open for [approved formats or collection]. Submit through [verified portal]. Please read Guide for Authors version [version], the fee policy and the privacy notice before uploading files."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Receipt template: Submission received. Your manuscript identifier is [actual ID]. Receipt is not editorial acceptance. You can follow your submission through [verified route]."}]}, {"id": "s4", "title": "About governance contact and content controls", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Short About copy"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: PRAPJ is being developed as an international journal connecting project research with professional practice. Its scope includes projects, programs, portfolios, project-based organisations and their wider contexts. Contributions should make their evidence, practical meaning and limitations clear."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Board page before appointments"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Heading: Editorial leadership and advisory governance"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy: Founding appointments are being developed. Confirmed members will be listed with their responsibilities, approved affiliations and expertise after appointments are accepted. The Advisory Board will support strategy and relevance; the Editorial Board will oversee assessment and editorial quality."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: Keep the prospective candidate shortlist internal. Interview participation, broad support and expressions of interest are not confirmed appointments."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Journal information status"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Publisher and host: To be confirmed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Publication dates: To be announced"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Identifiers and preservation: Arrangements to be confirmed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Indexing: No inclusion has been confirmed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Publication metrics: No verified publication history is available"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contact and accessibility copy"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public copy before contacts are confirmed: Editorial enquiries and technical support details will be published once the responsible teams and channels are confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Accessibility holding copy: Accessibility information and a contact for reporting barriers will be added with the confirmed website service."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: The host must supply an accurate privacy notice identifying the responsible entity, data collected, purpose, access, retention and contact routes before collecting personal data. Do not claim a retention period, legal compliance or accessibility conformance that has not been established. This is a content dependency, not a request to implement a system in this package."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "News and optional content"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed concept news copy: PRAPJ is developing a publication model that combines research, documented practice and accessible evidence briefs. Its focus is the quality of evidence, the professional decisions it informs and the practical questions that can shape future research. Pilot details and contribution opportunities will be announced as arrangements are confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Optional media copy: Interview-supported contributions and optional video or short explanations are being explored. Availability and requirements will be confirmed before these services are offered."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: Future feedback may ask whether an item was useful on a one-to-five scale and why, and whether the reader returned or would recommend it. Define denominators and privacy arrangements before collection. Keep testimonials permission-based and separate feedback from evidence of improved project outcomes."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Internal note: Hide empty awards, testimonials, video and calls pages until approved content exists. Optional media needs contributor approval, rights clearance, source and correction links, an accurate assessment label, captions and transcripts. Do not invent endorsements or imply professional CPD recognition."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Handoff acceptance"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The website content owner should check the guide version, word limits, terminology, review labels and development status before using the text. Record the page, approving person, approval date and any replacement fields. Review the accompanying digital mockup against this copy and record acceptance in the capstone completion record. The website team should resolve implementation changes before launch."}]}], "words": 1824}, {"id": "07", "title": "Author & Editorial Templates", "filename": "07_PRAPJ_Author_and_Editorial_Templates.docx", "sha256": "1644a1b9d86f099e578747a6c5353fc7edfc4285069dd6ce763f42c6f684b02f", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "PRAPJ Author and Editorial Template Kit"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Version 1.3 | 8 October 2026 | Editable working forms for the proposed pilot"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use these forms with the Guide for Authors and Reviewer Guide. Author_Templates supplies standalone T01–T06, T09, T13 and T14. The four manuscript shells use 12-point type, double spacing, 2.5 cm margins and page numbers; this kit uses compact reference formatting. Copy operational forms when needed, expand response spaces and complete prompts truthfully. Submissions are not open; the author guide controls requirements."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Template directory"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Code", "Template", "Handling"], ["T01", "Title page and declarations", "Private editorial file"], ["T02", "Research Article or Evidence Review", "Anonymous review manuscript"], ["T03", "Practitioner Case", "Anonymous where feasible; disclose identification limits"], ["T04", "Research to Practice Brief", "Public text after editorial translation review"], ["T05", "Practice Challenge or Evidence Note", "Editorial and domain assessment"], ["T06", "Cover letter and submission checklist", "Private editorial file"], ["T07", "Independent reviewer report", "Author comments and confidential editor comments separated"], ["T08", "Translation review record", "Editorial assessment of a brief"], ["T09", "Response to reviewers", "Revised submission record"], ["T10", "Permissions and production release record", "Confidential evidence retained separately"], ["T11", "Board invitation and acceptance", "Internal governance record; unsent"], ["T12", "Editorial decision letters", "Private correspondence; unsent"], ["T13", "Interview supported contribution", "Private consent evidence and approval record"], ["T14", "Media companion release", "Private fidelity rights and release checks"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Common preparation rules"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "All core manuscripts need a separate 100–150-word decision-relevance statement and 4–7 keywords. Research uses a 200–300-word abstract; other core formats use a 150–200-word summary. Count main text, footnotes, table text, captions and published appendices; exclude the title page, summaries, keywords, decision relevance, highlights, declarations, references and separate supplements. No automatic figure allowance applies."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use an editable Word manuscript, 11- or 12-point readable type, double spacing and approximately 2.5 cm margins. Number pages and, where practical, lines. Provide names, contact information and complete declarations privately. Remove identifying metadata and acknowledgements from anonymous files without obscuring methods or inventing identity details."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Information handling"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Do not put identifiable consent records, private commercial documents or reviewer identities in files intended for publication. Complete declarations only when true. A form is not a publishing contract, ethics approval or permission to release an organisation's data. The future host must adopt operational terms and secure storage before these forms collect real submissions."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "T01 Private title page and declarations", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Submission title: [Full title]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Article type: [Select core track and subtype] | Main-text word count: [Count]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authors in agreed order: [Full preferred names, affiliations, country and ORCID if held]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Corresponding author: [Name, institutional or professional email, postal affiliation]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Author contributions: [Names and relevant CRediT roles; describe substantial contributions accurately]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Required declarations"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Funding and support: [Funder, grant, in-kind support and involvement in design, analysis or reporting; state no support only if accurate]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Competing interests: [Employment, advisory roles, financial or personal interests relevant to this work; include relationships to PRAPJ editors or sponsors]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Ethics and consent: [Approval or exemption body, identifier and date, or a reasoned account of the applicable process; consent and privacy protections]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Organisational and third-party permissions: [Records, images, quotations and other material; authorised rights holder; permission evidence and restrictions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Data and materials: [Repository and persistent identifier, controlled access process, restrictions, or an accurate explanation that no new dataset was produced]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "AI and other assistance: [Tool, version or access date, task, affected content and verification performed; describe AI used as a research method in the methods section too]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Acknowledgements: [Contributors who do not qualify for authorship; permission to name each person]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Submission history and confirmations"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Preprint or earlier version: [Citation, link, date and substantive differences; include conference versions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Related submissions or overlapping work: [Describe and provide copies privately where needed]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Collection or invitation: [Name and details, or not applicable; invitation is not acceptance]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "All authors approved this version: [Yes / No; resolve before submitting]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Work is not under concurrent journal consideration: [Yes / No; explain any exception for editorial consideration]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authors have authority to submit and disclose this material: [Confirm and explain restrictions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Anonymisation exceptions: [Identify material that could reveal identity and why its inclusion is necessary]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Completed by: [Name] | Date: [Date] | Manuscript version: [Version]"}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "T02 Research manuscript shell", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research Article: 6,000–9,000 main-text words. Evidence Review subtype: 5,000–8,000. Prepare a separate private title page. At least two independent external reviews are proposed under double-anonymous review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Title abstract and relevance"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Descriptive title without identifying author details]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Abstract, 200–300 words: [Purpose and question; design and evidence; principal findings; implications and limitations]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Decision relevance, 100–150 words: [Who faces which decision; how the evidence could inform it; conditions and uncertainty]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Keywords: [4–7 terms]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "1 Introduction"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Define the project-management problem, why it matters, the knowledge gap and the precise question. Explain the intended contribution without assuming all existing research lacks practical relevance.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "2 Prior knowledge and conceptual basis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Critically position the contribution. Define key constructs and the argument or propositions tested. Distinguish original sources from secondary summaries.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "3 Methods and evidence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Design, setting, sampling or selection, observation period, sources, instruments, analysis and quality checks. Explain consent, ethics and access while preserving anonymity. Report limitations of design and deviations from protocols.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For an Evidence Review: [State the review design; databases and other sources; full search dates and strategies; inclusion and exclusion criteria; screening; quality appraisal; extraction; synthesis and limits. Provide a reproducible search supplement. Do not label a review systematic unless its method supports the label.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "4 Results or synthesis"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Report findings with units, denominators and uncertainty where applicable. Keep observed findings distinct from interpretation. Include conflicting or null results that affect the account.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "5 Discussion and use in practice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Explain the contribution relative to prior knowledge. Identify the decisions informed, possible actions, prerequisites, implementation resources and transfer conditions. Do not claim causal effects unsupported by the design.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "6 Limitations and conclusion"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[What remains unknown, where findings may not apply and the proportionate conclusion.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Declarations references and supplements"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Review-appropriate declarations; verified author–date references; numbered, described and cited supplements. Full identifying declarations belong in T01.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Linked brief planning note to editor: [Candidate decision, proposed author and source findings for a separate T04 brief. The research article may publish when ready without waiting for the brief.]"}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "T03 Practitioner Case manuscript shell", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "3,000–5,000 main-text words. Normally two external reviewers whose combined expertise covers practice and research evidence. A credible case can include mixed results or failure; it is not a promotional success story."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Title: [Specific project decision or learning problem]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Summary, 150–200 words: [Setting, challenge, action, observed evidence and limits]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Decision relevance, 100–150 words: [Who might use this account and for what decision]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Keywords: [4–7 terms]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "1 Context and author's role"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Project objective, sector, scale, stage, dates, governance and constraints. Explain your involvement and possible bias. Identify the conditions relevant to transferring the lesson. Use approved pseudonyms and disclose them.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "2 Challenge and alternatives"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[What decision was required? What options and baseline conditions existed? What was known at the time, as distinct from hindsight?]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "3 Intervention or response"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Who did what, when and with what resources? Explain the mechanism expected to help, implementation problems and concurrent changes.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "4 Evidence and outcomes"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Claim or observation", "Source and period", "Verification and limitation"], ["[Outcome or process observation]", "[Record, measure or interview]", "[Who checked; missing data; bias]"], ["[Conflicting or unexpected finding]", "[Source and period]", "[Alternative explanation]"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Compare available records, schedules, measures and accounts, explaining discrepancies and missing evidence. Separate intended from observed outcomes and give baselines and denominators. Do not imply that a descriptive account establishes causation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "5 Lessons and transfer conditions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[What could another project with a comparable objective try, including in another sector? State prerequisites, resource implications, contextual differences and reasons the lesson could fail.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "6 Unresolved questions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Which issues need further research or additional practice evidence?]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Permissions and declarations"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Summarise public permissions and confidentiality protections. Send identifying evidence privately. Include funding, conflicts, data availability and material AI assistance. Add verified references and any reviewable supplementary evidence.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Identification note to editor: [Explain if the project remains recognisable. Any departure from anonymous handling must be documented and agreed before sharing files. Do not promise anonymity that cannot be achieved.]"}]}, {"id": "s4", "title": "T04 Research to Practice Brief manuscript shell", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "1,200–2,000 main-text words. Review label: Editorial translation review. A brief must not imply that it received independent research peer review or that it supplies new tested results."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Title: [The decision informed by the evidence]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Summary, 150–200 words: [Source evidence, practical meaning and key boundary]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Decision relevance, 100–150 words: [Intended user, decision and circumstances]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Keywords: [4–7 terms]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "1 The decision"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Identify the professional reader, project objective and decision. Explain why the evidence matters without promising a universal solution.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "2 Source evidence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Full source citations and persistent links, version and review status. State whether this is one study or a synthesis. Confirm lawful access for assessment and permission for reused material.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "3 What the research examined and found"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Setting, population, methods and period. Report material findings and uncertainty faithfully. Explain technical terms. Do not convert association into causation.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "4 What it could mean for practice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Distinguish source findings from your interpretation. Identify possible actions, prerequisites, costs or effort and questions to ask before applying the findings.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "5 Where the evidence may not transfer"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Compare project objectives and material conditions, including across sectors. Identify design limits, conflicting evidence, untested assumptions and what a reader should not infer.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "6 A practical next step"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[A proportionate action or small test. Specify what evidence a practitioner should observe and when to reconsider. Do not invent a successful implementation.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Sources and declarations"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[References, interests, funding, contributions, data statement where relevant, permissions and AI assistance. Link to the source article and any subsequent correction.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Source traceability check for the translation editor"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Brief claim or suggested action", "Source location", "Finding interpretation or proposal"], ["[Claim]", "[Page, section, table or figure]", "[Classification and limitation]"], ["[Action]", "[Source or stated reasoning]", "[Interpretation or proposal]"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For a source published elsewhere, confirm the publication status and rights. A PRAPJ brief does not transfer the source journal's branding, endorsement or review label to the brief."}]}, {"id": "s5", "title": "T05 Practice Challenge or Evidence Note shell", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "1,500–2,500 main-text words. Editorial screening and domain assessment; substantive new empirical claims may require the Research or Case route and external review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Title: [The unresolved decision or focused evidence question]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Summary, 150–200 words: [Problem, evidence available and question remaining]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Decision relevance, 100–150 words: [Who needs an answer and what is at stake]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Keywords: [4–7 terms]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "1 Problem in context"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Describe the project objective, setting and decision. Explain how the issue was identified and why current guidance does not resolve it.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "2 Evidence currently available"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Identify records, observations, participant accounts or research. Compare sources where available, explain discrepancies and distinguish verified information from judgement and assumptions. State disclosure limits.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "3 Competing explanations or options"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Present plausible alternatives fairly. Identify consequences, uncertainty and missing evidence. Avoid making an unsupported commercial claim.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "4 Questions for research and practice"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Frame focused, answerable questions. Identify the kind of evidence or collaboration that would help, while making no promise of data access or participation.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "5 Contribution requested"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Request evidence, methods, documented comparable cases or constructive responses through an approved editorial route. Do not include private contact information without permission.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Limitations references and declarations"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Limits of the evidence, verified sources, author role, funding, conflicts, data restrictions, ethics and permissions, and material assistance.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Editorial triage record"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Selected route: [Challenge / Evidence Note / Refer to Research / Refer to Case]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Reason and required expertise: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Claims requiring independent assessment: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Permission or privacy conditions before review: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Handling editor and date: [Complete when assigned]"}]}, {"id": "s6", "title": "T06 Cover letter and submission checklist", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "To: PRAPJ editorial office [Use the confirmed submission portal only]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Date: [Date] | Manuscript: [Title] | Format: [Type]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dear Editor,"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Please consider [title] as a [type]. The work addresses [question] in [context]. Its principal contribution is [claim supported by evidence]. It is relevant to PRAPJ because [research and practice fit]. It may inform [specific professional decision], subject to [principal limitation]."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Describe prior dissemination and substantive differences. Disclose restrictions, editorial conflicts and any supported interview or drafting assistance. Identify proposed media and applicable private T13/T14 records.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Confirm author approval and exclusive consideration only where true. Identify necessary permissions, ethics information and any unresolved matter requiring editorial advice.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Corresponding author: [Name and contact in this private file]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Final checklist"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Mark each item Complete, Not applicable with reason, or Action required. Resolve material actions before submission."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Check", "Status and action"], ["Correct type, length and word-count calculation", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Abstract or summary, decision relevance and 4–7 keywords", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Methods or evidence provenance and limitations clear", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Separate title page and appropriate anonymisation", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["All authors approve; contributions and interests disclosed", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Ethics, consent, permissions and confidentiality addressed", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Data statement and accessible review materials provided", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["References verified and matched to citations", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Figures, accessible descriptions and supplements labelled", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Prior versions, overlap and material AI use disclosed", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["No confidential documents placed in public supplements", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Portal, fee policy and guide version verified", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Supported account: assistance credited, contributor approval and private T13 record completed where applicable", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"], ["Optional media: T14 status, rights, source links and accessible files recorded; unresolved release checks identified", "[Select status; add evidence or action]"]]}]}, {"id": "s7", "title": "T07 Independent reviewer report", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Manuscript ID: [ID] | Version: [Version] | Track: [Research / Evidence Review / Case]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Reviewer name and expertise: [Private to the editor] | Agreed due date: [Date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before accepting: [Confirm expertise, time and absence of disqualifying conflict; disclose uncertainty to the editor. Do not share the manuscript or upload it to AI tools.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Comments that may be shared with the author"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Summary of the work and contribution: [Two or three sentences showing your understanding]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Strengths: [Specific strengths supported by manuscript locations]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Major issues: [Number each concern; state evidence, why it matters and a feasible response. Separate validity problems from presentational preferences.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Minor issues: [Numbered clarifications or corrections with locations]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Assessment prompts"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For each dimension, select Meets, Partly meets, Does not meet or Not applicable and give reasons with manuscript locations. These ratings do not produce an automatic acceptance score."}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Dimension", "Assessment and evidence"], ["Fit and practical decision", "[Clarity, audience and contribution]"], ["Methods or evidence provenance", "[Appropriateness, transparency and verification]"], ["Claims and uncertainty", "[Support, alternatives, limitations and transfer]"], ["Ethics permissions and interests", "[Observed concerns; do not investigate independently]"], ["Communication and accessibility", "[Readability, tables, figures and terminology]"], ["References and supporting materials", "[Accuracy and sufficiency; no coercive citations]"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "For Evidence Reviews, assess search, selection, appraisal and synthesis. For Cases, assess the objective, author involvement, source comparison and discrepancies, baseline, observed versus intended outcomes and transfer conditions. For supported accounts, check contributor approval and the separation of preparation from independent review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Confidential comments to the editor"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Recommendation: [Accept / Minor revision / Major revision / Reject]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Reason: [Link recommendation to material strengths and weaknesses]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Integrity or conflict concern: [Specific evidence and location; avoid unsupported accusations]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Further expertise needed: [Topic and reason]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Willing to reassess a revision: [Yes / No / Conditions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Completed on: [Date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The editor makes the decision. Keep the report constructive and do not include author-facing comments only in this confidential section if the author needs them to revise."}]}, {"id": "s8", "title": "T08 Editorial translation review", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Brief ID and version: [ID] | Source article or articles: [Citations and links]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Translation editor: [Name] | Conflict check: [Result] | Date: [Date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Review outcome: [Ready / Revise / Escalate evidence concerns / Decline]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Fidelity and usefulness checks"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Criterion", "Pass revise or concern", "Evidence and required change"], ["Each material claim traced to source", "[Status]", "[Source and brief locations]"], ["Methods setting and period represented accurately", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Uncertainty and conflicting findings retained", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Findings separated from interpretation and action", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Decision and intended user are clear", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Transfer conditions and prerequisites explicit", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Language usable without specialist expertise", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Commercial neutrality and interests addressed", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Source status reuse rights and links verified", "[Status]", "[Record]"], ["Review label and corrections link accurate", "[Status]", "[Record]"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Required revisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Number changes; identify the claim, source evidence and acceptable correction. Escalate concerns about the underlying research rather than silently repairing or strengthening its findings.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Optional practitioner comprehension check"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Reader role and consent: [Record without unnecessary personal information]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Decision identified in the reader's own words: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Limitation identified: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Misinterpretation or jargon found: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Change made after feedback: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This check tests comprehension, not research validity. Record that it is a small formative exercise and do not describe it as market validation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Final release check"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Source article ready or publicly accessible: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Bidirectional links created where controlled by PRAPJ: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public review label: Editorial translation review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Approved version, editor and date: [Record]"}]}, {"id": "s9", "title": "T09 Response to reviewers", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Manuscript ID and title: [ID and title]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Revision number: [Number] | Decision letter date: [Date] | Due date: [Date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Files submitted: [Clean manuscript, marked version if requested, response, changed supplements]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Overview of the revision"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Summarise the principal changes and identify any consequential change to findings, interpretation, title, abstract, decision relevance or linked brief. Explain extensions or scope changes agreed with the editor.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Response record"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Repeat this block for every editorial and reviewer comment. Copy comments accurately or identify them clearly. Keep author responses distinguishable from reviewer text."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Comment ID: [Editor / Reviewer 1 / Reviewer 2 and number]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Comment or issue: [Comment]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Response and reasoning: [What changed and why, or a reasoned explanation where no change was made]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Exact manuscript location: [Section, page and line in revised version]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Supporting material: [New analysis, reference, table or supplement]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Related comments: [Cross-reference overlapping requests]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Additional changes initiated by the authors"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[List changes not directly requested. Identify new analyses as new; explain design changes and update relevant declarations. Seek editorial agreement before a material author-list change.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Verification before resubmission"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "All comments addressed: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Clean and marked files agree: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Abstract and conclusions reflect revised evidence: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "References, data links and permissions rechecked: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Author approval and declarations current: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Anonymous files remain appropriate for review: [Status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Corresponding author and date: [Private record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Disagreement is permitted when supported by evidence and explanation. A revision invitation does not guarantee acceptance."}]}, {"id": "s10", "title": "T10 Permissions and production release record", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Article ID, title and final version: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Managing Editor: [Name] | Handling editor: [Name] | Proposed release date: [Date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This internal record tracks checks; it is not an author licence or legal agreement. Retain identifying consent and rights documents in restricted storage, not in the public article package."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Rights inventory"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Material or file", "Rights holder and authority", "Evidence and permitted use", "Status"], ["[Figure, quotation, dataset or case record]", "[Holder and authorised contact]", "[Reference, scope, licence, limits]", "[Cleared / Hold]"], ["[Identifiable participant or organisation]", "[Applicable consent or authority]", "[Confidential record reference]", "[Cleared / Hold]"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Record restrictions on print, online, adaptation, territories or duration where they exist. Remove or replace uncleared material; do not infer permission from availability online."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Release checklist"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Release condition", "Evidence reference and checker"], ["Formal editorial acceptance and accurate review label", "[Record]"], ["All final source files and accessible descriptions complete", "[Record]"], ["Author order affiliations contributions and declarations confirmed", "[Record]"], ["Ethics privacy and permissions issues resolved", "[Record]"], ["Publishing agreement and adopted licence completed", "[Record]"], ["References metadata and identifier configuration checked", "[Record]"], ["Proof corrections approved and substantive changes reassessed", "[Record]"], ["Data supplements and linked brief links tested", "[Record]"], ["Preservation deposit and backup responsibility assigned", "[Record]"], ["Public article rights notices and third-party exceptions accurate", "[Record]"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Proof instruction to the corresponding author"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Please check factual content, names, affiliations, tables, figures, references and production errors in [proof version]. Return corrections by [date; normally five working days proposed]. Contact the office for an extension. Substantive new findings require editorial approval before release."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Release decision: [Ready / Hold and reason]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authorised by: [Name and role] | Date: [Date] | Published version and persistent link: [After release]"}]}, {"id": "s11", "title": "T11 Board invitation and acceptance", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Unsent template. Use only after the appointing authority, role, terms, resources and proposed recipient have been approved. Interest expressed in an interview does not replace written acceptance."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Invitation draft"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dear [Preferred name],"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "On behalf of [authorised publisher], I invite you to consider serving as [role] on the PRAPJ [Advisory / Editorial] Board for [dates]. PRAPJ is being developed to connect credible project evidence with professional decisions. We are approaching you because [verified expertise and specific contribution]."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The enclosed Terms of Reference describe the mandate, expected workload, independence, conflicts and review arrangements. For this role, we propose [specific activities and hours or meetings]. The position is [remuneration and expenses as approved; do not assume]. Funding and launch status are [accurate status]. Appointment does not guarantee publication of your work or imply endorsement by your institution."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Please indicate whether you would like to discuss the role by [date]. Before appointment, we will confirm availability, interests, publication of your approved profile and any institutional permission required. You may decline without explanation."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Yours sincerely,"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Authorised sender, role and confirmed contact]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Acceptance record"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Preferred name and role accepted: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Term and review date: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Time commitment and support agreed: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Conflicts and outside appointments: [Disclosures and management conditions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Confidentiality and editorial independence: [Acknowledged]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Public profile and affiliation approved: [Exact wording and permission]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Institutional representation authorised: [Yes with evidence / No, personal capacity]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Remuneration and expenses: [Agreed terms or none]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Member acceptance and date: [Actual written acceptance reference]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Appointing authority and date: [Actual approval reference]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "No candidate name should appear on the public board page until this process is complete."}]}, {"id": "s12", "title": "T12 Editorial decision letter templates", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Private drafts. Select the correct template and replace every bracket. The independent handling editor must approve the actual decision and reasons. Never insert a reviewer identity into author correspondence."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Desk decline"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dear [Corresponding author],"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Thank you for submitting [title and ID]. After initial editorial assessment, we will not send this version for external review because [specific scope, contribution, evidence or readiness reason]. [Give a concise explanation and, where useful, a preparation suggestion without promising future acceptance.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This decision concerns this submission. If you believe a material factual or procedural error affected the assessment, the proposed appeal route is [independent contact or portal] within 30 calendar days. Do not send an appeal to an unconfirmed address."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Editor and date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Revision requested"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dear [Corresponding author],"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Following [accurate review route], we invite a [minor / major] revision of [title and ID]. The principal issues are [numbered material issues]. The attached author-facing reports provide details. [Explain conflicting recommendations and what response is expected.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Please return a clean manuscript, any requested marked copy and a point-by-point response by [date; proposed defaults 30 calendar days minor and 60 major]. Request an extension if needed. Explain any disagreement with evidence and precise manuscript locations. This invitation does not establish acceptance; the revision may require further review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Editor and date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Accept subject to production requirements"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dear [Corresponding author],"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "I am pleased to confirm editorial acceptance of [title, ID and accepted version] as [type], following [actual review route]. Please complete [outstanding source files, declarations, permissions, agreement and proof requirements]. Publication will proceed only after these requirements are resolved."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[For Research Articles: please prepare the linked brief with the Translation Editor using T04; the accepted article may publish when ready without waiting for the brief.] No processing time or identifier should be stated unless confirmed for this article."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Editor and date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decline after review"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Dear [Corresponding author],"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "After considering the assessments and manuscript, we will not proceed with [title and ID]. The decisive reasons are [specific evidence or contribution issues]. [Explain why another revision is not invited and distinguish this from correctable presentation concerns.] The attached reports contain feedback. A procedural or material factual appeal may use the adopted independent route within 30 calendar days."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[Editor and date]"}]}, {"id": "s13", "title": "T13 Interview Supported Contribution Record", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Private preparation record. A supported route is proposed, subject to approved staff capacity. This form does not open submissions, record consent by default or guarantee publication. Keep recording and identity information in authorised restricted storage."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contribution scope"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Contributor and interviewer: [Names and roles]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Proposed track, project objective, reader and decision: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Support requested and agreed effort: [Interview, outline, drafting or factual checking; capacity owner and dates]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Permission before recording"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Recording consent: [Granted / Not granted / Pending; evidence, date, limits and storage]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Intended uses: [Notes, draft, quotations, audio/video or derivative explanation; permission status for each]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Confidentiality and authority: [Employer/client approval, rights owner, permitted generalisation, identification risks and restrictions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Evidence and account"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Main observations and sources: [Records, schedule, measures and participant accounts; distinguish recollection]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Discrepancies and missing evidence: [What differs, unresolved points and effect on the claim]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Comparable objectives and transfer conditions: [Prerequisites, sector differences and limits]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Closing question: [Is there anything we have not discussed that is important for this contribution or PRAPJ? Record the answer faithfully.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Approval and independent handling"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Contributor check: [Draft version, corrections, disputed points, quotations and generalisations approved or held]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Authorship and assistance: [Actual contributions, author criteria, acknowledgements and consent to naming]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Approval to submit: [Each author, exact version, date and evidence; leave pending until obtained]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Independent handling: [Unconflicted editor and review route; preparer excluded from independent assessment]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Publication or media approval remains separate: [Outstanding permissions and release conditions]"}]}, {"id": "s14", "title": "T14 Media Companion Release Record", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Use for an optional video, audio item or short derivative explanation linked to written content. Proposed service capacity and supported formats remain to be confirmed. This is an internal check record, not a licence or proof that checks passed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Identity and purpose"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Media title, format and version: [Record]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Related article and source version: [Verified citation/link, status and correction link]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Audience and project objective: [Experienced professionals or clearly identified introductory audience; intended decision]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Actual assessment label: [Editorial/media assessment performed; do not inherit research peer review]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Fidelity and access"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Claim check: [Each important claim, source location, limitation and correction required]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Transfer limits: [Comparable objectives, prerequisites, context differences and untested implications]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Access files: [Checked captions and transcript for spoken video; audio transcript; necessary visual descriptions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Simplification check: [Student/short-form edits retain meaning, limitations and source credit]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Permissions and release"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Speaker and contributor approval: [Exact final version, date, evidence and permitted use]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Third-party rights: [Images, music, footage, extracts and credits; authorised scope and restrictions]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Privacy and confidentiality: [Recording/publication permission; residual identification risk; unresolved issues]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Responsible editor and technical owner: [Names, final checks and correction responsibility]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Release decision: [Ready / Hold; reason, approver and date]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Published link and correction propagation: [Complete only after authorised release]"}]}], "words": 3736}, {"id": "08", "title": "Print Journal Specimen", "filename": "08_PRAPJ_Print_Journal_Specimen.docx", "sha256": "6d2772af0d693885a22e79668268341ec35007bee25e19039d652fa94b8c9d2d", "sections": [{"id": "intro", "title": "Introduction & document status", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Title", "text": "PRAPJ"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Project Research and Practice Journal"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Hardcopy journal design"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Evidence for project decisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Editable print specimen  |  8 October 2026"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ is being developed for formal journal publication, bringing credible research and documented experience to professional project decisions. This eight-page Word specimen demonstrates the proposed reading experience through a cover, contents, editorial, case, brief and practice challenge."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "In this specimen"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Designing evidence for professional decisions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Recording uncertainty before a project gate"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Turning a source finding into a practical question"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "When are lessons ready to transfer"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Publication status"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This is a design specimen, not a published issue. Submissions are closed. Publisher, appointments, launch date, identifiers, rights and preservation arrangements remain to be confirmed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Original demonstration text prepared for Team DOMINUS. All case details are fictional, and no example has undergone journal review. No real project, research outcome, DOI, ISSN or publication metric is presented."}]}, {"id": "s1", "title": "Contents and publication information", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contents"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Page", "Demonstration content", "Status"], ["3", "Designing evidence for professional decisions", "Concept editorial specimen"], ["4", "Recording uncertainty before a project gate", "Fictional case opening"], ["5", "Case evidence and transfer conditions", "Fictional case continuation"], ["6", "Turning a source finding into a practical question", "Brief layout specimen"], ["7", "When are lessons ready to transfer", "Practice challenge specimen"], ["8", "Preparing a contribution and print notes", "Proposed journal information"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "About PRAPJ"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "PRAPJ is intended for experienced and mid-career project professionals seeking credible evidence they can use, and for researchers who want to inform practice. Its four core tracks are Research Articles, Practitioner Cases, Research-to-Practice Briefs, and Practice Challenges or Evidence Notes. Selected explanations may also support students and emerging professionals."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Editorial and publication record"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Editorial appointments: unconfirmed. Publisher and institutional host: unconfirmed. Public submission channel: not open. This specimen does not imply a partnership with a university, professional body or commercial publisher."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The preferred access model is diamond open access, subject to funding and adoption. Author copyright and a CC BY 4.0 publication licence are proposals requiring an approved agreement. They are not represented as executed contracts through this specimen."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "How to read the examples"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The examples are deliberately short layout excerpts. They do not meet full submission word lengths and have not undergone journal review. Their labels show the kind of status information a real issue must communicate. The fictional case demonstrates separation of a record, an interpretation and a proposed action; it supplies no empirical evidence for the business case."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A future digital publication would link each accepted item to its full text, evidence, review label, version and corrections. Optional videos and short explanations are being explored as companions to written contributions. The offline digital mockup illustrates that concept without a recording, live publication or submission service."}]}, {"id": "s2", "title": "Designing evidence for professional decisions", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Concept editorial specimen | Original demonstration text | Not peer reviewed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "The decision comes first"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A professional may approach research with an objective already in view: securing the right skills, managing dependencies, improving governance or testing whether a business case is deliverable. A contribution becomes easier to use when it identifies the decision early and explains what its evidence can contribute."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Practical meaning depends on context. Readers need to know the objective, alternatives, constraints and limits of a comparison. A lesson from another sector may still be relevant when the objective is comparable; a shared industry label alone does not establish that it will transfer."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Practice can also define the question"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Practitioner experience can reveal where guidance is difficult to apply. A useful account compares relevant sources, such as project records, schedule evidence and participant perspectives, and explains discrepancies. It states the author's involvement and includes inconvenient outcomes rather than smoothing them away."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The proposed PRAPJ model therefore gives different contributions different routes. Research requires scholarly assessment. Cases require scrutiny of evidence and context. Briefs require careful comparison with source research. Challenges require enough documented substance for another reader to understand and investigate the question."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "A visible boundary around each claim"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Consider a team that introduces a short uncertainty record at an approval gate. A later improvement in documentation would not, by itself, establish better project outcomes. A case should identify what changed in the records, what else happened during the period, and what was not measured. Readers can then judge whether the practice is worth testing in their own setting."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The next pages use an entirely fictional example to show this separation. Its purpose is to demonstrate presentation, not to prove that uncertainty records work. Real publication would require evidence, permissions and the review route appropriate to the claims."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "A proposal for the journal"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Make the objective, evidence and principal limitation easy to locate. Explore supported conversations that help practitioners develop permission-ready written accounts, and short media companions that lead back to the source. Preserve the same standards for authorship, evidence and review across these routes."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "These are proposed design choices, not promised services or tested outcomes. Judge a future pilot through reader understanding, usefulness and return use as well as publication activity. This editorial specimen asserts no empirical results."}]}, {"id": "s3", "title": "Recording uncertainty before a project gate", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Practitioner Case layout excerpt | Fictional example | Not reviewed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Author line: PRAPJ design specimen. No real project, organisation or participant is described."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Summary"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This fictional account illustrates how a practitioner case could document a change to project decision records. A team proposes a short uncertainty record for a stage gate. The specimen identifies the context, intended mechanism, evidence that would be needed and limits of interpretation. It reports no real intervention or measured result."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Decision relevance"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The example is aimed at a project sponsor considering whether a gate record should make assumptions and missing evidence more visible. Its practical value is a structure for asking what should be documented and checked. The example provides no basis for claiming that the proposed record would improve cost, schedule or benefits outcomes."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Context and challenge"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Assume a project is approaching a decision about its next delivery stage. Its objective is to make the approval decision with a clearer understanding of uncertainty. The recommendation, schedule and working files may tell different parts of the story; several estimates lack an explicit, shared record of their assumptions."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Proposed response"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The fictional team adds a one-page uncertainty record to the gate pack. It identifies the decision, relevant assumptions, evidence available, unresolved questions and an owner and date for each follow-up. The record would accompany, rather than replace, existing governance controls."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Evidence required for a real case"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A real case would compare gate records, schedule evidence and consented participant accounts, identify discrepancies and explain how they were investigated. It would state the observation period, assessment method, author role and concurrent changes. Non-public records require appropriate permission; removing names alone does not authorise disclosure."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "What the next page demonstrates"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The evidence table on page 5 contains illustrative entries describing evidence categories. It avoids fabricated measurement results. A real author would replace these entries with traceable records, actual observations, limitations and permitted supporting material."}]}, {"id": "s4", "title": "Case evidence and transfer conditions", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Continuation of the fictional case | Illustrative evidence structure only"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "A record of claims and limits"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Question", "Evidence a real case would need", "What cannot be inferred from it alone"], ["Were assumptions easier to locate?", "Defined checklist applied to gate packs, dates and assessor notes", "Better ultimate project performance"], ["Were unresolved questions followed up?", "Action log, ownership, closure evidence and elapsed time", "That the new form caused follow-up"], ["Did decision participants understand uncertainty?", "Consented feedback using a stated protocol", "Understanding across other teams or sectors"], ["Did the process add effort?", "Time records and participant accounts", "Net value without considering benefits and opportunity cost"], ["Where do accounts disagree?", "Compare status reports, schedule records and consented participant accounts", "That one source alone gives a complete account"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "No counts, percentages or outcomes in this table represent observations. It specifies evidence to collect if the example were tested."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Alternative explanations"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Improved documentation could follow stronger sponsor attention, a different gate chair or additional time before approval. A real case should identify these influences and avoid assigning all change to the record. An absence of improvement may reflect poor use of the form or a problem that the form does not address."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Conditions for trying the approach"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A project would need a comparable decision objective, a clear owner, agreement on which uncertainties matter and a way to maintain the record after the gate. The format must fit its governance and evidence sensitivity. It may add little value where assumptions are already explicit and regularly reviewed."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Proportionate next step"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A practitioner could test whether readers can identify the key assumptions in an existing gate pack, then decide whether a small documentation change is warranted. Any test should define the observation method, protect confidential material and examine the effort involved. A positive response would still not prove improved delivery outcomes."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Declarations for this specimen"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Data: no project data were collected or analysed. Permissions: no third-party project records or participant quotations are reproduced. Interests: this is a capstone design example. AI assistance: drafting and layout assistance were used in preparing the demonstration; the example must not be cited as research evidence."}]}, {"id": "s5", "title": "Turning a source finding into a practical question", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Research to Practice Brief layout excerpt | No source study supplied | Not reviewed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "The decision"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "[In a real brief, state the professional decision and intended reader in plain language.]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Source evidence"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Source article: [Verified citation and persistent link]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Source review status and version: [Actual status]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Study setting, method and period: [Verified details]"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This page is a layout specimen. It does not cite an invented study or present a factual research finding. A publishable brief would need traceable source evidence and editorial translation review."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "From evidence to action"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Layer", "What belongs here", "Reader check"], ["Source finding", "What the study actually established", "Can I locate the claim in the source?"], ["Interpretation", "What that finding may mean for this decision", "Which assumptions connect the two?"], ["Possible action", "A proportionate step or question for practice", "What conditions and resources are required?"], ["Boundary", "What remains uncertain or outside the study", "Where might the advice fail?"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "A useful limitation is specific"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Avoid a generic sentence saying that further research is needed. Describe the setting, method or missing information that constrains the inference. If the evidence concerns one type of project, explain why applying it to another setting requires judgement or additional checking."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Questions before use"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Does the study examine a decision and objective like mine, even if the sector differs? Are scale, incentives, governance and resources comparable? What evidence would show that a proposed action is not helping? What consequences would make a small test inappropriate?"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Review information in a real brief"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The public label would state Editorial translation review when that process had actually been completed. A link would connect the brief to its source and any correction. The label would not imply that the brief itself had undergone independent research peer review."}]}, {"id": "s6", "title": "When are lessons ready to transfer", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Practice Challenge layout excerpt | Original concept question | Not reviewed"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Problem to investigate"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Project teams may record lessons in language that assumes the reader understands the original setting. A future team may encounter the lesson without knowing which conditions made the response plausible. This specimen frames a question for investigation; it does not claim a measured prevalence of the problem."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "The professional decision"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Before reusing a lesson, a sponsor or project manager needs to decide whether the earlier situation is sufficiently comparable and what further evidence would reduce uncertainty. A lesson repository might support that decision if each entry records more than an action and a result."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Candidate information to examine"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Field proposed for a lesson", "Question it helps a reader ask"], ["Project objective and alternatives", "What was the team trying to achieve and choosing between?"], ["Context and constraints", "What shaped the response?"], ["Evidence and observation period", "What supports the claimed result?"], ["Author role and interests", "What perspective may influence the account?"], ["Transfer conditions", "What must hold for reuse to be plausible?"], ["Contradictory evidence", "What would make us reconsider?"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Researchable questions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Which fields help another team identify whether a lesson applies? What additional effort does structured documentation require? Can readers distinguish observed outcomes from retrospective interpretation? How do confidentiality constraints affect the usefulness of a case?"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "These questions could be investigated through appropriately authorised case analysis or reader studies. A study would need a defined design, sampling rationale, consent or other applicable permissions and transparent limitations. No organisation or reviewer has committed to such a study through this specimen."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contribution route"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "A future call could invite documented cases, research designs or evidence notes addressing these questions. Any call would identify approved editors, dates and assessment routes. The present page is not an open call and supplies no submission address."}]}, {"id": "s7", "title": "Preparing a contribution", "blocks": [{"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Choose the format that matches your evidence"}, {"type": "table", "rows": [["Format", "Main-text words", "Proposed assessment"], ["Research Article", "6,000–9,000", "At least two independent external reviews"], ["Evidence Review within research", "5,000–8,000", "Research review with method expertise"], ["Practitioner Case", "3,000–5,000", "Normally two external reviewers"], ["Research to Practice Brief", "1,200–2,000", "Editorial translation review"], ["Challenge or Evidence Note", "1,500–2,500", "Editorial and domain assessment"]]}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The separate Guide for Authors sets word-count exclusions, summaries, decision relevance, keywords, declarations and preparation requirements. These abbreviated specimen pages are not submission-length examples."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Before submitting"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Check the official guide version and the confirmed submission channel once announced. Prepare a separate private title page and review-appropriate manuscript. Address evidence provenance, uncertainty, author interests, permissions and data access. Do not submit to a contact address invented from the proposed journal name."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Production specification for this specimen"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "This Word document is the editable hardcopy design. Use A4 portrait at 100 percent scale, with duplex printing and long-edge binding for a conventional document. It is arranged in normal reading order. Check pagination and a physical proof after editing; use printer imposition only if a folded booklet is required."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "The page design uses approximately 18 mm margins, native text and editable tables. No bleed or crop marks are needed. Black-and-white printing retains the text and table structure. Keep the specimen labels visible until real publication details have been approved."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Contributor support and media companions"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Interview-supported contributions and optional video or short explanations are being explored. Availability and requirements will be confirmed before these services are offered. Contributors would approve the text, establish authorship and permissions, and follow the ordinary assessment route. Spoken video would need captions and a transcript; audio would need a transcript. All companions require source links, an accurate review label and linked corrections."}, {"type": "text", "style": "Heading 2", "text": "Publication readiness"}, {"type": "text", "style": "Normal", "text": "Assess readability, evidence visibility and review labels before producing a real issue. Publication requires an accountable host, approved policies, editorial capacity and confirmed support. All project budget figures are hypothetical planning assumptions; this specimen establishes no funding or service commitment."}]}], "words": 2091}];
